WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.966
STUDENT 1: Hi!

00:00:14.966 --> 00:00:18.170
This is Kelly,
and I have with me

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:23.345
my friends David, Matye,
Steven, Michael, and Sandra.

00:00:23.345 --> 00:00:26.330
JONES: Oh my goodness, that's
quite a collection of students.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.150
So where are you all from?

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.329
STUDENT 1: Well,
I'm from California.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:31.370
STUDENT 2: New York City.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.659
STUDENT 3: Toronto, Canada.

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:34.230
STUDENT 4: Kansas City.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.007
STUDENT 5: Westborough,
Massachusetts.

00:00:36.007 --> 00:00:37.340
STUDENT 6: Knoxville, Tennessee.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.165
JONES: Quite the range.

00:00:39.165 --> 00:00:40.522
You having a good day today?

00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:42.605
STUDENT 1: I'm sorry, can
you repeat that, please?

00:00:42.605 --> 00:00:44.720
JONES: Are you
having a good day?

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.730
STUDENT 1: Are we
having a good day?

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:47.585
Yeah.

00:00:47.585 --> 00:00:50.120
JONES: Is this better
than the day you

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.680
got your admit letter to MIT?

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.600
STUDENT 1: Ooh, is this
better than the day we

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.035
got our admit letter to MIT?

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.282
That's a pretty tough call.

00:00:59.282 --> 00:01:00.670
STUDENT 2: I would say so.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.378
STUDENT 6: This is
much more of a relief.

00:01:02.378 --> 00:01:03.766
STUDENT 5: Probably, yeah.

00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:06.400
STUDENT 1: I guess
much more of a relief.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.489
JONES: It's much more of a
relief because you got out?

00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:11.030
STUDENT 1: I'm sorry,
say that again?

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.920
JONES: Because you
got out finally?

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.730
STUDENT 1: Well, because we
got out, finally, I guess so.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.205
JONES: So Kelly.

00:01:24.205 --> 00:01:25.080
STUDENT 1: Yeah.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.652
JONES: Just give me one
memorable moment at MIT.

00:01:28.652 --> 00:01:32.000
STUDENT 1: One
memorable moment at MIT.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.115
JONES: A person,
a thing, a course?

00:01:38.044 --> 00:01:40.280
STUDENT 1: I guess I don't know.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.330
Right now, standing
here with Sandra,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.330
who I work with on the
ambulance, the only thing

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.040
that's coming to my
mind's the ambulance.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.410
So hi to all the EMTs out there.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.830
JONES: Did you save
anybody's life this week?

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:53.780
STUDENT 1: Not this week.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.440
Did we save anyone's life?

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.320
STUDENT 6: No.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.620
We didn't work this week,
I don't think we were--

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.448
[LAUGHS]

00:01:58.448 --> 00:02:02.120
JONES: So what's the most
traumatic EMT thing you did?

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.985
I used to be an EMT years
ago in in New York State.

00:02:04.985 --> 00:02:05.891
STUDENT 1: Oh, wow.

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:07.023
Wait, what was your
question again?

00:02:07.023 --> 00:02:08.090
I'm sorry, it's really loud.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.214
JONES: It's really loud
over there with everybody--

00:02:10.214 --> 00:02:11.534
a million graduates.

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:15.190
What's the most memorable
EMT thing you did?

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.225
STUDENT 1: The most
memorable EMT thing

00:02:17.225 --> 00:02:23.010
I did was showing up one
time to a choking victim

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.810
and finding him completely
out on the ground.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.540
And I wasn't actually
involved with his care,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.410
but I was involved with the
hysterical wife care, which

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.470
ended up being a lot more
memorable than anything

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:38.723
else I've ever done.

00:02:38.723 --> 00:02:39.900
JONES: Ah, yes.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.130
So MIT students save the world,
and individual lives as well.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.425
So, you guys, we have
to close this now.

00:02:47.425 --> 00:02:50.275
You want to give a big
shout-out and a thank

00:02:50.275 --> 00:02:52.175
you to people who
are here today?

00:02:52.175 --> 00:02:53.125
Each of you--

00:02:53.125 --> 00:02:54.383
STUDENT 1: --shout-out and thank
you to people who are here.

00:02:54.383 --> 00:02:55.049
STUDENT 5: What?

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.522
STUDENT 1: Shout-out and thank
you to people who are here.

00:02:58.522 --> 00:03:00.340
STUDENT 5: Thanks
to all my friends.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:03.130
I mean, I wouldn't be
here without you guys.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.880
STUDENT 6: Thank to all my
friends, and my mom and dad,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.390
who--

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.445
I wouldn't be here without them.

00:03:08.445 --> 00:03:10.196
STUDENT 2: Thank
you, mom and dad.

00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:12.935
STUDENT 3: Thanks to my mom and
dad for helping me get here.

00:03:12.935 --> 00:03:14.847
STUDENT 4: Thanks,
mom, dad, and Eric.

00:03:14.847 --> 00:03:16.430
STUDENT 1: And I
have to say the same.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.540
Thanks, mom and dad.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.290
STUDENT 6: I got to
thank my brother, too.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.480
He graduated class of '99.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.192
He's also the
reason why I'm here.

00:03:23.192 --> 00:03:25.095
JONES: Have fun today, you guys!

00:03:25.095 --> 00:03:26.711
STUDENT 1: Thanks, you too.

00:03:30.926 --> 00:03:32.550
ROSKO: Now, the
technology that enables

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.120
us to interview the students
over at the Johnson Athletic

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.090
Center is the same
technology that we

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.880
use for much greater distances.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.440
Last year at Tech Day--

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.010
MIT's annual alumni reunion--

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.390
President Charles Vest conducted
a particularly long-distance

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.630
interview with a distinguished
alumnus from the class

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.250
of 1989--

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.550
astronaut Mike Fincke--
aboard the International Space

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.290
Station, some 400
kilometers above the earth.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.942
Let's see this
long-distance communication.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.030
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.040
- Mike, welcome to your reunion.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.020
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.160
- President Vest, thank
you very much for your kind

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.430
introduction.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.590
I'd like to check our coms.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.130
Can you hear me?

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.960
- You can-- we can
hear you very well.

00:04:35.852 --> 00:04:37.960
- When we were setting
this up with NASA,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.880
we were wondering if
we'd be able to get

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.450
a good audio and video, and
I can see you guys great.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.050
I'm glad you guys can hear me.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.320
And I said, if anybody could
do great audio and video,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.892
it's MIT.

00:04:49.892 --> 00:04:51.836
VEST: Lot of applause there.

00:04:51.836 --> 00:04:56.320
- I miss MIT, 15 years ago.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.170
And everybody who is an
alumnus in front of me

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.810
said, you know, it goes by fast.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.840
And boy, it really does.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.920
And I'm sure I'll probably feel
that way after 20 and 25 years.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.900
But the nice thing
about having time

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.320
is it's given me a little
bit of perspective.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.170
And the view we have on
the planet from up here

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.060
gives me some more perspective.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.020
And what MIT does
is very important--

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.230
the mission of MIT,
the way that MIT

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.870
goes about teaching
its students,

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.780
and the way that MIT's not
afraid to be world-class.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.190
These are very important
things to the entire planet.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.840
[END PLAYBACK]

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.050
ROSKO: Now, when the class
of 2005 were sophomores,

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.110
a good number of them took MIT's
renowned mechanical engineering

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.650
course, 2.007.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.700
This is a much-emulated course
where students design, engineer

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.780
and build a machine that
accomplishes specific tasks.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.710
All this hard work culminates in
an end of the semester contest

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:05.090
where students compete head
to head in MIT's most exciting

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.250
spectator sport.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.800
We offer first a
retrospective look

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:14.050
at the evolution of the
course over the last 30 years,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:18.400
followed by highlights
from the contest from 2002,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.480
the year the class of 2005 were
hard at work as sophomores.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:27.590
Pay close attention, and you
might recognize your graduates

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.240
among some of the competitors.

00:06:35.072 --> 00:06:36.530
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:37.988
[BUZZING SOUND]

00:06:42.362 --> 00:06:43.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.300
- 2.70 started long, long ago,
in a place actually not far

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.150
away--

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.770
in the basement
of this building.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.966
We gave the students
a creativity kit.

00:06:51.966 --> 00:06:55.010
- Inside the tube is a rod--

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.970
that is, the piece
of welding rod.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.096
- And I was a graduate
student at the time,

00:06:59.096 --> 00:07:00.470
worked closely
with the students,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.120
and learned that for their
first design project,

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.480
it was really hard for
them to decide what to do.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.930
- This is where the lanyard's
going to be attached,

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:09.380
and that will be stretched out.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.420
- So the next year, we
decided that we give them

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:14.420
a kit of materials, but give
them a very specific thing

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:15.710
to try to accomplish.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.670
- I'm doing that for
lightness to cut mass,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.140
but basically it's for
looks and steadiness.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.040
- And they got into
it immediately,

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.350
and that sort of got
the ball rolling.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:28.790
And 2.70 began with a very
simple contest that involved

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.660
making the machine go down
a hill in three minutes.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.540
- --down and just drill a big
hole to know until you're okay.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.770
- The 2.70 contest evolved
to where it is today.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.460
When we first started,
it was a simple machine

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.860
doing a single thing
while people watched.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.810
And it was quite slow.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.900
After a while, we had machines,
side by side, doing things,

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.520
so you could see
some competition.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.740
And then we got
interested in what

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.440
happens if the machines
actually collide in the middle,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.940
and compete for territory.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:59.030
- Tie the knot, move it back,
replace it, set it in the sand.

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:02.510
- From that, we evolved
robotically-controlled things.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:04.900
- Ready, get set, go.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.812
- Oh!

00:08:06.812 --> 00:08:08.246
Yeah!

00:08:08.246 --> 00:08:10.160
Oh!

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.734
- And that has
become a much more

00:08:12.734 --> 00:08:14.400
interactive, interesting
thing to watch.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.750
But the most important
thing that happened

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:24.050
was the students took
on the challenge,

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:29.060
they competed like crazy,
but helped each other

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.340
in the process.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.460
That, I think, is one of the
most important things about

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.439
2.70, 2.007 first--

00:08:36.439 --> 00:08:41.210
the whole idea that you can
work together, but still

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.799
compete and help each other.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:46.064
A shared environment, a
shared learning experience,

00:08:46.064 --> 00:08:48.230
a great model for what the
profession of engineering

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.639
ought to be about.

00:08:49.639 --> 00:08:52.055
- I first came in contact and
actually heard about it when

00:08:52.055 --> 00:08:54.530
I was here as an undergraduate.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:59.110
And Woodie Flowers
was my UROP advisor.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:03.250
Actually, I was blueprinting
an engine for my pickup truck.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:06.280
And I was kind of looking
forward to taking 2.70,

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.710
but I had blown my
engine on my truck,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:13.090
so he actually agreed to
let me at that point rebuild

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:14.180
the engine on my truck.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.540
So I was peripherally
involved, I was always looking

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.300
in on the class-- gee, that
looks like a lot of fun--

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.990
but never really got
around to doing it.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:26.900
Always kind of wishing I had,
but vicariously living it

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.500
by helping my friends and stuff.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.900
And then I graduated,
went off to work,

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.190
came back as a professor.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:34.850
And then I taught
one section in it,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.870
because the course has the
main instructor in charge

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.820
of the whole class,
where you're really

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.030
the manager or the
captain of the ship.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:45.230
So if I recall, there was
a committee for a while,

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.421
and they were debating to
whom to give the class to.

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:50.920
And they said, well, the only
person who really should teach

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:54.979
is Alex, because he has
good taste in fashion.

00:09:54.979 --> 00:09:57.020
And we need someone with
a good taste in fashion.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:00.260
So they ask me if I would do
it even though I have tenure,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.052
and I said, okay,
but let's go ahead

00:10:02.052 --> 00:10:04.510
and involve the undergraduates
more in actually running it,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.510
and that will be like a
whole army of people helping.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:09.670
Those are the-- that
was kind of a birth

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.940
of the undergraduate
assistant arm of it.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.638
And we've been having
fun ever since.

00:10:15.638 --> 00:10:17.634
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:32.604 --> 00:10:34.910
- The contest is
just a lot of fun.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.495
- It's one of the best
classes I will ever take.

00:10:37.495 --> 00:10:38.120
I'm sure of it.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.780
- 2.70 rocks.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.680
- 2.70 has changed a lot.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:45.260
Now, it's remote
control, interactive,

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.940
lots of things happening.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:48.836
That happened gradually.

00:10:48.836 --> 00:10:50.210
- The real thing
that has changed

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:54.200
is that we gone to radio control
from the old days of a tether.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.810
So now the machines
have a lot more freedom.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.700
And it also means in
designing a contest,

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.130
we can put more things
into it, so it's

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.350
more like a pinball machine.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:05.750
- At first, the
students were required

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:09.710
to do pencil drawing
of devices before they

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:10.850
would go build them.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:15.200
And most of them wouldn't,
they just go start building.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:18.140
Then we learned drafting--
computer-aided drafting--

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:20.120
and they use those tools
to help a little bit.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.370
Now, we're at the point where
you can do solid models,

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:24.036
and tools are becoming
much more useful,

00:11:24.036 --> 00:11:25.760
and students will
do that because it's

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.760
actually fast and works.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.180
And we're getting
to the point now

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.639
where the onscreen dynamic
simulations are good enough

00:11:32.639 --> 00:11:35.180
that you can actually use them
to get an insight about what's

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.780
going to happen.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.740
- So the kit has
involved with the class,

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:41.880
and with technology in general.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.380
We just give them
primarily raw materials--

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:48.800
sheets of aluminum,
bars of metal,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:51.460
just gears, bearings, motors.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:52.130
That's it.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.620
We just give them the
most basic of LEGO bricks.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:59.210
And then they design,
cut, fold, weld,

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:01.470
put together real
machinery from it.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:05.240
- We always try to change the
character of the transportation

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.700
problem if there
is one involved.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:12.050
One year, we had
putting a square peg

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:14.450
in a round hole, which
was a lot of fun,

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.820
and MIT students can do that.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.070
That's probably a
good experience.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:22.550
- So the contests have
not really changed at all

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.340
in the sense that they
are to provide a challenge

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:29.420
to the students-- that when
you look at the contest,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:33.170
there is no single right answer.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:36.600
No one can look at this and
say, aha, that's how you win.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:38.240
- When you get to
the end and there's

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:41.300
four or five completely
different designs that are

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.870
competitive, it's a good sign.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:46.660
- And it's fun, because
all the students are all

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:47.360
really excited.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.490
And it's not just,
oh, no, a problem set.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.327
Let's see.

00:12:50.327 --> 00:12:52.410
It's like, wow, I'm really
doing something useful.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:52.900
Wow, cool.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:53.240
This is cool.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:53.948
I'm going to win.

00:12:53.948 --> 00:12:54.740
Yeah.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:58.190
- In 2.70 and 2.007, we try hard
to make sure the students know

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.554
that they have the freedom
to try something different.

00:13:00.554 --> 00:13:02.970
Just get your instructor on
board, and say, you know what?

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.803
I don't know if I'm
going to do this or not,

00:13:04.803 --> 00:13:06.650
but I want to try
this crazy thing.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:08.390
And that's okay,
because it's not

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:11.150
about winning the
competition, It's

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:14.630
about doing a
meaningful, good job.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:19.280
- When I start the class off,
first lecture, I said, okay.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:21.740
Everyone wants to
win this contest.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.960
First of all, it's not a
contest, it's a celebration.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.310
It's just a great geeking time,
get together with your robots,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:31.370
and yow, going to have fun.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:35.450
Because statistically,
everyone here is going to lose,

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.540
because your chances of
winning are only one in 120.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:43.080
So like, chill out,
relax, have fun.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.520
And when you're
having fun, A, you

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.610
will do better, and B, when
other people ask you for help,

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.970
or you see someone needs help
and you help them, you learn.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:55.850
And the more you learn,
the better chance you will

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.910
of doing better yourself.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:13:59.910
And that's true in your
professional life, also.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:04.220
- And you compete like crazy,
and you get a lot of kudos

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.159
for helping other people.

00:14:06.159 --> 00:14:07.950
One of the things that
teaches, by the way,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.240
is that ideas are cheap.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.410
The combination of
ideas and execution,

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.910
and excellence in
the whole process,

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:15.420
is what really matters.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:19.250
So, occasionally, someone
would keep their secret.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:22.777
They would say, this is my idea,
and I'm not telling anybody.

00:14:22.777 --> 00:14:24.235
And they would do
that for a while,

00:14:24.235 --> 00:14:26.300
and they looked up, and
said, wait a minute.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:29.360
Everybody else has had the same
idea, and they chose not to.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:30.530
Maybe this is not--

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.312
maybe I'll talk to people
about what I want to do.

00:14:33.312 --> 00:14:54.460
[MUSIC - CHUMBAWAMBA,
 "TUBTHUMPING"]

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:57.050
- When you're a student,
failing early and often

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:58.680
is a very healthy
thing to go through.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.030
Because every time that
happens, pick yourself up, say,

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:05.270
I now understand something
that is very important here.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:07.650
I'm going to use that
information go forward.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:11.664
- If you don't fail,
you will never have fun.

00:15:11.664 --> 00:15:15.690
The trick is to
learn how to fail,

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:17.640
and that's a very
important part of what

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.830
we want to teach in this class.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.280
- It's also nice to have an
environment in which failing

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.230
is okay.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:28.200
The competition is always
designed so that there

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:30.570
are lots of ways to go.

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:33.940
And consequently, there are a
lot of ways to hide your ego,

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:35.490
and you can try
something that has

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.800
very low probability of
working, but your instructor

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.320
will say, hey, go for it.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:44.100
Later in life, if you have
not established a faith

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.270
in your own ability
to be creative,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:49.800
it's really hard to do that
when the stakes are so high.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.780
If you make a mistake,
you might lose your job.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.640
So while you have a chance
to test your creative wings,

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:57.796
you better do it.

00:15:57.796 --> 00:15:59.780
