00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:38.650
ROSKO: Now, here at
MIT, there's a program

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:40.330
in the School of
Engineering that

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:43.450
helps prepare aspiring engineers
for the realities they'll

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:47.382
face in the day-to-day world.

00:24:47.382 --> 00:24:49.840
Unlike the Apprentice, where
only one individual is crowned

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:52.420
a winner, the UPOP program--

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:55.330
Undergraduate Practice
Opportunities Program--

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:57.109
aims to make them all winners.

00:24:57.109 --> 00:24:58.400
Let's take a look at the video.

00:25:01.526 --> 00:25:02.192
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:25:02.192 --> 00:25:04.088
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:09.989
- UPOP stands for Undergraduate
Practice Opportunities Program.

00:25:09.989 --> 00:25:11.780
It's a program in the
School of Engineering

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:15.200
that we have designed to help
engineering students gain

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:18.840
a better appreciation
of the engineering

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:23.280
practice, the workplace,
and to help them acquire

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.990
the skills they
need to be effective

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:28.690
and be leaders in engineering
practice out there.

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:31.790
- We have four phases
to the program.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:35.530
The first is a 40-hour
corporate training seminar.

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:38.260
It's called the Introduction to
Engineering Practice Workshop

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:40.070
that we teach during IAP.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.310
The second is a
spring seminar series

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:46.090
taught by alumni that focus
on career development topics.

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.790
The third is the summer
practice experience,

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.850
where the students do an
internship for 10 weeks

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:52.900
during the summer.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:55.810
And then, finally, we have a
fall reflective experience,

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:58.180
where the students
come back and share

00:25:58.180 --> 00:26:00.580
with other students,
faculty and alumni

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:03.010
what they've learned from
their engineering practice

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.433
experience, and how it relates
to what we taught them.

00:26:05.433 --> 00:26:07.599
- I think in doing a program
like UPOP in the School

00:26:07.599 --> 00:26:13.390
of Engineering [INAUDIBLE]
responds to that [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:13.390 --> 00:26:16.550
in the sense that not only do
we have to have our students

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:19.360
strong in their
technical discipline,

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:22.090
but it is important that
they have an appreciation,

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:25.540
and have the skills and ability,
to deal with engineering

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:27.920
practice as they are
in the real world.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.760
- The topics I was most
interested about in the UPOP

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:35.980
program were how organizations
work, what engineers can do

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.980
and what their role is
within an organization,

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:42.520
and what sort of factors you
need to take into account when

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:45.340
you are planning a business,
such as marketing, knowing

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:49.000
your customers, and working
together with your whole team--

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:50.620
rather than just
your engineers--

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:52.840
to deliver the product
that people really want.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:54.430
- There is a little
bit of warming up

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:55.929
to do with the
people I worked with.

00:26:55.929 --> 00:26:57.780
And I guess there
were some challenges.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:00.250
They gave my project for the
summer, and on the first day

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:01.080
I finished it.

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:03.910
And they were a little confused
as to what to do with me.

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:08.710
But I explored around, I ended
up learning a huge amount,

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:10.720
and I had a
marvelous time there.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.677
- It definitely provided a
different approach to things.

00:27:13.677 --> 00:27:15.760
You think engineering, you
think private industry.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:17.889
You don't necessarily
think the public sector.

00:27:17.889 --> 00:27:19.930
People miss out on that,
especially MIT students.

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:22.470
They miss out on the opportunity
to explore other realms,

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:23.800
because you have these
great companies who

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:25.674
come here, recruit the
students, provide them

00:27:25.674 --> 00:27:28.090
with great packages
for the summer.

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:29.480
So it's hard to turn that down.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:32.860
And to have that opportunity,
to have some other opportunities

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:36.250
available, like non-profits
or government or whatever,

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:37.120
I think is a good--

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.460
it's a good thing
for MIT students,

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:43.720
because you need MIT students in
all aspects of life, including

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:44.890
government.

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:48.292
- I definitely see myself
really curious, and not just

00:27:48.292 --> 00:27:50.500
being able to come up with
an idea, but marketing it.

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:52.124
Because I've seen
these companies fail,

00:27:52.124 --> 00:27:55.242
and this company was on the
edge for a long time now.

00:27:55.242 --> 00:27:56.200
And so now I'm really--

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.127
I really wonder.

00:27:57.127 --> 00:27:59.210
I really want to learn
about what makes a company,

00:27:59.210 --> 00:28:01.944
and what can really
make it great.

00:28:01.944 --> 00:28:03.360
- I think that the
UPOP experience

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.426
has been very important to
my MIT education as a whole,

00:28:06.426 --> 00:28:07.800
because it's really
helped me get

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:10.650
a perspective on the
technologies and skills

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.710
that I've been
learning in my classes.

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:15.120
While I know from my classes
how to get the right answer

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:19.800
to a problem from UPOP, I know
how to put together questions,

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:22.620
how to think about
an organization,

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:25.170
or new technology,
or about my career,

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.090
and really how to set things up.

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:28.630
So it's really
taking a broader view

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:30.505
and incorporating a lot
of different elements

00:28:30.505 --> 00:28:34.470
from psychology, managerial
science, marketing,

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:35.550
pretty much everything.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:37.660
And when I add my MIT
education to that,

00:28:37.660 --> 00:28:39.600
I feel very prepared for
my career and future.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:41.004
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:42.408 --> 00:28:43.812
[END PLAYBACK]

