00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:03.750
like I'm on Peter Jennings.

00:36:03.750 --> 00:36:06.760
Anyway, it's time for us to
interview some of the families

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:09.100
here today, because
the point is that this

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:10.260
is all about our graduates.

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:11.750
So here, we have
several families.

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:14.600
I have our first family here.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.155
So I want to introduce you.

00:36:16.155 --> 00:36:17.770
MILLER: I'm Tony Miller.

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:23.920
This is Annette [? Taverner's ?]
parents, Manuel and Barbara.

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:24.710
JONES: Welcome.

00:36:24.710 --> 00:36:28.320
So your wife is graduating
today, their daughter.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:32.350
MILLER: She's receiving
her PhD in Speech

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:35.620
and Hearing Bioscience
and Technology

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:38.060
from the Division of Health
Sciences and Technology.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:38.935
JONES: You are also--

00:36:38.935 --> 00:36:42.005
MILLER: I'm also a PhD
student in the same program.

00:36:42.005 --> 00:36:43.630
JONES: But she's
graduating before you?

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:44.620
MILLER: She is.

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:45.875
She's two years ahead of me.

00:36:45.875 --> 00:36:47.500
JONES: She's a little
smarter than you?

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:50.740
MILLER: Oh, definitely.

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:52.372
JONES: So tell me something.

00:36:52.372 --> 00:36:53.330
Her name is what again?

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:54.760
MILLER: Annette Taverner.

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:55.780
JONES: Annette Taverner.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:56.279
Okay.

00:36:56.279 --> 00:36:58.205
So let's talk about
Annette for a minute.

00:36:58.205 --> 00:36:59.770
So Annette grew up where?

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:01.690
MILLER: In Miami, Florida.

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:04.240
Her mother is a Cuban exile.

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:06.880
Her father is from Argentina.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:08.770
She's the first
person of the family

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:11.000
to graduate from college.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:14.290
JONES: So the very first
person in your family

00:37:14.290 --> 00:37:18.505
to graduate from college, and
she's getting her PhD from MIT.

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:24.050
Nothing like going to the top.

00:37:24.050 --> 00:37:26.230
So here's a big
call out to Annette,

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:29.380
over there robing and
getting ready for this today.

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:31.720
So what-- why do you
think her interest is

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:33.220
in hearing and speech?

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:35.095
Is there any of
that in your family?

00:37:35.095 --> 00:37:37.540
MILLER: No, but
she was originally

00:37:37.540 --> 00:37:40.420
trained as an
electrical engineer,

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:45.039
and she was very interested
in communication technologies.

00:37:45.039 --> 00:37:46.080
JONES: And how about you?

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:47.040
Did you meet her here?

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:48.630
MILLER: I met her here.

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:51.300
JONES: Oh my god, so you're
like a real MIT family?

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:53.340
We became your matchmakers.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:54.480
This is wonderful.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.120
MILLER: She not only
received her PhD.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:37:59.670
She received her MRS at MIT.

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:04.920
JONES: Oh, her
MRS. No babies yet?

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:06.400
MILLER: No babies yet.

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:07.740
JONES: Sorry, I'm a mother.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:10.410
Sorry, forgive me.

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:12.702
So tell me a little
about your daughter.

00:38:12.702 --> 00:38:14.160
Tell me a little
bit about Annette.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.185
What's she like?

00:38:15.185 --> 00:38:18.210
MILLER: Annette is
very personable.

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:21.810
She served on many
committees here.

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:24.750
She's an avid trap shooter
when she's in Miami.

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:26.290
JONES: A trap shooter?

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:27.390
MILLER: Shotguns.

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:30.190
JONES: Shotguns, so
like, pull, bang.

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:30.690
Like that?

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:31.760
MILLER: Exactly.

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:32.362
Yeah.

00:38:32.362 --> 00:38:34.320
JONES: So she's got good
hand-eye coordination?

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:35.380
MILLER: Very good.

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:37.379
JONES: She's nobody you
want to make mad at you?

00:38:37.379 --> 00:38:41.160
MILLER: No, especially if you're
one of those little clay disks.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.174
Don't do it.

00:38:43.174 --> 00:38:44.590
JONES: And where
do you live here?

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:46.813
MILLER: We live in Davis
Square, Somerville.

00:38:46.813 --> 00:38:48.396
JONES: Okay, so not
in the [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:48.396 --> 00:38:48.896
MILLER: No.

00:38:48.896 --> 00:38:50.230
JONES: Well, welcome to you.

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:51.874
I hope it's a
wonderful day for you,

00:38:51.874 --> 00:38:53.790
and a wonderful day for
your daughter Annette.

00:38:53.790 --> 00:38:54.510
MILLER: Thank you.

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:54.900
JONES: Thank you.

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:55.799
Nice to meet you.

00:38:59.292 --> 00:39:01.290
Great.

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:04.166
So Tom.

00:39:04.166 --> 00:39:07.130
TOM: Yes, Marilee?

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:10.870
I'm supposed to--
so the students

00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:13.870
being honored today have
been at MIT during a period

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:17.200
of unprecedented
growth in student life

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:20.310
and learning programs,
resulting in the construction

00:39:20.310 --> 00:39:23.340
of new dorms, new classrooms,
and a beautiful new athletic

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:24.550
center.

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:28.220
We recently produced a series
of short video retrospectives

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:31.160
where students talk about
their MIT experiences

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:33.590
outside of the classrooms
and laboratories.

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:36.241
Watch the screen, and you
might once again catch

00:39:36.241 --> 00:39:37.365
a glimpse of your graduate.

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.112
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:39:45.598 --> 00:39:47.092
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:39:57.550 --> 00:39:59.586
- When I came as a
freshman coming in,

00:39:59.586 --> 00:40:01.335
there's all of these
things thrown at you.

00:40:01.335 --> 00:40:05.034
So many activities to do,
so many organizations.

00:40:05.034 --> 00:40:07.450
- And that's what's really so
impressive about this place,

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:09.420
is that everyone is
passionate about something.

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:10.750
You just have to talk to
them for five minutes,

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:12.010
and you'll start
to hear about it.

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:14.110
- I think what surprised me most
is really just how much there

00:40:14.110 --> 00:40:15.520
is to do, and how
many people are

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:19.330
doing that stuff, like there's
a club for almost anything you

00:40:19.330 --> 00:40:19.990
can imagine.

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:24.070
There's enough people that
actually fill the club, too.

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:27.660
- We're about to perform an
opera made up on the spot

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:28.670
from Italy.

00:40:28.670 --> 00:40:29.957
Let the opera begin.

00:40:29.957 --> 00:40:31.388
[NON-ENGLISH SONG]

00:40:32.565 --> 00:40:33.440
- I came here at MIT.

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:35.620
I was interested in
computer science majoring,

00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:37.380
but I also like the arts.

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:38.790
My favorite type
of art is dance.

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:39.490
I love to dance.

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:40.978
I've been dancing
since I was two.

00:40:40.978 --> 00:40:42.466
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:52.310
- I've been studying
mechanical engineering at MIT.

00:40:52.310 --> 00:40:53.820
And during the time
I've been here,

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:55.660
I've been really involved
in the Hobby Shop.

00:40:55.660 --> 00:40:57.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:05.320
- I came to MIT to major
in computer science,

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:07.236
but while I was here, I
discovered lion dance.

00:41:07.236 --> 00:41:09.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:17.440
- What I also found
here is a passion

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:21.499
for service on a scale, which
is different from anything

00:41:21.499 --> 00:41:23.290
that I had really been
able to conceive of.

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:27.490
- We're going to explain about
filter systems for water.

00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:30.069
- I was a coordinator of the
freshman leadership program.

00:41:30.069 --> 00:41:32.110
I've also done a lot of
work within the community

00:41:32.110 --> 00:41:34.590
at different schools within
the Cambridge system.

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:38.080
- The thing that I'm most
involved in right now

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:41.730
is MIT African Internet
Technology Initiative.

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:43.990
- I play both field
hockey and lacrosse.

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:47.980
I am involved in a
sorority alpha phi.

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:49.264
- I'm a small group leader--

00:41:49.264 --> 00:41:50.680
a small group Bible
study leader--

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:52.390
for Asian Christian Fellowship.

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:54.940
And I was involved in
MIT Cross Products,

00:41:54.940 --> 00:41:58.781
which is the only Christian
a capella group on campus.

00:41:58.781 --> 00:42:02.680
- [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:02.680 --> 00:42:04.240
- The range is just so large.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:05.980
It would take a huge
amount of time just

00:42:05.980 --> 00:42:07.271
to enumerate the opportunities.

00:42:07.271 --> 00:42:09.550
And in some sense, this
challenge for the student

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:13.035
is figuring out which of
those things they want to do,

00:42:13.035 --> 00:42:14.410
because they can't
do everything.

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:16.493
They can't even do a small
fraction of everything.

00:42:16.493 --> 00:42:17.701
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:22.174
[END PLAYBACK]

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:25.740
JONES: Hi, everybody,
we're back.

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:28.820
We're back with
the Mahoney family.

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:31.920
So let's talk about your kids.

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:35.680
So who's graduating today?

00:42:35.680 --> 00:42:38.920
MRS. MAHONEY: 2/3 of our
triplets are graduating today--

00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:41.650
Maya and Quinn Mahoney.

00:42:41.650 --> 00:42:45.940
Their sister Brenna graduated
from Cornell University

00:42:45.940 --> 00:42:47.690
last weekend.

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:50.660
JONES: So 2/3 of
your triplets are

00:42:50.660 --> 00:42:53.430
going to be in the great
Court in about an hour.

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:56.960
So how come your other one
chose to go to Cornell?

00:42:56.960 --> 00:42:59.619
That's what I want to know.

00:42:59.619 --> 00:43:00.910
MR. MAHONEY: She's very bright.

00:43:05.090 --> 00:43:08.380
JONES: He's mad at me
because we were not

00:43:08.380 --> 00:43:11.770
able to get enough kids
out of his high school.

00:43:11.770 --> 00:43:13.120
You teach there, right?

00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:13.911
MR. MAHONEY: Right.

00:43:13.911 --> 00:43:16.480
I teach in an inner city high
school in Washington, DC.

00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:18.790
It's a public
school, and we want

00:43:18.790 --> 00:43:21.125
to send our graduates to MIT.

00:43:21.125 --> 00:43:21.970
JONES: Amen.

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:23.080
Benjamin Banneker, right?

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:26.220
MR. MAHONEY: Benjamin
Banneker Academic High School.

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:28.921
JONES: And so where did
your triplets go to school?

00:43:28.921 --> 00:43:30.420
MRS. MAHONEY: Sidwell
Friends School

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:35.400
in Washington DC, where
John taught for 24 years,

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:37.500
before he went to
Benjamin Banneker.

00:43:37.500 --> 00:43:39.990
JONES: You know, I remember
these cases very well.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:41.870
I remember your
children's cases.

00:43:41.870 --> 00:43:42.480
Oh yeah.

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:46.780
It's not every day you see
twins or even triplets.

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:49.022
So did they have a
good experience here?

00:43:49.022 --> 00:43:50.730
MR. MAHONEY: They had
a great experience.

00:43:50.730 --> 00:43:54.750
And our son Quinn was just
involved with the Time Traveler

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:56.460
Convention.

00:43:56.460 --> 00:43:59.490
And we're anticipating
seeing some sort of prank

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:02.100
this morning, but we don't
know anything about it.

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:04.980
He certainly is not involved.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:07.050
JONES: So he could
be someone who

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.510
knows people, who knows
people who have something

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:11.698
to do with hacks?

00:44:11.698 --> 00:44:13.866
MRS. MAHONEY:
Could be, could be.

00:44:13.866 --> 00:44:14.790
JONES: Could be.

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:16.640
MR. MAHONEY: Could be.

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:18.500
JONES: So where
do they live here?

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:20.005
Which living groups?

00:44:20.005 --> 00:44:22.130
MR. MAHONEY: Even though
they have the same major--

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:25.010
Course 6-- they live on
opposite ends of the campus.

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:27.050
Our son lives on East
Campus, and our daughter

00:44:27.050 --> 00:44:30.410
lives in Next House.

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:32.640
JONES: In two
different ZIP codes.

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:36.170
So how are you financing
three big educations?

00:44:36.170 --> 00:44:39.020
Plus you said you also
have an older son.

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:40.020
Is that right?

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:41.436
MRS. MAHONEY: And
he's here today.

00:44:41.436 --> 00:44:43.850
He graduated from
Carnegie Mellon University

00:44:43.850 --> 00:44:47.390
with a Bachelor's
and Master's degree.

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:49.730
We had help from
the grandparents.

00:44:49.730 --> 00:44:51.830
JONES: Help from
the grandparents.

00:44:51.830 --> 00:44:54.830
And would you have to hitchhike
here for the graduation?

00:44:54.830 --> 00:44:58.530
Got no gas money anymore?

00:44:58.530 --> 00:45:02.730
MR. MAHONEY: And we're going
to ask them to pay us back.

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:04.420
JONES: They better
get good jobs.

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:07.904
Do they have good jobs lined up?

00:45:07.904 --> 00:45:08.570
MR. MAHONEY: No.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:16.850
Our daughter's going to get a
PhD in biomedical engineering

00:45:16.850 --> 00:45:19.220
at Purdue University, and our
son's staying here for his

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:20.120
master's.

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:21.950
But they will eventually
have good jobs--

00:45:21.950 --> 00:45:23.351
good, lucrative jobs.

00:45:23.351 --> 00:45:24.350
And they'll pay us back.

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:26.682
JONES: And they'll take care of
their parents in their old age.

00:45:26.682 --> 00:45:27.920
MRS. MAHONEY: Hopefully.

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:30.890
JONES: So did any future time
travelers show up at the time

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:33.740
traveler convention?

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:37.938
MR. MAHONEY: They decided they
rather show up at graduation.

00:45:37.938 --> 00:45:39.710
JONES: That's good.

00:45:39.710 --> 00:45:41.220
Well, it's a
pleasure to meet you.

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:43.780
Thank you for sending
your two kids to MIT.

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:44.680
Any more at home?

00:45:44.680 --> 00:45:46.880
Any other Mahoneys at home
we should be looking for?

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:48.296
MRS. MAHONEY: No,
no younger ones.

00:45:48.296 --> 00:45:48.972
This is it.

00:45:48.972 --> 00:45:50.930
MR. MAHONEY: But there'll
be another generation

00:45:50.930 --> 00:45:53.090
coming in about 30 years.

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:55.512
So now that you guys
are empty nesters,

00:45:55.512 --> 00:45:56.720
now when are you going to do?

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.699
Now that you're broke
and empty-nested,

00:45:59.699 --> 00:46:00.740
what are you going to do?

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:02.480
MRS. MAHONEY: Well,
we travel a lot now.

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:05.560
We're not totally broke.

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:07.910
MR. MAHONEY: And at my school,
we do the first robotics

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:11.700
competition, which is co-founded
by Woodie Flowers and Dean

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:12.980
Segue, and--

00:46:12.980 --> 00:46:13.190
MRS. MAHONEY: Dean Kamen.

00:46:13.190 --> 00:46:14.273
MR. MAHONEY: --this is a--

00:46:14.273 --> 00:46:15.560
Dean Kamen.

00:46:15.560 --> 00:46:18.320
And this is a way in
which the influence of MIT

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:20.630
has spread through the
schools of this nation.

00:46:20.630 --> 00:46:22.730
Robotics is just turning
kids into engineers.

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:24.240
It's just been a lot of fun.

00:46:24.240 --> 00:46:26.060
JONES: So your kids are
really loving that, are they?

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:26.510
MR. MAHONEY: Yeah.

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:27.870
It's just been a great program.

00:46:27.870 --> 00:46:31.070
It's an extension of Course
2.70 into the high schools,

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:34.239
same sort of kit that
we get, and it's neat.

00:46:34.239 --> 00:46:35.780
We do it in the back
of my classroom.

00:46:35.780 --> 00:46:37.274
JONES: Well, that's great.

00:46:37.274 --> 00:46:38.690
Well, it's a
pleasure to meet you.

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:40.820
And we're going to get some
of those kids out at Banneker.

00:46:40.820 --> 00:46:41.782
MR. MAHONEY: Thank you.

00:46:41.782 --> 00:46:42.782
MRS. MAHONEY: Thank you.

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:49.600
ROSKO: Hello again.

00:46:49.600 --> 00:46:52.840
Once again, I'm Tom Rosko,
the Institute Archivist

00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:55.750
and head of special lectures and
archives in the libraries here.

00:46:55.750 --> 00:47:01.130
And that was Marilee
Jones, Dean of Admissions.

00:47:01.130 --> 00:47:04.090
And welcome to you all,
those just arriving

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:07.250
for the ceremonies that
are about to occur.

00:47:07.250 --> 00:47:12.280
Now, most people understand that
MIT is a research institution,

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:15.350
and that new inventions
happen all the time.

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:20.110
And here's a look at the genesis
of a particular recent student

00:47:20.110 --> 00:47:21.221
intervention.

00:47:24.658 --> 00:47:26.140
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:27.712
- I like working
on projects where

00:47:27.712 --> 00:47:29.170
I'm dealing with
the smaller things

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:31.720
in life, the little things
that perturb you every day.

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:33.747
And this problem of
waking up in the morning

00:47:33.747 --> 00:47:36.080
is certainly something that
I used to think about a lot.

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:40.390
When I was waking up I would hit
the snooze button accidentally

00:47:40.390 --> 00:47:43.240
for up to two hours, or
even accidentally turn

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:44.920
the alarm clock off.

00:47:44.920 --> 00:47:46.930
So this is just sort
of a way I could

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:50.410
include some sort of
playfulness inside technology,

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:54.460
and solve a problem
without adding complexity.

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:57.040
Alarm clocks are traditionally
very annoying devices,

00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:59.440
and I wanted to
create something that

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:03.220
was playful and meaningful,
and that could sort of even

00:48:03.220 --> 00:48:05.440
excite some laughter in one
of the most hateful times

00:48:05.440 --> 00:48:05.980
of the day.

00:48:08.980 --> 00:48:11.400
So I took an industrial
design course

00:48:11.400 --> 00:48:16.350
at the MIT Media Lab that
inspired me to create Clocky,

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:19.500
and I came up with the idea
and just sort of prototyped it

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:21.010
for a few days.

00:48:21.010 --> 00:48:23.970
And from there, I think
somebody picked up on it

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:28.650
and realized it was something
that everybody could relate to.

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:31.560
- Part of the goal of consumer
electronics research here

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:34.590
at the MIT Media Lab is to take
advantage of the intelligence

00:48:34.590 --> 00:48:37.200
that we could put
inside ordinary products

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:39.870
to make them more delightful,
to make them do the right thing,

00:48:39.870 --> 00:48:42.330
to make them behave in a
predictable and understandable

00:48:42.330 --> 00:48:43.470
way.

00:48:43.470 --> 00:48:47.150
Clocky is a clock
that has behavior,

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:48.990
and as Gauri likes
to say, it has

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:51.990
the behavior of a somewhat
troublesome pet that you still

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:53.280
love.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:57.640
And in this case, we also
think the behavior is useful.

00:48:57.640 --> 00:48:59.640
And one of the reasons
I believe that we've

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:02.550
seen such a response from press,
and just from ordinary people

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:06.420
to Clocky, is that they
understand exactly what it

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:09.600
does, and they think
that's going to help

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:11.020
them wake up in the morning.

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:12.720
- The concept for
Clocky originally

00:49:12.720 --> 00:49:14.850
was not to use the most
advanced technology,

00:49:14.850 --> 00:49:18.870
but to create a new place for
technology that was playful

00:49:18.870 --> 00:49:24.750
and that has a personal meaning
for an individual's life.

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:27.990
Clocky is made up of a
small computer inside that

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:31.770
is programmed with a random
generating sequence of numbers

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:33.510
that control the speed
and the direction

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.160
that Clocky will move in.

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:36.900
And it's connected
to a couple of motors

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:39.360
and a couple of wheels,
and that does enable

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:42.187
it to move around the room.

00:49:42.187 --> 00:49:43.770
When you hit the
snooze button, Clocky

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:46.850
will roll off the
bedside table and wheel

00:49:46.850 --> 00:49:50.400
away on the floor running in
random directions and speeds,

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:52.770
until he finds a place to rest.

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:54.960
And every day, Clocky will
find a new place to rest,

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:56.839
so it's sort of like
a hide and seek game

00:49:56.839 --> 00:49:58.005
that you play every morning.

00:50:01.870 --> 00:50:04.360
Clocky is of course a research
project at the Media Lab,

00:50:04.360 --> 00:50:08.350
and I'm currently developing
on more prototypes.

00:50:08.350 --> 00:50:12.130
But Clocky could be soon be
in production within a year.

00:50:15.050 --> 00:50:16.790
The Media Lab is a
great environment

00:50:16.790 --> 00:50:19.430
for a project like
this to emerge.

00:50:19.430 --> 00:50:23.240
I've been able to do
everything from fabricating

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:26.240
to programming, to
coming up with ideas

00:50:26.240 --> 00:50:28.420
and thinking about real
world problems, all

00:50:28.420 --> 00:50:32.240
in the space of one lab.

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:36.830
- Well, I think Clocky
answers a need that

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:39.810
is felt across cultures
throughout the world,

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:42.500
and that is being able
to get up in the morning

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:44.300
when one really doesn't want to.

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:46.670
And the snooze bar
makes it very easy

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:48.030
not to get up in the morning.

00:50:48.030 --> 00:50:51.740
Well, in this case, Clocky lets
you use the snooze bar once,

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:55.250
and then you have to go find
the alarm clock in order

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:56.780
to turn it off the second time.

00:50:56.780 --> 00:50:59.390
And by that point,
you're out of bed.

00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:03.810
And it's a very simple
scenario to explain.

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:08.240
Anybody who has an alarm clock
can understand exactly why

00:51:08.240 --> 00:51:10.250
this is an interesting idea.

00:51:10.250 --> 00:51:13.100
And the kinds of responses
we've seen from people who

00:51:13.100 --> 00:51:16.130
have written to us or
who have written on blogs

00:51:16.130 --> 00:51:18.590
on the internet around
the world is that they

00:51:18.590 --> 00:51:19.950
understand what this is about.

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:22.190
They may agree or
they may disagree,

00:51:22.190 --> 00:51:24.460
but they can immediately
see what the point is.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:30.100
[END PLAYBACK]

