00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:40.400
ROSKO: Now we're going to take
another look at a research

00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:43.152
video about a recent invention.

00:54:48.344 --> 00:54:49.760
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:52.130
- This is Toddler.

00:54:52.130 --> 00:54:54.570
And this is Dr. Russ Tedrake.

00:54:54.570 --> 00:54:57.910
Together, they're covering
new ground at MIT.

00:54:57.910 --> 00:55:01.110
- So we call the robot Toddler
because it learns to walk,

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:03.270
and because it
toddles when it walks.

00:55:03.270 --> 00:55:05.490
- Toddler's a passive
dynamic walker.

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:09.180
That means he's a simple
mechanical device with joints.

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:11.850
In the past, the only way
these robots could walk

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:13.830
was to trundle down a slope.

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:16.060
They couldn't walk
on flat surfaces.

00:55:16.060 --> 00:55:18.520
This is where Toddler steps
to the front of the class.

00:55:18.520 --> 00:55:21.780
He's simple, like the
passive dynamic robots,

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:24.870
and he could walk on
flat surfaces, too.

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:29.250
The difference is, he uses
very little power to do so.

00:55:29.250 --> 00:55:31.260
- We're letting the
dynamics of the robot

00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:32.990
solve most of the
control problem,

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:34.573
and we just add a
little bit of energy

00:55:34.573 --> 00:55:36.700
here or there to kind of
push and pull the robot.

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:39.431
And that's a much more
energy efficient strategy

00:55:39.431 --> 00:55:40.680
for designing a walking robot.

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:43.295
It turned out that the dynamic
walking was a great way

00:55:43.295 --> 00:55:44.670
to make a system
that could learn

00:55:44.670 --> 00:55:46.440
in real time on a real machine.

00:55:46.440 --> 00:55:49.740
- The robot has a computer
and battery packs on board,

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:52.470
and the steering is controlled
with a wireless connection

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:56.910
and a laptop, but Toddler
does a lot of the work itself.

00:55:56.910 --> 00:56:00.300
Because the robot already
knew how to walk down a slope,

00:56:00.300 --> 00:56:02.400
it required less work
to find a solution

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:04.560
for walking on the flat.

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:06.960
Dr. Tedrake gave
Toddler the ability

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:09.930
to use sensors that
communicate with motors.

00:56:09.930 --> 00:56:12.000
So with each step
the robot takes,

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:13.922
it's able to make adjustments.

00:56:13.922 --> 00:56:15.380
- And that's what
we call learning.

00:56:15.380 --> 00:56:17.910
It's a simple idea,
but it's the best way

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:20.790
we can embody what we think the
nervous system is doing, too,

00:56:20.790 --> 00:56:22.470
when we're talking
about learning.

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:25.650
When we put it on the flat, and
we start asking it to learn,

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:28.230
then it can actually change
the parameters quickly enough

00:56:28.230 --> 00:56:30.960
that in we say, 20
minutes, to be safe--

00:56:30.960 --> 00:56:33.200
in 20 minutes, it learns
to walk on the flat,

00:56:33.200 --> 00:56:36.360
and it continues to adapt
to the terrain as it goes.

00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:39.180
- Toddler can also be adjusted
to learn very quickly,

00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:41.797
but with faster
learning comes a price.

00:56:41.797 --> 00:56:43.380
- There's this very
interesting result

00:56:43.380 --> 00:56:46.080
in learning theory, which tells
that you can't have learning

00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:47.730
without generalization.

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:49.819
The problem is, when
you start generalizing,

00:56:49.819 --> 00:56:52.110
then there's a possibility
that you'll overwrite things

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:53.940
that you've learned in the past.

00:56:53.940 --> 00:56:55.860
- So if it learns
faster, the robot

00:56:55.860 --> 00:56:57.840
will walk from
linoleum to carpet

00:56:57.840 --> 00:56:59.760
with only a quick adjustment.

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:03.480
However, the return to linoleum
may take a little longer.

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:07.200
But it's easy to see that
Toddler has in fact learned,

00:57:07.200 --> 00:57:11.370
and that ability has enabled
Toddler to tackle new terrain.

00:57:11.370 --> 00:57:14.700
- It's just such a thrill
whenever you see the robot

00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:17.577
walking, and especially when
you do it in a public place

00:57:17.577 --> 00:57:19.410
and you can see all the
people go, oh, look,

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:20.970
it's a cool little robot.

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.830
- So how does
studying a robot that

00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:26.310
walks like a human
actually benefit humans?

00:57:26.310 --> 00:57:28.620
- So by just looking
at how a machine walks

00:57:28.620 --> 00:57:30.990
and understanding that, we
can actually learn a lot

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:32.400
about how humans walk.

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:34.309
Ultimately, down the line--

00:57:34.309 --> 00:57:36.600
far down the line-- if we
understand the motor learning

00:57:36.600 --> 00:57:39.540
system better, then there's a
potential that we can use that

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:42.690
information to design
rehabilitation programs--

00:57:42.690 --> 00:57:44.490
even neural
prosthetics-- to help

00:57:44.490 --> 00:57:48.107
people recover from injuries
or augment their performance.

00:57:48.107 --> 00:57:50.190
There's a lot of different
potential applications.

00:57:50.190 --> 00:57:53.950
- As for Toddler, he's got a big
brother already in the works.

00:57:53.950 --> 00:57:55.800
This new version
includes changes

00:57:55.800 --> 00:57:58.590
such as smaller
feet, new components,

00:57:58.590 --> 00:58:01.050
and a fine set of knees.

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:03.480
Knees that can both walk
and swing through a step

00:58:03.480 --> 00:58:06.510
will enable the robot
to be more versatile.

00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:09.060
It's also one of the
steps being taken to make

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:11.340
the robot appear more human--

00:58:11.340 --> 00:58:14.710
something Dr. Tedrake thinks is
very important for the future

00:58:14.710 --> 00:58:16.050
of robot design.

00:58:16.050 --> 00:58:18.270
- When you see a robot
that looks like a human,

00:58:18.270 --> 00:58:20.228
you kind of have a tendency
to reach out to it,

00:58:20.228 --> 00:58:23.100
and it really helps, I
think, with the human-robot

00:58:23.100 --> 00:58:23.910
interaction.

00:58:23.910 --> 00:58:25.590
- Dr. Tedrake says
there is still

00:58:25.590 --> 00:58:28.110
much to learn from
the original Toddler,

00:58:28.110 --> 00:58:29.964
and from its new brother.

00:58:29.964 --> 00:58:32.130
- It's an iterative process
where we basically start

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:34.530
adding components back
in and see just how

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:36.037
far the learning can take us.

00:58:36.037 --> 00:58:38.370
And once you've built one,
and once you watched it walk,

00:58:38.370 --> 00:58:39.960
the idea really sells itself.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:42.750
I think it's the first step
in a long road of building

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:44.547
better robots, more
efficient robots,

00:58:44.547 --> 00:58:45.880
and learning systems for robots.

00:58:45.880 --> 00:58:47.842
[END PLAYBACK]

00:58:47.842 --> 00:58:49.550
JONES: So let's see
what's happening over

00:58:49.550 --> 00:58:51.270
in Johnson with our graduates.

00:58:55.260 --> 00:58:57.450
They look like
they're all in line.

00:58:57.450 --> 00:59:01.000
It looks like they're
in alpha order.

00:59:01.000 --> 00:59:04.640
If they step out of line,
they don't graduate.

00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:07.308
How many MIT students
can follow directions?

00:59:14.020 --> 00:59:16.630
Good, a quick commercial break--

00:59:16.630 --> 00:59:22.810
for those of you who want the
CD or video of this graduation,

00:59:22.810 --> 00:59:24.760
you can order them
at the information

00:59:24.760 --> 00:59:27.700
tables on either side in
the back of the court.

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:31.505
Every graduate will be
photographed as they graduate,

00:59:31.505 --> 00:59:33.880
and you may want to purchase
one of those photos as well.

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:36.830
And you can arrange that at
those tables in the back.

00:59:36.830 --> 00:59:38.440
So here, we're back with family.

00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:40.480
Here's a wonderful
family from Canada.

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:42.290
So would you introduce yourself?

00:59:42.290 --> 00:59:44.120
GILBERT: Hi, I'm Marcy Gilbert.

00:59:44.120 --> 00:59:45.680
SCHACTER: And I'm
Shalom Schacter.

00:59:45.680 --> 00:59:47.950
JONES: And who is your graduate?

00:59:47.950 --> 00:59:49.450
GILBERT: Matye Gilbert-Schacter.

00:59:49.450 --> 00:59:51.190
JONES: So his name is Matye?

00:59:51.190 --> 00:59:51.940
SCHACTER: Correct.

00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:53.199
JONES: And you're from where?

00:59:53.199 --> 00:59:54.740
SCHACTER: We're from
Toronto, Canada.

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:55.810
JONES: In Toronto.

00:59:55.810 --> 00:59:58.810
So you just reminded me that
this weather is not necessarily

00:59:58.810 --> 01:00:01.720
California, it's
actually Toronto weather.

01:00:01.720 --> 01:00:03.772
So we thank the Canadians.

01:00:03.772 --> 01:00:05.980
The Canadians-- you know
what, a Canadian was a first

01:00:05.980 --> 01:00:08.350
international student at MIT--

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:10.690
1866.

01:00:10.690 --> 01:00:14.470
SCHACTER: The year before
Canada was confederated.

01:00:14.470 --> 01:00:16.240
JONES: So a true pioneer.

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:18.160
So tell me a little about Matye.

01:00:18.160 --> 01:00:20.770
SCHACTER: Well, Matye has been
only here for three years,

01:00:20.770 --> 01:00:22.270
and he's had to take it all in.

01:00:22.270 --> 01:00:25.480
He was a transfer student
from University of Toronto.

01:00:25.480 --> 01:00:29.050
He switched disciplines
from engineering to physics,

01:00:29.050 --> 01:00:32.020
and he'll be graduating with
a physics major and a minor

01:00:32.020 --> 01:00:33.910
in mathematics.

01:00:33.910 --> 01:00:35.290
JONES: In three years.

01:00:35.290 --> 01:00:35.990
SCHACTER: Yes.

01:00:35.990 --> 01:00:37.510
He's really enjoyed his stay.

01:00:37.510 --> 01:00:40.847
He's dreamed as a
child of coming to MIT.

01:00:40.847 --> 01:00:42.430
We tried to support
him and his dream,

01:00:42.430 --> 01:00:43.900
but he made it come
true, and we're

01:00:43.900 --> 01:00:46.850
very happy to be here to
see it come to fruition.

01:00:46.850 --> 01:00:47.950
JONES: That's wonderful.

01:00:47.950 --> 01:00:49.390
You know, we hear
about stories like this

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:51.473
where students have been
dreaming their whole life

01:00:51.473 --> 01:00:52.001
to come.

01:00:52.001 --> 01:00:53.750
And mostly we hear
those stories of people

01:00:53.750 --> 01:00:55.160
of students we turn down.

01:00:55.160 --> 01:00:57.280
So it's always wonderful
to hear that we actually

01:00:57.280 --> 01:01:00.670
took people who've been dreaming
their whole lives about MIT.

01:01:00.670 --> 01:01:01.870
It's wonderful.

01:01:01.870 --> 01:01:04.195
So tell me a little
about Matye as a person.

01:01:04.195 --> 01:01:07.430
GILBERT: Matye's a
wonderful person.

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:11.620
And he's going to be moving
on to Philadelphia, where

01:01:11.620 --> 01:01:14.920
he'll be a trader with
one of the companies.

01:01:14.920 --> 01:01:16.810
JONES: So he has sibs
at home in Toronto?

01:01:16.810 --> 01:01:17.351
GILBERT: Yes.

01:01:17.351 --> 01:01:21.010
He has one sibling who is
living in Waterloo right now,

01:01:21.010 --> 01:01:24.150
attending the University
of Waterloo in engineering.

01:01:24.150 --> 01:01:27.020
JONES: In engineering So
does this run in the family,

01:01:27.020 --> 01:01:29.220
does it, this engineering thing?

01:01:29.220 --> 01:01:30.460
SCHACTER: No.

01:01:30.460 --> 01:01:36.320
My father in fact is a practical
person in terms of technology.

01:01:36.320 --> 01:01:39.700
But we're both
social scientists,

01:01:39.700 --> 01:01:42.880
although I did start myself
in the physics and mathematics

01:01:42.880 --> 01:01:45.110
as an undergraduate.

01:01:45.110 --> 01:01:46.874
JONES: So was Matye
on the screen before?

01:01:46.874 --> 01:01:47.790
SCHACTER: Yes, he was.

01:01:47.790 --> 01:01:48.539
JONES: Oh, he was.

01:01:48.539 --> 01:01:50.080
I thought I remembered his name.

01:01:50.080 --> 01:01:52.990
So he was with the
first group of students.

01:01:52.990 --> 01:01:53.980
SCHACTER: That's right.

01:01:53.980 --> 01:01:58.390
He's been active in many of
the special resources at MIT.

01:01:58.390 --> 01:02:00.430
He's been active in sports.

01:02:00.430 --> 01:02:03.190
He took pistols, which
was a shock to us.

01:02:03.190 --> 01:02:05.410
He was active in the
student organizations--

01:02:05.410 --> 01:02:08.290
he became president of
Hillel, the Jewish student

01:02:08.290 --> 01:02:09.980
organization.

01:02:09.980 --> 01:02:12.340
And he was active
in his fraternity.

01:02:12.340 --> 01:02:14.632
JONES: I remember Matye now.

01:02:14.632 --> 01:02:16.686
Oh yeah.

01:02:16.686 --> 01:02:18.685
GILBERT: It's nice to
know that he's remembered.

01:02:18.685 --> 01:02:20.410
JONES: Oh yes.

01:02:20.410 --> 01:02:22.790
Every student at MIT is
handpicked, you know.

01:02:22.790 --> 01:02:23.650
It's true.

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:26.470
And we don't take very many
transfer students each day,

01:02:26.470 --> 01:02:28.510
so this is good.

01:02:28.510 --> 01:02:30.230
So where does he live here?

01:02:30.230 --> 01:02:33.430
SCHACTER: Well, he's moved into
the fraternity house that's

01:02:33.430 --> 01:02:35.140
on the other side of
the Charles River,

01:02:35.140 --> 01:02:37.510
but for the first
year that he was here,

01:02:37.510 --> 01:02:40.940
he was in a number
of the residences.

01:02:40.940 --> 01:02:43.240
JONES: So I'm so happy he's
had a good experience here,

01:02:43.240 --> 01:02:44.590
and it's a pleasure to meet you.

01:02:44.590 --> 01:02:47.211
And now we're going to
turn it back to Tom.

01:02:47.211 --> 01:02:48.002
GILBERT: Thank you.

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:54.820
ROSKO: Now, MIT
is very well known

01:02:54.820 --> 01:02:59.110
for its first-class engineering
education and research.

01:02:59.110 --> 01:03:03.790
But MIT also has an
excellent arts program,

01:03:03.790 --> 01:03:06.610
and it compliments all
the other disciplines.

01:03:06.610 --> 01:03:08.740
In fact, the arts
and humanities have

01:03:08.740 --> 01:03:11.980
been a part of MIT's
educational experience

01:03:11.980 --> 01:03:14.280
since MIT's very founding.

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:17.170
And when William Barton
Rogers, MIT's founder

01:03:17.170 --> 01:03:19.390
and first President,
drew up the plan

01:03:19.390 --> 01:03:22.270
of what the education
at MIT should be,

01:03:22.270 --> 01:03:25.800
the humanities and arts were
a key part of the curriculum.

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:29.080
And over the years,
that part that

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:34.000
includes humanities and
the arts and its importance

01:03:34.000 --> 01:03:38.970
has always been a part of MIT's
curriculum and its education.

01:03:41.820 --> 01:03:48.060
And in fact, MIT prides itself
on educating lifelong learners

01:03:48.060 --> 01:03:52.720
who understand the societal
and humanistic effects

01:03:52.720 --> 01:03:55.220
of technology and science.

01:03:55.220 --> 01:03:58.640
And now we offer you
an excerpt of a program

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:01.055
overview of the arts at MIT.

01:04:03.644 --> 01:04:04.310
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:04:04.310 --> 01:04:08.290
- One morning, I came up the
steps of Massachusetts Avenue,

01:04:08.290 --> 01:04:11.890
and walked through the door.

01:04:11.890 --> 01:04:18.190
And the entire space had been
transformed into a wheat field.

01:04:18.190 --> 01:04:20.620
Innovative visual arts projects
like that are happening

01:04:20.620 --> 01:04:23.680
all the time on campus.

01:04:23.680 --> 01:04:29.740
The arts at MIT, like science
and engineering at MIT,

01:04:29.740 --> 01:04:35.020
are on the cutting edge
of their disciplines.

01:04:35.020 --> 01:04:38.465
They are serving the students.

01:04:41.440 --> 01:04:43.690
And in the way they're
serving the students,

01:04:43.690 --> 01:04:46.392
ultimately they're going to
be serving the entire society.

01:04:49.368 --> 01:04:53.100
- At MIT, you'll find some of
the best musicians-- virtuosos,

01:04:53.100 --> 01:04:53.600
completely.

01:04:53.600 --> 01:04:56.490
A person who lives next door
to me has perfect pitch,

01:04:56.490 --> 01:04:58.460
and if you name any
song whatsoever he

01:04:58.460 --> 01:05:00.530
can strum it out on his guitar.

01:05:00.530 --> 01:05:02.180
If it has multiple
lines of music,

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:04.430
he can strum out all
lines on his guitar.

01:05:04.430 --> 01:05:07.310
And you can see that it's
indicative of the level

01:05:07.310 --> 01:05:10.910
of quality of the MIT symphony
orchestra, the MIT concert

01:05:10.910 --> 01:05:12.740
choir, et cetera.

01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:14.720
- Last year I got to
premier a song cycle

01:05:14.720 --> 01:05:18.350
written by an MIT composer,
and that was a lot of fun.

01:05:18.350 --> 01:05:21.020
Modern poetry, modern
music, and everything.

01:05:21.020 --> 01:05:22.912
- Everyone at MIT
cares about something,

01:05:22.912 --> 01:05:24.620
whether it's their
classes or their UROP,

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:26.402
or some student
activity they do.

01:05:26.402 --> 01:05:28.610
There's always something
that they really care about.

01:05:28.610 --> 01:05:31.790
- The thing about MIT students
that I most appreciate

01:05:31.790 --> 01:05:34.940
is the elegance
of their thinking,

01:05:34.940 --> 01:05:38.755
and the fact that it's sort
of "live and let live" here.

01:05:38.755 --> 01:05:40.695
[GROUP SINGING]

01:05:48.460 --> 01:05:53.170
There's a point of concentration
that any discipline requires,

01:05:53.170 --> 01:05:55.790
whether it's engineering
or it's directing

01:05:55.790 --> 01:05:57.700
for acting, or painting.

01:05:57.700 --> 01:06:01.160
That point of concentration
is the focal point.

01:06:01.160 --> 01:06:04.630
It is the thing around which
you organize your thinking

01:06:04.630 --> 01:06:06.700
and your technique.

01:06:06.700 --> 01:06:09.640
And so the fact that there is
ensemble work, where people

01:06:09.640 --> 01:06:12.550
learn to work
collaboratively, is something

01:06:12.550 --> 01:06:16.349
that I see that people take
into their labs in a way

01:06:16.349 --> 01:06:18.474
that you would hope that
labs work collaboratively.

01:06:18.474 --> 01:06:19.956
[MUSIC - ROB THOMAS, "SMOOTH"]

01:06:19.956 --> 01:06:21.438
- (SINGING) Cuz
you're so smooth.

01:06:24.402 --> 01:06:28.354
And it's just like the
ocean under the moon,

01:06:28.354 --> 01:06:32.800
it's the same as the
emotion that I get from you.

01:06:32.800 --> 01:06:37.246
You got the kind of loving
that can be so smooth, yeah.

01:06:37.246 --> 01:06:41.482
Give me your heart, make it
real, or else forget about it.

01:06:41.482 --> 01:06:43.912
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:46.342 --> 01:06:47.300
- I'll kill you!

01:06:47.300 --> 01:06:47.800
- Ooh!

01:06:47.800 --> 01:06:49.258
He kills me.

01:06:49.258 --> 01:06:51.220
Ooh!

01:06:51.220 --> 01:06:53.140
- Well, right now,
it's sort of--

01:06:53.140 --> 01:06:54.830
theater is a way
I can investigate

01:06:54.830 --> 01:06:56.530
my own sort of
identity, especially

01:06:56.530 --> 01:06:57.940
my own cultural identity.

01:06:57.940 --> 01:07:01.752
But I also enjoy theater because
it's such a collaborative art,

01:07:01.752 --> 01:07:04.210
and it's a lot like engineering,
which is one of my majors.

01:07:04.210 --> 01:07:06.430
And I also intend to
pursue engineering,

01:07:06.430 --> 01:07:07.841
and that is so collaborative.

01:07:07.841 --> 01:07:10.090
And so you get a lot of
different people's perspective

01:07:10.090 --> 01:07:10.590
on things.

01:07:15.230 --> 01:07:18.800
- We find our students--

01:07:18.800 --> 01:07:22.310
a certain number of them go
on to graduate study in music

01:07:22.310 --> 01:07:23.360
and in the arts.

01:07:23.360 --> 01:07:26.390
Some of them go on to become
leaders of arts organizations

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:27.900
within their communities.

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:30.920
Some of them go on to sing in
organizations or performing

01:07:30.920 --> 01:07:34.370
organizations, like the
Tanglewood Festival Chorus.

01:07:34.370 --> 01:07:39.890
And in a couple of notable
situations, I have myself

01:07:39.890 --> 01:07:42.730
a number of times have been
engaged by organizations

01:07:42.730 --> 01:07:48.020
whose presidents were either in
my class at MIT 20 years ago,

01:07:48.020 --> 01:07:50.104
or had been at MIT
years before that.

01:07:50.104 --> 01:07:52.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:08:04.430 --> 01:08:09.430
My first visit to MIT, when
I walked through the halls,

01:08:09.430 --> 01:08:11.540
I remember passing through
the math department

01:08:11.540 --> 01:08:14.630
on the way to Building
14, where we used to be.

01:08:14.630 --> 01:08:20.029
And I looked in the door and
saw mathematicians hunched

01:08:20.029 --> 01:08:23.240
in carols, and I
felt right at home,

01:08:23.240 --> 01:08:25.332
because that looked
exactly like walking down

01:08:25.332 --> 01:08:27.290
the halls of Juilliard
into the practice rooms,

01:08:27.290 --> 01:08:31.960
and seeing people working
personally on something.

01:08:31.960 --> 01:08:34.670
- One of the amazing
things about MIT students

01:08:34.670 --> 01:08:38.180
is the incredible wide range
of interests and abilities

01:08:38.180 --> 01:08:41.720
that extend far beyond the
intellectual and the academic.

01:08:41.720 --> 01:08:45.319
And our special guest with us,
this section of the concert

01:08:45.319 --> 01:08:46.939
is no exception to that.

01:08:46.939 --> 01:08:48.330
His name is Jonathan Lee.

01:08:48.330 --> 01:08:50.290
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:53.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:09:12.380 --> 01:09:15.710
- I think that being involved
in activities like this

01:09:15.710 --> 01:09:18.140
only enhances your education.

01:09:18.140 --> 01:09:20.810
I think it's important
that while you're

01:09:20.810 --> 01:09:25.080
at school to focus on things
besides your own research.

01:09:25.080 --> 01:09:28.925
- So people come here as
scientists, as engineers,

01:09:28.925 --> 01:09:30.430
as mathematicians.

01:09:30.430 --> 01:09:32.554
But when they are making
art, they're artists.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:46.260
- I think that it's
a way of chilling out

01:09:46.260 --> 01:09:48.279
some of the stresses you have.

01:09:48.279 --> 01:09:50.310
It's a way of looking
at the things you're

01:09:50.310 --> 01:09:53.790
doing with a broader picture.

01:09:53.790 --> 01:10:05.580
[GROUP SINGING "WE ARE THE
 ENGINEERS"]

01:10:05.580 --> 01:10:07.800
- So you shouldn't
think that MIT

01:10:07.800 --> 01:10:10.680
has no art, because they've
got them like sprawling

01:10:10.680 --> 01:10:12.420
all over the place.

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:15.300
- Well, now, the advice I
give to incoming freshmen

01:10:15.300 --> 01:10:20.040
consistently is don't
leave your trumpet at home.

01:10:20.040 --> 01:10:21.750
You can use it here.

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:22.990
- (SINGING) Engineers!

01:10:22.990 --> 01:10:25.300
- Go, go, go, go, go, go--

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:25.800
- Engineers!

01:10:25.800 --> 01:10:28.790
- We are, we are, we
are the engineers.

01:10:28.790 --> 01:10:29.510
- Engineers!

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:32.250
- We are, we are, we
are the engineers.

01:10:32.250 --> 01:10:32.750
- Engineers!

01:10:32.750 --> 01:10:35.550
- We are, we are, we
are the engineers.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:36.296
- Engineers!

01:10:36.296 --> 01:10:39.606
- We are, we are, we
are the engineers.

01:10:39.606 --> 01:10:42.790
- A lady and an engineer
were sitting in the park,

01:10:42.790 --> 01:10:46.740
and the engineer was working
on some research after dark.

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:49.624
His scientific method
was a wonder to observe.

01:10:49.624 --> 01:10:50.540
While his right hand--

01:10:50.540 --> 01:10:51.123
[END PLAYBACK]

01:10:51.123 --> 01:10:52.460
JONES: Okay.

01:10:52.460 --> 01:10:54.440
We got progress.

01:10:54.440 --> 01:10:57.500
I understand the procession
may have already begun.

01:10:57.500 --> 01:11:00.320
Let's take a look at what's
happening in Johnson.

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:03.130
Here they come.

01:11:03.130 --> 01:11:07.300
Just so you know,
they're across Mass Ave.

01:11:07.300 --> 01:11:09.340
in the Johnson Athletic Center.

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:11.020
They're going to
come across, they're

01:11:11.020 --> 01:11:13.450
going to come down
Mass Ave., they're

01:11:13.450 --> 01:11:16.060
going to curve
around Memorial Drive

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:18.640
down here to the
bottom of the court.

01:11:18.640 --> 01:11:22.900
And very shortly, the
commencement will begin,

01:11:22.900 --> 01:11:26.680
and they will enter straight
up the middle aisle.

01:11:26.680 --> 01:11:29.320
So this is-- we've
already begun.

01:11:29.320 --> 01:11:31.160
So we have one more
interview for a family,

01:11:31.160 --> 01:11:33.160
because then you have to
quickly grab your seats

01:11:33.160 --> 01:11:35.119
and get ready for this big show.

01:11:35.119 --> 01:11:36.910
So why don't you
introduce yourselves here,

01:11:36.910 --> 01:11:38.710
and who are you here for?

01:11:38.710 --> 01:11:40.720
FATHER 1: My name is
Melvin [? Magny. ?]

01:11:40.720 --> 01:11:45.420
My son is Eric Magny, and he's
graduating from biology today.

01:11:45.420 --> 01:11:47.860
STUDENT 16: I'm his
sister, Sonya Magny.

01:11:47.860 --> 01:11:50.275
MOTHER 3: I am [INAUDIBLE]
Magny, Eric's mother.

01:11:50.275 --> 01:11:52.540
STUDENT 17: And I'm Melvin
Magny, Eric's brother.

01:11:52.540 --> 01:11:56.010
JONES: And Melvin, you
are a junior here, right?

01:11:56.010 --> 01:11:58.900
Good, so another brother at MIT.

01:11:58.900 --> 01:12:00.220
So tell me about Eric.

01:12:00.220 --> 01:12:03.080
So what's his degree
going to be in?

01:12:03.080 --> 01:12:04.250
STUDENT 16: Biology.

01:12:04.250 --> 01:12:06.230
JONES: It's good
to be in biology.

01:12:06.230 --> 01:12:08.260
And then what is
Eric going to do?

01:12:08.260 --> 01:12:11.210
STUDENT 16: He's going
to UCLA for med school.

01:12:11.210 --> 01:12:12.785
JONES: He's going
to be a doctor.

01:12:12.785 --> 01:12:13.629
STUDENT 16: Yep.

01:12:13.629 --> 01:12:14.420
JONES: That's good.

01:12:14.420 --> 01:12:17.870
This is how he's going to
take care of you parents, huh?

01:12:17.870 --> 01:12:19.697
So you which department
are you in, Melvin?

01:12:19.697 --> 01:12:21.030
STUDENT 17: I'm in biology, too.

01:12:21.030 --> 01:12:22.100
JONES: And you're
in biology as well.

01:12:22.100 --> 01:12:23.060
Same kind of track.

01:12:23.060 --> 01:12:24.414
STUDENT 17: Basically, yep.

01:12:24.414 --> 01:12:25.580
JONES: Are you guys doctors?

01:12:25.580 --> 01:12:26.450
FATHER 1: Yes.

01:12:26.450 --> 01:12:27.170
MOTHER 3: Yes.

01:12:27.170 --> 01:12:29.910
JONES: Oh, you're an
all doctor family.

01:12:29.910 --> 01:12:31.340
So if you have an
emergency here,

01:12:31.340 --> 01:12:33.140
we know who to call right now.

01:12:33.140 --> 01:12:34.040
And how about you?

01:12:34.040 --> 01:12:35.570
So you're a junior
in high school?

01:12:35.570 --> 01:12:38.209
STUDENT 16: Yes, in Florida.

01:12:38.209 --> 01:12:38.750
JONES: Where?

01:12:38.750 --> 01:12:39.583
STUDENT 16: Florida.

01:12:39.583 --> 01:12:40.344
JONES: In Florida?

01:12:40.344 --> 01:12:41.340
STUDENT 16: Mhm.

01:12:41.340 --> 01:12:44.480
JONES: And then what
are you going to do?

01:12:44.480 --> 01:12:47.630
STUDENT 16: Hopefully
come to MIT, I don't know.

01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:50.650
JONES: Well, you're talking
to the right person.

01:12:50.650 --> 01:12:53.790
So where is your family from?

01:12:53.790 --> 01:12:54.540
MOTHER 3: Florida.

01:12:54.540 --> 01:12:55.860
STUDENT 16: Florida.

01:12:55.860 --> 01:12:57.810
MOTHER 3: We are from
Fort Pierce, Florida.

01:12:57.810 --> 01:12:59.020
JONES: Where's Fort Pierce?

01:12:59.020 --> 01:13:00.482
MOTHER 3: It's in--

01:13:00.482 --> 01:13:01.930
STUDENT 16: Hurricane.

01:13:01.930 --> 01:13:05.774
MOTHER 3: It's 100
miles north of--

01:13:05.774 --> 01:13:06.440
FATHER 1: Miami.

01:13:06.440 --> 01:13:07.020
North of Miami.

01:13:07.020 --> 01:13:08.390
MOTHER 3: It could
be Miami or Orlando.

01:13:08.390 --> 01:13:10.999
JONES: So how many hurricanes
did you get hit by last year?

01:13:10.999 --> 01:13:12.040
STUDENT 16: Two in a row.

01:13:12.040 --> 01:13:13.070
JONES: Two in a row.

01:13:13.070 --> 01:13:14.590
Everything's okay
with your home?

01:13:14.590 --> 01:13:16.322
MOTHER 3: No, no.

01:13:16.322 --> 01:13:17.030
JONES: Bad story.

01:13:17.030 --> 01:13:20.440
So tell me about
Eric and Melvin.

01:13:20.440 --> 01:13:24.250
STUDENT 17: Eric's a great
guy, and he'll be here soon.

01:13:24.250 --> 01:13:26.350
And I mean, he has
really given himself

01:13:26.350 --> 01:13:28.510
to MIT these four
years, and he's

01:13:28.510 --> 01:13:29.990
one of the reasons I'm here.

01:13:29.990 --> 01:13:32.720
And he also took a lot
from it, so he's also

01:13:32.720 --> 01:13:34.270
someone to learn from.

01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:37.662
JONES: And so you said he would
perform at any place, any time.

01:13:37.662 --> 01:13:38.370
STUDENT 17: Yeah.

01:13:38.370 --> 01:13:40.960
I mean, he was big
in the culture shows,

01:13:40.960 --> 01:13:44.770
he emceed two culture shows,
he was varsity tennis all four

01:13:44.770 --> 01:13:46.360
years, he did research.

01:13:46.360 --> 01:13:49.300
He was an active member of
our house, the vise president

01:13:49.300 --> 01:13:50.950
of our house, PB.

01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:54.130
And just all around,
he was all over MIT.

01:13:54.130 --> 01:13:59.620
JONES: So he sounds like a very
active and fun student, huh?

01:13:59.620 --> 01:14:00.370
So how about you?

01:14:00.370 --> 01:14:01.840
Are you the same way?

01:14:01.840 --> 01:14:03.772
STUDENT 17: I'm kind
of the same way.

01:14:03.772 --> 01:14:05.230
JONES: You're kind
of the same way?

01:14:05.230 --> 01:14:06.100
Are you a performer too?

01:14:06.100 --> 01:14:07.724
STUDENT 17: Yeah, I
do some performing.

01:14:07.724 --> 01:14:10.600
I do some class council
work, same varsity tennis

01:14:10.600 --> 01:14:14.810
and research, and basically
pre-med track, so.

01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:17.152
JONES: So next year,
you're going to be here--

01:14:17.152 --> 01:14:18.860
is it next year or
the year after they're

01:14:18.860 --> 01:14:19.610
going to be here for you?

01:14:19.610 --> 01:14:20.600
STUDENT 17: Two years.

01:14:20.600 --> 01:14:21.530
JONES: Two more years.

01:14:21.530 --> 01:14:22.274
Okay.

01:14:22.274 --> 01:14:23.690
Well, it's a
pleasure to meet you,

01:14:23.690 --> 01:14:25.340
and you better get your
seats, because they're coming,

01:14:25.340 --> 01:14:27.650
and they're going to be coming
around the corner any second.

01:14:27.650 --> 01:14:28.460
Thanks very much.

01:14:28.460 --> 01:14:28.955
MOTHER 3: Pleasure meeting you.

01:14:28.955 --> 01:14:30.040
JONES: Oh, you're welcome.

01:14:30.040 --> 01:14:33.800
And now, I'm going to turn you
over to Tom Rosko, my buddy.

01:14:33.800 --> 01:14:37.490
You'll notice he's at the top
of the Court at this stage.

01:14:37.490 --> 01:14:38.390
Wave to Tom.

01:14:38.390 --> 01:14:39.607
Hi, Tom!

01:14:39.607 --> 01:14:40.190
Tom has some--

01:14:40.190 --> 01:14:41.023
ROSKO: Hi everybody.

01:14:41.023 --> 01:14:44.460
JONES: --announcements for
everyone, so listen up.

01:14:44.460 --> 01:14:47.910
ROSKO: I have just a few
public service announcements.

01:14:47.910 --> 01:14:49.680
We know that each of
you is very anxious

01:14:49.680 --> 01:14:52.696
to get the best photograph or
the best look at your graduate,

01:14:52.696 --> 01:14:54.570
but we just ask that
you be courteous and not

01:14:54.570 --> 01:14:58.597
stand on the chairs
or block the aisles,

01:14:58.597 --> 01:15:00.930
and just think about the
people that are trying to watch

01:15:00.930 --> 01:15:01.979
as well from behind you.

01:15:09.386 --> 01:15:10.760
And also, there's
a medical tent.

01:15:10.760 --> 01:15:13.677
It's located at the
rear of Killian Court.

01:15:13.677 --> 01:15:15.635
And the commencement
program contains the words

01:15:15.635 --> 01:15:18.100
to the national
anthem and MIT's song.

01:15:18.100 --> 01:15:20.600
And we also request that if you
have cell phones, if you put

01:15:20.600 --> 01:15:22.860
them in a non-audible position.

01:15:22.860 --> 01:15:25.040
And now, I'd like to
say that the MIT color

01:15:25.040 --> 01:15:27.590
guard is about to
come up representing

01:15:27.590 --> 01:15:29.990
the MIT police and the ROTC--

01:15:29.990 --> 01:15:33.500
I believe-- with the
presentation of the colors.

01:15:33.500 --> 01:15:37.105
And then-- sorry,
just the MIT police.

01:15:39.720 --> 01:15:42.030
And after that,
we'll hand you over

01:15:42.030 --> 01:15:46.820
to Larry Isaacson and
the MIT Brass Ensemble.

01:15:55.210 --> 01:15:57.460
So once again, I was Tom Rosko--

01:15:57.460 --> 01:15:59.410
I am Tom Rosko, the
Institute Archivist here,

01:15:59.410 --> 01:16:02.170
and my partner was Marilee
Jones, Dean of Admissions.

01:16:02.170 --> 01:16:06.520
And we hope you enjoyed what
we did for you this morning,

01:16:06.520 --> 01:16:08.860
and also that-- thank you--

01:16:08.860 --> 01:16:11.050
and also that the
procession has begun.

01:16:11.050 --> 01:16:14.110
Congratulations to
the graduates of 2005,

01:16:14.110 --> 01:16:15.587
and we hope you
enjoy the ceremony.

01:16:15.587 --> 01:16:16.420
Thank you very much.

01:16:31.390 --> 01:19:29.034
[MUSIC - BACH, "SHEEP MAY SAFELY
 GRAZE"]

01:20:53.365 --> 01:20:55.361
[NO AUDIO]

01:21:31.788 --> 01:24:28.434
[MUSIC - HARDIMAN, "LORD OF THE
 DANCE"]

01:24:56.877 --> 01:24:58.873
[NO AUDIO]

01:25:44.282 --> 01:25:46.278
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:28:32.445 --> 01:28:34.441
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:37:04.300 --> 01:37:06.078
JONES: Yes, we're ready.

01:37:06.078 --> 01:37:09.072
Can you hear us, Larry?

01:37:09.072 --> 01:37:11.068
Okay.

01:37:11.068 --> 01:37:13.064
[FANFARE MUSIC PLAYING]

01:38:05.040 --> 01:38:08.520
ROSKO: Welcome to MIT's
139th commencement exercise

01:38:08.520 --> 01:38:11.040
the commencement of
the class of 2005

01:38:11.040 --> 01:38:13.080
I'm Tom Rosko, the
Institute Archivist and head

01:38:13.080 --> 01:38:16.320
of MIT's archives and
special collections.

01:38:16.320 --> 01:38:19.890
JONES: And I'm Marilee Jones,
the Dean of Admissions at MIT.

01:38:19.890 --> 01:38:21.540
We will provide
commentary leading up

01:38:21.540 --> 01:38:24.050
to the beginning of the
commencement ceremony,

01:38:24.050 --> 01:38:25.966
and offer insight into
what you're witnessing,

01:38:25.966 --> 01:38:29.039
as well as providing some
current and historical context.

01:38:29.039 --> 01:38:30.580
ROSKO: Periodically,
we'll break away

01:38:30.580 --> 01:38:32.137
to allow our listeners
to appreciate

01:38:32.137 --> 01:38:33.970
the ambience of the
courtyard, and to listen

01:38:33.970 --> 01:38:36.570
to the processional music
being played in Killian Court

01:38:36.570 --> 01:38:38.755
by the MIT Brass Ensemble.

01:38:38.755 --> 01:38:40.130
We welcome those
just joining us,

01:38:40.130 --> 01:38:42.088
and we welcome our audience
from across the MIT

01:38:42.088 --> 01:38:44.148
campus, the greater
Boston campus community,

01:38:44.148 --> 01:38:45.231
and from around the world.

01:38:45.231 --> 01:38:48.100
And thanks to MIT professor
emeritus Jay Keyser,

01:38:48.100 --> 01:38:50.450
and retired MIT museum
director Warren Seamans,

01:38:50.450 --> 01:38:53.370
for their work on the
previous MIT's commencement.

01:38:53.370 --> 01:38:55.220
Thank you, Jay, and
thank you, Warren.

01:38:55.220 --> 01:38:57.170
Now, here we are at MIT.

01:38:57.170 --> 01:39:01.760
MIT was founded in 1865,
and its first commencement

01:39:01.760 --> 01:39:04.010
was held in 1868.

01:39:04.010 --> 01:39:07.310
And what you're
seeing now is a view

01:39:07.310 --> 01:39:11.130
of the great Court, the
main Great Dome of MIT,

01:39:11.130 --> 01:39:14.390
which was built and
completed in 1916.

01:39:14.390 --> 01:39:16.760
MIT's original campus
was across the Charles

01:39:16.760 --> 01:39:21.320
River in Boston's Back Bay, and
it was known as Boston Tech.

01:39:21.320 --> 01:39:24.650
So its first classes
were held in 1865,

01:39:24.650 --> 01:39:27.080
and then the move
occurred in 1916.

01:39:27.080 --> 01:39:30.410
And the first commencement
exercises held in Killian--

01:39:30.410 --> 01:39:33.750
in the Court area were
in 1917 in celebration.

01:39:33.750 --> 01:39:37.070
And then in 1922,
when a tent blew down,

01:39:37.070 --> 01:39:38.930
they moved the exercises inside.

01:39:38.930 --> 01:39:41.000
But back in 1979,
they once again

01:39:41.000 --> 01:39:44.440
moved to this the greater
Court that you see here,

01:39:44.440 --> 01:39:46.360
and commencement has
been held outside

01:39:46.360 --> 01:39:49.974
ever since-- except in 1992,
when severe weather forced it

01:39:49.974 --> 01:39:50.474
inside.

01:39:58.740 --> 01:40:02.170
JONES: I've always been struck
by how the Dome in Lobby 10

01:40:02.170 --> 01:40:05.930
looks like the Pantheon in Rome.

01:40:05.930 --> 01:40:07.340
ROSKO: Indeed, it does.

01:40:07.340 --> 01:40:09.740
The architecture is actually
the Beaux-arts style,

01:40:09.740 --> 01:40:11.620
drawing from Roman
and Greek influences.

01:40:11.620 --> 01:40:14.120
And it's an interesting set of
buildings in that it's really

01:40:14.120 --> 01:40:17.870
one giant building with
interconnected smaller

01:40:17.870 --> 01:40:20.326
buildings with
corridors, so that--

01:40:20.326 --> 01:40:21.950
and it was designed
purposely that way,

01:40:21.950 --> 01:40:25.790
so that the students
and the faculty

01:40:25.790 --> 01:40:30.530
could share ideas and be
able to work together more

01:40:30.530 --> 01:40:33.650
easily than having to run
from one building to another.

01:40:33.650 --> 01:40:36.740
JONES: I think of those
corridors coming off the Lobby

01:40:36.740 --> 01:40:40.580
10 area and into the court as
being great arms reaching out,

01:40:40.580 --> 01:40:42.065
surrounding the students.

01:40:47.520 --> 01:40:50.610
ROSKO: Now what we'll be
seeing today for commencement

01:40:50.610 --> 01:40:55.160
is a procession, and the
honor guard of MIT police

01:40:55.160 --> 01:40:56.990
has already walked
with the colors

01:40:56.990 --> 01:40:59.219
and presented them at
the-- on the podium.

01:40:59.219 --> 01:41:01.010
And then there'll be
an academic procession

01:41:01.010 --> 01:41:03.051
led by the president of
the Association of Alumni

01:41:03.051 --> 01:41:04.970
and Alumnae, Linda Sharpe.

01:41:04.970 --> 01:41:08.300
And then she'll be followed
by the stage principals,

01:41:08.300 --> 01:41:11.120
including MIT's President,
Susan Hockfield, as well

01:41:11.120 --> 01:41:14.740
as past presidents; the
Corporation chair; the mayor

01:41:14.740 --> 01:41:18.880
of Cambridge, Michael
Sullivan; the individual giving

01:41:18.880 --> 01:41:20.500
the invocation--

01:41:20.500 --> 01:41:25.330
Swami Tyagananda; the provost;
the chancellor; and the deans.

01:41:25.330 --> 01:41:28.120
They'll be followed by
members of the Corporation,

01:41:28.120 --> 01:41:31.910
and then members of the faculty,
members of the Class of 1955.

01:41:31.910 --> 01:41:36.374
Each 50th-year reunion class
marches in commencement.

01:41:36.374 --> 01:41:38.540
And then following that
will be the guests of honor,

01:41:38.540 --> 01:41:39.490
the graduates.

01:41:43.290 --> 01:41:46.420
The ceremony itself will include
a greeting from the Chair

01:41:46.420 --> 01:41:49.280
of the Corporation; the
national anthem, sung by the MIT

01:41:49.280 --> 01:41:53.180
Chorallaries; the invocation
by the Hindu chaplain;

01:41:53.180 --> 01:41:57.680
guest speaker, who this year
is Irwin Jacobs, the former CEO

01:41:57.680 --> 01:42:01.910
of Qualcomm; and then a
salute to the class given

01:42:01.910 --> 01:42:05.960
by the president of the graduate
student council, Barun Singh.

01:42:05.960 --> 01:42:08.750
Then the class gift,
given by the President

01:42:08.750 --> 01:42:12.310
of the Class of 2005,
Rohit Gupta; and then the

01:42:12.310 --> 01:42:15.380
charge to the class given
by President Hockfield.

01:42:15.380 --> 01:42:18.140
And following that will be the
actual awarding of degrees.

01:42:18.140 --> 01:42:22.010
And that will occur on both
sides of the stage, the left

01:42:22.010 --> 01:42:24.560
and the right side, and
each graduate will actually

01:42:24.560 --> 01:42:26.900
get their individual degree.

01:42:26.900 --> 01:42:29.090
And then after that
is the Association

01:42:29.090 --> 01:42:32.090
of Alumni and Alumnae President
Linda Sharpe will speak.

01:42:32.090 --> 01:42:34.829
Then we'll have the school
song and then the recession.

01:42:51.950 --> 01:42:56.360
JONES: Oh, there's Tom Byrne,
the husband of Susan Hockfield,

01:42:56.360 --> 01:42:57.290
president of MIT.

01:43:00.850 --> 01:43:02.860
ROSKO: Dr. Tom
Byrne, who actually

01:43:02.860 --> 01:43:05.740
has an appointment with I
believe Brain and Cognitive

01:43:05.740 --> 01:43:10.168
Sciences faculty here,
and also at Manhasset--

01:43:10.168 --> 01:43:11.662
Massachusetts General Hospital.

01:43:28.100 --> 01:43:31.660
Once again, we see a
shot of the Great Dome,

01:43:31.660 --> 01:43:33.610
and what you see
there is the stage,

01:43:33.610 --> 01:43:36.160
and the empty seats will
be filled up momentarily

01:43:36.160 --> 01:43:38.680
by the graduates who
are currently processing

01:43:38.680 --> 01:43:40.810
from the Johnson
Athletic Center, which

01:43:40.810 --> 01:43:43.150
is on the west side of campus.

01:43:43.150 --> 01:43:45.920
This would be the
central part of campus.

01:43:45.920 --> 01:43:50.110
The procession began a few
moments ago, and at any moment

01:43:50.110 --> 01:43:55.555
now, we'll see the president
of the Association of Alumni

01:43:55.555 --> 01:44:00.550
and Alumnae coming
forth, carrying the mace.

01:44:00.550 --> 01:44:03.550
And following her will be
the academic procession.

01:44:12.550 --> 01:44:14.350
JONES: It's an
absolute beautiful day

01:44:14.350 --> 01:44:15.516
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

01:44:15.516 --> 01:44:17.200
PA ANNOUNCER: Ladies
and gentlemen,

01:44:17.200 --> 01:44:21.070
the academic procession,
led by the Chief Marshal,

01:44:21.070 --> 01:44:22.880
will now enter Killian Court.

01:44:26.056 --> 01:44:28.000
[FANFARE MUSIC PLAYING]

01:44:33.827 --> 01:44:36.160
ROSKO: And there we see the
President of the Association

01:44:36.160 --> 01:44:38.500
of Alumni and
Alumnae, Linda Sharpe,

01:44:38.500 --> 01:44:42.460
followed by on her right--
the camera's left--

01:44:42.460 --> 01:44:44.660
Susan Hockfield, and
on the other side

01:44:44.660 --> 01:44:49.060
is Dana Mead, the Chair
of the Corporation.

01:44:49.060 --> 01:44:55.120
Behind them are the former
chairs of the Corporation,

01:44:55.120 --> 01:44:58.960
as well as past
presidents at MIT.

01:44:58.960 --> 01:45:04.580
The mace she's carrying--
the gold stick--

01:45:04.580 --> 01:45:07.250
is a gift from
the class of 1907,

01:45:07.250 --> 01:45:09.530
given at the time of
their 50th class reunion.

01:45:09.530 --> 01:45:12.770
It's created by [? Leverett ?]
Kutten of the Class of 1907,

01:45:12.770 --> 01:45:15.410
and it's adorned with a--
on top of it is a beaver--

01:45:15.410 --> 01:45:17.930
which is the MIT mascot--

01:45:17.930 --> 01:45:19.590
nature's engineer.

01:45:19.590 --> 01:45:22.760
And there are also some symbols
of acorns and oak leaves,

01:45:22.760 --> 01:45:24.380
which symbolize strength.

01:45:24.380 --> 01:45:31.200
And then around are other
symbols representing various--

01:45:31.200 --> 01:45:33.480
representing symbols
that are representative

01:45:33.480 --> 01:45:35.820
of the technological
culture of MIT,

01:45:35.820 --> 01:45:39.270
rather than specific
courses of study.

01:45:39.270 --> 01:45:42.120
The procession is making
its way up to the Great Dome

01:45:42.120 --> 01:45:45.584
from the Charles River
basin along Memorial Drive

01:45:45.584 --> 01:45:47.396
at the foot of the campus.

01:45:52.830 --> 01:45:57.140
Now, President Hockfield was
just inaugurated this past--

01:45:57.140 --> 01:45:58.030
about a month ago.

01:45:58.030 --> 01:46:01.790
She became the 16th president
and took office December 6

01:46:01.790 --> 01:46:03.760
of 2004.

01:46:03.760 --> 01:46:06.320
She was MIT's first
woman president, and also

01:46:06.320 --> 01:46:09.530
the first life scientist.

01:46:09.530 --> 01:46:14.510
The robe she's wearing-- you see
the colors of the silver gray

01:46:14.510 --> 01:46:16.040
and the cardinal red--

01:46:16.040 --> 01:46:17.870
are the colors of
MIT, which were

01:46:17.870 --> 01:46:23.750
established in 1875 by a vote
of the faculty and the alumni.

01:46:23.750 --> 01:46:26.530
We see the various
deans coming through.

01:46:26.530 --> 01:46:30.989
JONES: We love these
beautiful robes

01:46:30.989 --> 01:46:32.280
from all the different schools.

01:46:34.870 --> 01:46:37.400
ROSKO: The academic
garb, the caps and gowns,

01:46:37.400 --> 01:46:39.350
date back to the 12th
and 13th centuries,

01:46:39.350 --> 01:46:44.570
and it actually stems
from the clerical garb,

01:46:44.570 --> 01:46:47.300
because many of the original
institutions of learning

01:46:47.300 --> 01:46:51.110
were based in
religious societies.

01:46:51.110 --> 01:46:52.820
And the colors
represent the various--

01:46:52.820 --> 01:46:56.390
often the robes will be
of the school attended,

01:46:56.390 --> 01:46:58.799
and then the hoods
representing the school

01:46:58.799 --> 01:46:59.840
and the doctoral degrees.

01:47:08.660 --> 01:47:13.034
JONES: There's Michael Sullivan,
the mayor of Cambridge.

01:47:13.034 --> 01:47:15.200
ROSKO: We see in the back
Rafael [? Bras ?] carrying

01:47:15.200 --> 01:47:18.816
the shepherd's staff, and
he is the current chair

01:47:18.816 --> 01:47:19.628
of the faculty.

01:47:30.829 --> 01:47:32.730
JONES: There's Howard Johnson--

01:47:32.730 --> 01:47:35.290
ROSKO: Former MIT president
in the late 1960s,

01:47:35.290 --> 01:47:37.430
and also former chair
of the Corporation.

01:47:46.250 --> 01:47:48.980
There you see all of the
diplomas to be handed out,

01:47:48.980 --> 01:47:50.992
which is something
MIT didn't always do.

01:47:50.992 --> 01:47:51.950
it was actually in 19--

01:47:51.950 --> 01:47:54.200
and most universities
don't do that today.

01:47:54.200 --> 01:47:56.240
It's actually something
special about MIT.

01:47:56.240 --> 01:47:58.670
Originally, before
1923 they used

01:47:58.670 --> 01:48:00.854
to simply put all the
diplomas in a big basket,

01:48:00.854 --> 01:48:02.270
and have the
graduates just run up

01:48:02.270 --> 01:48:06.320
and get which ones they
wanted to try and find theirs.

01:48:06.320 --> 01:48:10.560
Here, we see the
procession of MIT faculty.

01:48:10.560 --> 01:48:13.934
JONES: Better not mix
up those diplomas.

01:48:13.934 --> 01:48:15.600
ROSKO: Here we see
some of the marshals.

01:48:15.600 --> 01:48:18.840
Each of the different academic
divisions that are processing

01:48:18.840 --> 01:48:22.860
are led by a marshal and
also associate marshals.

01:48:22.860 --> 01:48:27.930
Those would be those in the
more floppier cardinal red caps.

01:48:27.930 --> 01:48:34.450
JONES: That is Kim Vandiver,
Professor and Associate Dean

01:48:34.450 --> 01:48:36.280
for Undergraduate Education.

01:48:36.280 --> 01:48:38.920
ROSKO: And head of
the Edgerton Center.

01:48:38.920 --> 01:48:40.494
JONES: An all-around
wonderful guy.

01:48:52.210 --> 01:48:54.940
ROSKO: You see the faculty
precessing in two lines.

01:48:54.940 --> 01:48:58.120
One side of the
stage will be awarded

01:48:58.120 --> 01:49:00.000
the undergraduate degrees
for the most part,

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:02.208
and then the other side will
be the graduate degrees.

01:49:24.000 --> 01:49:29.390
And there once again, we
see MIT's mace, the symbol

01:49:29.390 --> 01:49:31.342
of the academic procession.

01:49:40.600 --> 01:49:42.100
The music you hear
in the background

01:49:42.100 --> 01:49:48.630
is the MIT Brass Ensemble, being
conducted by Larry Isaacson.

01:50:07.130 --> 01:50:10.820
And here we see precessing
members of the Class of 1955.

01:50:10.820 --> 01:50:16.700
It's become a commencement
part that the 50th reunion

01:50:16.700 --> 01:50:18.850
class does process,
and you see that they

01:50:18.850 --> 01:50:21.160
have their class jackets,
which are awarded

01:50:21.160 --> 01:50:22.660
to members of the 50th class.

01:50:25.660 --> 01:50:28.523
JONES: In those days, their
favorite activities coming out

01:50:28.523 --> 01:50:34.430
of high school were music,
ham radio, and tinkering.

01:50:34.430 --> 01:50:36.430
ROSKO: And how has that
changed with the classes

01:50:36.430 --> 01:50:38.600
today, Marilee?

01:50:38.600 --> 01:50:40.840
JONES: Well, the
most recent class,

01:50:40.840 --> 01:50:44.500
their favorite activities
were volunteering,

01:50:44.500 --> 01:50:48.070
music and the arts, leadership
activities, and athletics.

01:50:48.070 --> 01:50:50.700
Very different
life for kids today

01:50:50.700 --> 01:50:58.120
than for the 17-year-olds
of the Class of 1955.

01:50:58.120 --> 01:51:00.750
ROSKO: In the class
of 2005, there

01:51:00.750 --> 01:51:05.860
are 2177 undergraduate
and graduate students.

01:51:05.860 --> 01:51:10.230
There will be awarded 1,094
Bachelor's degrees, 1078

01:51:10.230 --> 01:51:14.832
Master's degrees, 257 Doctorates
and 12 engineering degrees.

01:51:14.832 --> 01:51:17.520
And here, we see a shot of
President Hockfield talking

01:51:17.520 --> 01:51:20.010
to the provost, Bob Brown.

01:51:20.010 --> 01:51:23.685
And behind her,
left to right, we

01:51:23.685 --> 01:51:27.832
see Alice Gast, the
Associate Dean of Research;

01:51:27.832 --> 01:51:30.340
and Claude Canizares,
Associate Provost--

01:51:30.340 --> 01:51:34.660
Alice Gast is
Associate Provost--

01:51:34.660 --> 01:51:36.852
behind, I believe, members
of the Corporation.

01:51:36.852 --> 01:51:38.310
And I believe that
was Irwin Jacobs

01:51:38.310 --> 01:51:39.560
we saw standing next to Susan.

01:51:39.560 --> 01:51:41.340
He will be today's
guest speaker.

01:51:45.993 --> 01:51:48.428
JONES: Isn't it wonderful to
see all those red jackets.

01:51:52.120 --> 01:51:54.430
ROSKO: The alumni
certainly have a warmth

01:51:54.430 --> 01:51:56.400
for MIT and their
experiences here,

01:51:56.400 --> 01:51:59.158
as you can tell by the number.

01:51:59.158 --> 01:52:04.277
JONES: Many of these people are
the reasons why this nation is

01:52:04.277 --> 01:52:05.110
so prosperous today.

01:52:11.580 --> 01:52:14.380
ROSKO: Here we see a shot of
the stage, and on the podium

01:52:14.380 --> 01:52:21.757
we see a view of MIT's seal,
which if we get a shot later,

01:52:21.757 --> 01:52:23.796
we'll probably talk a
little more about that.

01:52:23.796 --> 01:52:25.296
JONES: More than a
decade ago, there

01:52:25.296 --> 01:52:27.597
was a study by
the Bank of Boston

01:52:27.597 --> 01:52:32.830
of all of the employment
provided by graduates of MIT

01:52:32.830 --> 01:52:34.410
over time.

01:52:34.410 --> 01:52:38.430
It determined that
our alumni together

01:52:38.430 --> 01:52:42.682
created the 24th largest
GNP in the world.

01:52:46.640 --> 01:52:47.940
Mind and hands indeed.

01:52:50.470 --> 01:52:53.790
ROSKO: And here we see the
marshal for the faculty, Paul

01:52:53.790 --> 01:52:57.150
Lagace, standing next to
Linda Sharpe of the Alumni

01:52:57.150 --> 01:52:58.200
Association.

01:52:58.200 --> 01:53:01.314
JONES: Paul's professor in
Aeronautics and Astronautics.

01:53:01.314 --> 01:53:03.000
ROSKO: And a big
Boston Red Sox fan.

01:53:03.000 --> 01:53:04.680
JONES: And a great
baseball player.

01:53:04.680 --> 01:53:08.760
PA ANNOUNCER: --the guests
of honor, the Class of 2005.

01:53:08.760 --> 01:53:10.230
[APPLAUSE]

01:53:11.210 --> 01:53:14.988
JONES: And now, the
real guests of honor

01:53:14.988 --> 01:53:18.518
are graduates themselves.

01:53:18.518 --> 01:53:20.406
[BRASS MUSIC PLAYING]

01:53:24.190 --> 01:53:28.054
ROSKO: They are being led
by the Dean of Students,

01:53:28.054 --> 01:53:29.220
Larry Benedict, on the left.

01:53:29.220 --> 01:53:30.678
JONES: And my boss.

01:53:30.678 --> 01:53:32.670
ROSKO: At the
right, Bob Redwine--

01:53:32.670 --> 01:53:36.654
JONES: The dean of
undergraduate education.

01:53:36.654 --> 01:53:39.144
They look pretty spiffy
with those hats on.

01:53:55.387 --> 01:53:57.220
ROSKO: You note some
of the students dressed

01:53:57.220 --> 01:53:58.510
in different regalia.

01:53:58.510 --> 01:54:01.740
Some of the recipients
of doctoral degrees

01:54:01.740 --> 01:54:05.170
are wearing the MIT colors,
with the robes-- with the three

01:54:05.170 --> 01:54:08.560
bars on the sleeves denoting
the doctoral degree.

01:54:13.430 --> 01:54:16.040
JONES: They're moving
nice and slowly so

01:54:16.040 --> 01:54:17.878
that people don't
get out of line.

01:54:17.878 --> 01:54:20.520
It almost looks like one of
those slow-motion movies.

01:54:20.520 --> 01:54:22.210
It's really quite
a beautiful sight.

01:54:22.210 --> 01:54:23.918
ROSKO: And it is quite
a scene here today

01:54:23.918 --> 01:54:26.746
with not only the lovely
weather, but also--

01:54:26.746 --> 01:54:32.114
it's packed to the brim with
students and well-wishers,

01:54:32.114 --> 01:54:36.270
the family of the graduates.

01:54:36.270 --> 01:54:38.700
JONES: The energy at MIT
is very different today.

01:54:38.700 --> 01:54:40.380
Commencement is
always such a joy.

01:54:40.380 --> 01:54:45.330
The energy is relief,
relaxation, fun, it's real joy

01:54:45.330 --> 01:54:46.342
here.

01:54:46.342 --> 01:54:48.300
Very different from the
rest of the year, where

01:54:48.300 --> 01:54:50.695
people are buckling down
and getting their work done.

01:54:50.695 --> 01:54:52.570
It's very similar to
the first day of school,

01:54:52.570 --> 01:54:54.753
but much more relaxed.

01:54:54.753 --> 01:54:55.719
Very happy day.

01:55:00.550 --> 01:55:02.470
ROSKO: And as well
as the marshals--

01:55:02.470 --> 01:55:04.240
we see Larry Benedict
and Bob Redwine--

01:55:04.240 --> 01:55:06.860
behind them are the
associate marshals--

01:55:06.860 --> 01:55:12.610
Professor Deb Fitzgerald and
Professor Leslie Norford,

01:55:12.610 --> 01:55:19.210
as well as Rohit Gupta, the
President of the Class of 2005.

01:55:19.210 --> 01:55:23.010
And Mr. John [? Blasko, ?]
the Treasurer of the class,

01:55:23.010 --> 01:55:25.920
and Barun Singh, the President
of the Graduate Student

01:55:25.920 --> 01:55:27.534
Council.

01:55:27.534 --> 01:55:29.200
And Hector Hernandez,
the Vice President

01:55:29.200 --> 01:55:33.196
of the Graduate Student Council,
followed by Julie Norman,

01:55:33.196 --> 01:55:34.660
the head assistant marshal.

01:55:34.660 --> 01:55:37.100
JONES: You can see they're
coming in four lines.

01:55:37.100 --> 01:55:40.028
Two will go to the left, and
two will go to the right.

01:55:45.890 --> 01:55:47.440
I have to say, as
Dean of Admissions,

01:55:47.440 --> 01:55:49.600
my heart really
bursts with pride

01:55:49.600 --> 01:55:54.730
when I see these young people,
because in the Admissions

01:55:54.730 --> 01:55:58.160
Office, we have handpicked every
single one of those students.

01:55:58.160 --> 01:56:00.535
It is very difficult
to be admitted here.

01:56:05.805 --> 01:56:07.180
ROSKO: Marilee,
perhaps you could

01:56:07.180 --> 01:56:09.388
talk a little bit about the
admission process itself,

01:56:09.388 --> 01:56:14.984
then-- what the students
did to get into MIT.

01:56:14.984 --> 01:56:17.150
JONES: Well, they had to
be extraordinarily prepared

01:56:17.150 --> 01:56:19.820
in terms of their academic
work, but they also

01:56:19.820 --> 01:56:21.610
had to be a good match for MIT.

01:56:21.610 --> 01:56:25.760
They had to be
very self-motivated

01:56:25.760 --> 01:56:30.370
and have some kind of
emotional resilience,

01:56:30.370 --> 01:56:32.090
not being afraid of failure.

01:56:32.090 --> 01:56:34.570
They have-- we look for people
who are willing to risk,

01:56:34.570 --> 01:56:38.200
because at MIT students
risk themselves every day.

01:56:38.200 --> 01:56:40.710
There's a great look.

01:56:40.710 --> 01:56:42.240
They risk their egos every day.

01:56:42.240 --> 01:56:44.960
They need to be brave.

01:56:44.960 --> 01:56:48.290
And there are so many thousands
of students who apply here,

01:56:48.290 --> 01:56:55.140
nearly 11,000 this year for this
soon-to-enter freshman class.

01:56:55.140 --> 01:56:57.970
For this particular
group, the Class of 2005,

01:56:57.970 --> 01:57:00.740
we had about 10,500
of them applying.

01:57:03.650 --> 01:57:08.050
and we admitted just
18% of those students.

01:57:08.050 --> 01:57:11.040
They're really extraordinary.

01:57:11.040 --> 01:57:13.110
For those of you listening
who are MIT graduates,

01:57:13.110 --> 01:57:17.435
you may wonder if you had been
admitted today, many of us

01:57:17.435 --> 01:57:18.810
look at these
students and think,

01:57:18.810 --> 01:57:20.975
"We would never get
admitted to college today,

01:57:20.975 --> 01:57:22.350
and we feel very
badly about it."

01:57:22.350 --> 01:57:23.725
And I just want
to let you know--

01:57:23.725 --> 01:57:25.506
you should not feel
bad, because we admit

01:57:25.506 --> 01:57:27.340
the best of each generation.

01:57:27.340 --> 01:57:31.270
And you alumni out there
were the best of yours.

01:57:31.270 --> 01:57:32.395
ROSKO: Very nicely said.

01:57:32.395 --> 01:57:34.270
JONES: It's comparing
the apples and oranges,

01:57:34.270 --> 01:57:37.750
but now I'm going to really
shock you by telling you that

01:57:37.750 --> 01:57:41.110
the average-- the mean SAT
scores for this particular

01:57:41.110 --> 01:57:46.395
class as you're watching
them enter was 711 Verbal--

01:57:46.395 --> 01:57:49.305
that's 711 out of 800, folks--

01:57:49.305 --> 01:57:52.215
and 756 Math.

01:57:52.215 --> 01:57:56.600
So many of you alumni
from previous years

01:57:56.600 --> 01:57:59.422
may not have scores like that.

01:57:59.422 --> 01:58:00.922
You might have had
high Math scores,

01:58:00.922 --> 01:58:04.241
but perhaps the verbal wasn't
where you wanted it to be.

01:58:04.241 --> 01:58:08.090
These young ones
are extraordinary.

01:58:08.090 --> 01:58:13.630
41% of them were valedictorians
of their high school classes.

01:58:13.630 --> 01:58:17.140
Well over 90% of them are in the
top 5% of their high schools.

01:58:17.140 --> 01:58:21.010
They were very well
prepared coming in.

01:58:21.010 --> 01:58:24.488
And every single one of those
students out there has a story.

01:58:24.488 --> 01:58:26.276
They come from
somewhere, they come

01:58:26.276 --> 01:58:29.370
from a family who loves them.

01:58:29.370 --> 01:58:33.580
Every one of them have had
to face tough times at MIT.

01:58:33.580 --> 01:58:35.880
They've been
challenged to the max.

01:58:35.880 --> 01:58:43.460
As you know, this is a
very rigorous curriculum,

01:58:43.460 --> 01:58:44.954
and they all made it.

01:58:48.440 --> 01:58:51.515
They come from 56
different countries

01:58:51.515 --> 01:58:52.970
in the world, this group.

01:58:57.820 --> 01:59:01.980
48 different languages
spoken in their homes.

01:59:01.980 --> 01:59:07.260
That and a 711 mean
SAT Verbal score.

01:59:07.260 --> 01:59:08.640
Am I proud of my kids?

01:59:08.640 --> 01:59:11.300
Yes, I am.

01:59:11.300 --> 01:59:13.806
ROSKO: And well you
should be, Marilee.

01:59:13.806 --> 01:59:15.180
JONES: Anybody
who knows me knows

01:59:15.180 --> 01:59:20.076
I take full responsibility
for everyone in the class.

01:59:20.076 --> 01:59:21.450
When they do
something wonderful,

01:59:21.450 --> 01:59:22.908
I write them little
notes of pride.

01:59:22.908 --> 01:59:25.344
When they do something
that's not so wonderful,

01:59:25.344 --> 01:59:26.820
I blame everybody else.

01:59:26.820 --> 01:59:28.296
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:35.184 --> 01:59:38.460
ROSKO: Now, this year's
Class of 2005 also certainly

01:59:38.460 --> 01:59:40.650
was different from
the Class of 1905.

01:59:40.650 --> 01:59:45.340
The much earlier alumni and
graduates was a class of 244,

01:59:45.340 --> 01:59:49.600
and as far as foreign countries
represented, they were three--

01:59:49.600 --> 01:59:52.950
Brazil, England, and Ireland.

01:59:52.950 --> 01:59:56.380
And there were only 18 master's
degrees awarded that year.

01:59:56.380 --> 01:59:58.970
And 55% of the class--

01:59:58.970 --> 02:00:02.460
or of the student body
came from Massachusetts.

02:00:02.460 --> 02:00:05.840
The number of clubs that
they were involved in was 11.

02:00:05.840 --> 02:00:09.600
The number of sports were 10,
including a tug of war team,

02:00:09.600 --> 02:00:11.243
and the number of
musical clubs three.

02:00:19.266 --> 02:00:21.640
Here, we see another side of
the stage and the principals

02:00:21.640 --> 02:00:23.470
sitting there, members of--

02:00:23.470 --> 02:00:29.365
the deans, the members of the
Corporation, the marshals,

02:00:29.365 --> 02:00:31.490
various members of the
faculty seated at the front,

02:00:31.490 --> 02:00:33.041
and department heads and chairs.

02:00:37.720 --> 02:00:41.354
Also I said members of the
Corporation towards the back.

02:00:41.354 --> 02:00:43.346
Once again, you note
the different regalia

02:00:43.346 --> 02:00:44.840
that's being worn.

02:00:44.840 --> 02:00:46.832
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:01:05.780 --> 02:01:08.300
JONES: The students have
been told to just follow

02:01:08.300 --> 02:01:10.190
the student in front of them--

02:01:10.190 --> 02:01:13.540
no matter what follow the
student in front of them.

02:01:13.540 --> 02:01:16.270
So now we get to see how
well they take direction.

02:01:16.270 --> 02:01:20.240
ROSKO: And it really is quite
a process to award each student

02:01:20.240 --> 02:01:21.380
their degrees.

02:01:21.380 --> 02:01:23.400
I say it will be
done in two parts.

02:01:23.400 --> 02:01:25.895
It will be simultaneously
the degrees being given.

02:01:25.895 --> 02:01:28.850
At the left side of the
stage, President Hockfield

02:01:28.850 --> 02:01:31.950
will present the Bachelor
of Science degrees,

02:01:31.950 --> 02:01:35.330
the Bachelor of Science slash
Master of Science degrees,

02:01:35.330 --> 02:01:37.610
Bachelor of Science slash
Master of Engineering--

02:01:37.610 --> 02:01:40.680
that would be on the left
side of the stage looking out.

02:01:40.680 --> 02:01:42.680
Also President Hockfield--
I know you see a shot

02:01:42.680 --> 02:01:45.054
of President Hockfield talking
to the guest speaker Irwin

02:01:45.054 --> 02:01:46.650
Jacobs--

02:01:46.650 --> 02:01:48.690
President Hockfield
will also give out

02:01:48.690 --> 02:01:50.520
advanced degrees in
the School of Science,

02:01:50.520 --> 02:01:53.010
the Woods Hole
Oceanographic Institution,

02:01:53.010 --> 02:01:55.950
and the Whitaker College of
Health Sciences and Technology.

02:01:55.950 --> 02:01:58.226
The right side,
provost Bob Brown

02:01:58.226 --> 02:02:00.600
will award advanced degrees
in the School of Architecture

02:02:00.600 --> 02:02:02.850
and Planning, the
School of Engineering,

02:02:02.850 --> 02:02:05.400
the School of Humanities,
Arts and Social Sciences,

02:02:05.400 --> 02:02:08.420
and the Sloan School
of Management.

02:02:08.420 --> 02:02:10.670
JONES: Glad to see that
Susan Hockfield is staying

02:02:10.670 --> 02:02:12.050
hydrated up there on the stage.

02:02:12.050 --> 02:02:14.210
It's very warm in the
Great Court today,

02:02:14.210 --> 02:02:18.425
and everyone has a big bottle
of water under their chair.

02:02:18.425 --> 02:02:21.820
Push fluids.

02:02:21.820 --> 02:02:24.752
ROSKO: I'm sure no one minds
the heat after the rain and cold

02:02:24.752 --> 02:02:26.252
that we've had for
the month of May.

02:02:26.252 --> 02:02:27.704
JONES: What a May we had.

02:02:34.490 --> 02:02:36.050
This is Susan's
first commencement.

02:02:36.050 --> 02:02:39.420
She looks like she's
having a wonderful time.

02:02:39.420 --> 02:02:42.300
There's Dana Mead, Chairman
of the Corporation,

02:02:42.300 --> 02:02:45.235
and Howard Johnson.

02:02:45.235 --> 02:02:46.860
ROSKO: Howard Johnson,
former president

02:02:46.860 --> 02:02:49.620
of MIT in the late
1960s early 1970s,

02:02:49.620 --> 02:02:51.500
and then also a former chair--

02:02:51.500 --> 02:02:52.700
JONES: There's Paul Gray.

02:02:52.700 --> 02:02:55.669
ROSKO: Paul Gray, the
president from 1980 to 1990.

02:02:55.669 --> 02:02:57.960
JONES: I want to say just a
quick word about Paul Gray.

02:02:57.960 --> 02:03:00.240
Paul Gray's a true
citizen of MIT.

02:03:00.240 --> 02:03:01.920
He reminds me of
George Washington

02:03:01.920 --> 02:03:04.590
in that after serving as
President and then Chairman

02:03:04.590 --> 02:03:06.780
of the Corporation, he's
gone back onto the faculty.

02:03:06.780 --> 02:03:10.410
And he's now joining
our admissions office

02:03:10.410 --> 02:03:12.900
faculty oversight committee,
the Committee on Undergraduate

02:03:12.900 --> 02:03:14.730
Admissions and
Financial Aid, which

02:03:14.730 --> 02:03:16.460
is staffed by faculty members.

02:03:16.460 --> 02:03:20.704
He has not served on CUAFA
for many years, and he's back.

02:03:20.704 --> 02:03:22.120
ROSKO: And there
in the middle, we

02:03:22.120 --> 02:03:25.540
saw a shot of the Mayor of
Cambridge, Michael Sullivan.

02:03:25.540 --> 02:03:28.318
And he was talking to Alex
d'Arbeloff, former chair--

02:03:28.318 --> 02:03:33.796
the preceding chair of
the MIT Corporation.

02:03:33.796 --> 02:03:35.510
So some facts about MIT itself.

02:03:35.510 --> 02:03:37.820
There are over 10,000 students--

02:03:37.820 --> 02:03:39.980
approximately 4,000
undergraduate students,

02:03:39.980 --> 02:03:42.810
and approximately 6,000
graduate students.

02:03:42.810 --> 02:03:46.080
There are 900-- over
almost 1,000 faculty,

02:03:46.080 --> 02:03:50.300
including 178 women, and
there are nearly 10,000 staff.

02:03:50.300 --> 02:03:51.584
MIT consists of five schools.

02:03:51.584 --> 02:03:53.000
There's the School
of Engineering,

02:03:53.000 --> 02:03:56.120
the School of Science, the
School of Humanities and Social

02:03:56.120 --> 02:03:58.400
Sciences, the School of
Architecture and Urban

02:03:58.400 --> 02:04:01.130
Planning, and the Sloan
School of Management,

02:04:01.130 --> 02:04:03.590
as well as the Whitaker
College of Health Sciences

02:04:03.590 --> 02:04:04.722
and Technology.

02:04:11.802 --> 02:04:13.740
You see some members
of the Corporation.

02:04:13.740 --> 02:04:17.710
The MIT Corporation is
the governing body of MIT.

02:04:17.710 --> 02:04:20.070
The president reports
to them, and we

02:04:20.070 --> 02:04:21.780
see in the bottom-left
Kathryn Willmore,

02:04:21.780 --> 02:04:26.086
who is the secretary
of the Corporation.

02:04:26.086 --> 02:04:29.064
So the MIT Corporation would be
similar to a board of trustees,

02:04:29.064 --> 02:04:29.980
or a board of regents.

02:04:29.980 --> 02:04:31.588
[FANFARE MUSIC PLAYING]

02:04:32.752 --> 02:04:34.460
Many of them are
members of the faculty--

02:04:34.460 --> 02:04:37.668
I mean, members of the alumni.

02:04:37.668 --> 02:04:42.990
In fact, just 100 years
ago, the Alumni Association

02:04:42.990 --> 02:04:46.160
was strengthened through--

02:04:46.160 --> 02:04:48.320
there was a proposed merger--

02:04:48.320 --> 02:04:50.990
one of several in
the history of MIT--

02:04:50.990 --> 02:04:52.190
with Harvard University.

02:04:52.190 --> 02:04:56.960
And the alumni did
not want to see that.

02:04:56.960 --> 02:04:58.610
However, the
Corporation and its vote

02:04:58.610 --> 02:05:00.230
voted that the
merger should occur.

02:05:00.230 --> 02:05:03.800
This was to have MIT become
sort of the School of Science

02:05:03.800 --> 02:05:04.345
of Harvard--

02:05:04.345 --> 02:05:05.720
I mean, the School
of Engineering

02:05:05.720 --> 02:05:07.160
of Harvard University.

02:05:07.160 --> 02:05:11.870
But it was a vote, and
many of the Corporation

02:05:11.870 --> 02:05:13.770
were actually Harvard graduates.

02:05:13.770 --> 02:05:16.320
But the Alumni
Association fought back

02:05:16.320 --> 02:05:17.670
and petitioned against it.

02:05:17.670 --> 02:05:21.270
It was actually a legal
case that MIT was not

02:05:21.270 --> 02:05:25.950
allowed to join the merger
because their land, which

02:05:25.950 --> 02:05:29.550
was at that time in Boston's
Back Bay, could not be sold.

02:05:29.550 --> 02:05:32.880
But anyway, that really was a
strengthening of the alumni,

02:05:32.880 --> 02:05:35.987
and in fact, more Corporation
members since that time

02:05:35.987 --> 02:05:39.730
have been MIT alumni.

02:05:39.730 --> 02:05:43.190
JONES: Well, was
that a good decision.

02:05:43.190 --> 02:05:44.732
Because MIT is the
premier university

02:05:44.732 --> 02:05:46.398
of science and
technology on the planet.

02:05:46.398 --> 02:05:49.100
ROSKO: It is, and it was
founded to be a stand-alone

02:05:49.100 --> 02:05:52.400
institution, and there were
other thoughts that it might--

02:05:52.400 --> 02:05:54.590
it was really a new
sort of way of thinking

02:05:54.590 --> 02:05:58.070
when MIT was founded, as to
what a polytechnic institute

02:05:58.070 --> 02:05:59.270
should be, and would be.

02:05:59.270 --> 02:06:01.520
And William Barton
Rogers, MIT's founder--

02:06:01.520 --> 02:06:02.420
JONES: My hero.

02:06:02.420 --> 02:06:04.370
ROSKO: --established
a scope and a plan

02:06:04.370 --> 02:06:08.600
as to what that should
include, and it was not just

02:06:08.600 --> 02:06:09.810
the applied sciences.

02:06:09.810 --> 02:06:11.960
It was that there should
be a firm founding as well

02:06:11.960 --> 02:06:14.630
in the humanities and the arts,
which continues to this day,

02:06:14.630 --> 02:06:18.320
and has been reinforced many
times over the importance

02:06:18.320 --> 02:06:22.200
of a well-rounded education,
but also an education that

02:06:22.200 --> 02:06:24.630
does solve problems.

02:06:24.630 --> 02:06:27.990
JONES: He added to the
charter after the Civil War

02:06:27.990 --> 02:06:33.690
that MIT should teach students
to apply science and technology

02:06:33.690 --> 02:06:37.224
for the benefit of humankind,
because he lost family members,

02:06:37.224 --> 02:06:39.380
his wife lost a brother
in the Civil War.

02:06:39.380 --> 02:06:42.714
He lived in Virginia, although
he was actually a Northerner,

02:06:42.714 --> 02:06:46.345
and lost many friends in
that terrible tragedy.

02:06:46.345 --> 02:06:48.880
And he could not believe
how so many good people

02:06:48.880 --> 02:06:52.840
could do such stupid things.

02:06:52.840 --> 02:06:54.820
ROSKO: And it continues
as MIT's mission

02:06:54.820 --> 02:06:57.790
to have an impact on the
world and for the betterment

02:06:57.790 --> 02:07:01.260
of mankind.

02:07:01.260 --> 02:07:02.700
JONES: That's what they all do.

02:07:02.700 --> 02:07:04.380
It's a pleasure to
look out over these--

02:07:04.380 --> 02:07:07.500
so many students are about to
be unleashed into the world.

02:07:07.500 --> 02:07:09.900
When you think of all the
previous people from MIT gone

02:07:09.900 --> 02:07:13.230
into the world, and all the good
that they do because they fix

02:07:13.230 --> 02:07:16.220
the world for the people who
can't fix it for themselves,

02:07:16.220 --> 02:07:17.985
for the disenfranchised.

02:07:17.985 --> 02:07:19.909
And I just look at
the sea of faces

02:07:19.909 --> 02:07:21.450
and think of all
the potential there.

02:07:21.450 --> 02:07:23.366
Just think of all the
magic that they're going

02:07:23.366 --> 02:07:24.915
to bring into this planet.

02:07:29.390 --> 02:07:32.050
I want to say one thing
about the Class of 2005--

02:07:32.050 --> 02:07:36.510
they entered MIT
in September 2001.

02:07:36.510 --> 02:07:40.570
So they had about two
weeks of the old world,

02:07:40.570 --> 02:07:43.330
and then September
11th happened,

02:07:43.330 --> 02:07:47.260
and their lives changed, as
all of our lives changed.

02:07:47.260 --> 02:07:49.750
MIT had a lot of pressure,
as other universities did

02:07:49.750 --> 02:07:54.100
after that, to close its doors
to international students,

02:07:54.100 --> 02:07:56.290
to close its doors
to researchers,

02:07:56.290 --> 02:08:03.762
and fought back very hard
to remain a free university.

02:08:03.762 --> 02:08:06.220
The currency of universities,
the lifeblood of universities

02:08:06.220 --> 02:08:08.600
is ideas.

02:08:08.600 --> 02:08:11.584
And our previous president,
Chuck Vest, fought very hard.

02:08:14.937 --> 02:08:17.270
ROSKO: And the sharing with
those ideas with the world

02:08:17.270 --> 02:08:19.068
is something that MIT
has always promoted.

02:08:23.840 --> 02:08:27.060
There we see in the
front Paul Lagace

02:08:27.060 --> 02:08:28.560
again, one of the marshals.

02:08:28.560 --> 02:08:31.786
And talking to Susan Hockfield
is today's guest speaker

02:08:31.786 --> 02:08:33.600
Irwin Jacobs.

02:08:33.600 --> 02:08:34.680
And--

02:08:34.680 --> 02:08:36.540
JONES: Bob Brown, the provost.

02:08:36.540 --> 02:08:39.420
Next to him, Phil
Clay, the chancellor.

02:08:39.420 --> 02:08:40.770
Next to him--

02:08:40.770 --> 02:08:42.530
ROSKO: Chair of the
faculty, Rafael Bras

02:08:42.530 --> 02:08:43.690
in the wonderful regalia.

02:08:43.690 --> 02:08:46.915
JONES: It's a great, puffy
neck thing he has on there.

02:08:46.915 --> 02:08:49.290
ROSKO: In the back, we see
the Dean of Graduate Students,

02:08:49.290 --> 02:08:51.780
Ike Colbert; the Dean of
Architecture and Urban

02:08:51.780 --> 02:08:55.500
Planning, Adéle Santos;
and the Dean of the School

02:08:55.500 --> 02:08:59.577
of Humanities, Arts, and
Social Sciences, Phil Khoury.

02:08:59.577 --> 02:09:01.410
JONES: Dick Schmalensee
of the Sloan School,

02:09:01.410 --> 02:09:02.580
the Dean of Sloan School.

02:09:02.580 --> 02:09:04.770
ROSKO: And then Tom Magnanti
just exiting the screen

02:09:04.770 --> 02:09:10.390
as the Dean of the
School of Engineering.

02:09:10.390 --> 02:09:12.010
Today's guest
speaker, Irwin Jacobs,

02:09:12.010 --> 02:09:15.120
is an MIT alumnus,
with a master's in--

02:09:15.120 --> 02:09:16.790
Doctorate in Science.

02:09:16.790 --> 02:09:19.300
He's a former faculty member
in electrical engineering,

02:09:19.300 --> 02:09:22.745
and co-founder and chairman
and CEO of Qualcomm.

02:09:22.745 --> 02:09:25.120
He's known as an innovative
entrepreneur and engineer who

02:09:25.120 --> 02:09:26.926
places high value on research.

02:09:26.926 --> 02:09:28.894
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:09:54.970 --> 02:09:59.037
JONES: Ah, yes
there's Tom Magnanti--

02:09:59.037 --> 02:10:00.620
ROSKO: --leaning
forward, sitting next

02:10:00.620 --> 02:10:05.605
to Dick Schmalensee,
Dean of the Sloan School.

02:10:05.605 --> 02:10:08.035
JONES: Tom's wearing
a nice outfit.

02:10:08.035 --> 02:10:09.785
ROSKO: That's Princeton
colors, I believe.

02:10:09.785 --> 02:10:10.326
JONES: I see.

02:10:10.326 --> 02:10:11.900
I see Phil Khoury there.

02:10:11.900 --> 02:10:13.430
ROSKO: He has Harvard colors.

02:10:13.430 --> 02:10:16.080
JONES: Dean of the Humanities,
Arts, and Social Sciences

02:10:16.080 --> 02:10:18.330
school.

02:10:18.330 --> 02:10:21.450
ROSKO: Adéle Santos, the Dean
of the School of Architecture

02:10:21.450 --> 02:10:23.430
and Urban Planning.

02:10:23.430 --> 02:10:26.810
And once again to the bottom,
Phil Clay, the chancellor.

02:10:26.810 --> 02:10:29.180
To the left up above,
we see Ike Colbert,

02:10:29.180 --> 02:10:30.690
the Dean of Graduate Students.

02:10:33.430 --> 02:10:34.418
JONES: Wonderful hat.

02:10:37.880 --> 02:10:39.780
ROSKO: And Provost Bob Brown--

02:10:39.780 --> 02:10:42.660
Bob Brown, being the
highest academic officer

02:10:42.660 --> 02:10:45.234
of the Institute-- aside from
the president-- and the Deans

02:10:45.234 --> 02:10:47.142
would report to him.

02:10:47.142 --> 02:10:50.481
JONES: Chancellor Phil
Clay, and Rafael Bras.

02:10:53.819 --> 02:10:56.360
ROSKO: The chancellor dealing
with student-related activities

02:10:56.360 --> 02:10:59.180
and services, for the
most part, and Rafael Bras

02:10:59.180 --> 02:11:02.780
is chair of the faculty,
representing them.

02:11:11.857 --> 02:11:13.440
We see a lot of
colors coming through,

02:11:13.440 --> 02:11:16.830
with MIT's colors
there-- the gray and--

02:11:16.830 --> 02:11:21.040
the silver gray and
the cardinal red.

02:11:21.040 --> 02:11:25.967
And now at MIT, only doctoral
degree recipients wear hoods,

02:11:25.967 --> 02:11:28.050
and indeed there was a
hooding ceremony yesterday,

02:11:28.050 --> 02:11:30.690
where they all
received as you see

02:11:30.690 --> 02:11:37.320
there the hood behind that
graduate walking past.

02:11:37.320 --> 02:11:40.020
JONES: They're so happy.

02:11:40.020 --> 02:11:43.300
They're so glad to be
out of graduate school.

02:11:43.300 --> 02:11:45.140
ROSKO: The doctoral
degrees, the PhD,

02:11:45.140 --> 02:11:47.220
has a blue velvet trim,
and the doctor of science

02:11:47.220 --> 02:11:49.140
a yellow velvet
trim, and the lining

02:11:49.140 --> 02:11:50.760
would be the school colors.

02:11:50.760 --> 02:11:54.330
Here, we see Bob Redwine, Dean
of Undergraduates to the left.

02:11:54.330 --> 02:11:57.300
Linda Sharpe, the President
of the Association

02:11:57.300 --> 02:12:00.540
of Alumni and Alumnae.

02:12:00.540 --> 02:12:02.288
JONES: There's Dean
Larry Benedict, who's

02:12:02.288 --> 02:12:04.024
the Dean for Student Life.

02:12:07.496 --> 02:12:08.984
There's Gayle Gallagher.

02:12:16.450 --> 02:12:21.715
You see Barun Singh, President
of the Grad Student Council,

02:12:21.715 --> 02:12:26.502
and Hector Hernandez, who's
the Vice President of the Grad

02:12:26.502 --> 02:12:27.002
Student.

02:12:29.920 --> 02:12:32.740
ROSKO: We also got a shot
there of Gayle Gallagher, who

02:12:32.740 --> 02:12:35.840
is the ranger of this event, and
coordinating all this activity

02:12:35.840 --> 02:12:37.200
that must go on.

02:12:37.200 --> 02:12:38.200
Every year.

02:12:38.200 --> 02:12:39.241
JONES: Commencement star.

02:12:39.241 --> 02:12:42.200
She rules.

02:12:42.200 --> 02:12:43.667
She does a fabulous job.

02:12:55.403 --> 02:13:00.730
There's Barun and Hector of
the Graduate Student Council.

02:13:05.630 --> 02:13:06.610
[LAUGHS]

02:13:14.470 --> 02:13:16.310
ROSKO: And you note
some military uniforms,

02:13:16.310 --> 02:13:20.710
and MIT has an ROTC program,
representing the three

02:13:20.710 --> 02:13:23.210
branches-- the Marines,
the Air Force, and--

02:13:23.210 --> 02:13:26.490
I mean, the Army,
Marines, and Navy.

02:13:26.490 --> 02:13:29.820
And actually, ROTC
and military training

02:13:29.820 --> 02:13:33.540
has been a part of MIT's history
since the very beginning.

02:13:33.540 --> 02:13:37.260
MIT was partially founded
as a land-grant university

02:13:37.260 --> 02:13:40.020
by the Morrill Land-Grant
Act, and that was compulsory

02:13:40.020 --> 02:13:41.610
as part of that training.

02:13:41.610 --> 02:13:44.109
Of course, it was also
just during the Civil War

02:13:44.109 --> 02:13:45.528
that MIT came about.

02:13:50.270 --> 02:13:52.670
JONES: So here's Bob Redwine
in the middle, and then

02:13:52.670 --> 02:13:55.940
we see Rohit Gupta, who is the
President of the Class of 2005,

02:13:55.940 --> 02:13:58.040
and then you see
John [? Velazco, ?]

02:13:58.040 --> 02:14:00.920
who's the Treasurer
of the Class of 2005.

02:14:00.920 --> 02:14:07.242
We see Professor Deb Fitzgerald,
who is the associate marshal.

02:14:07.242 --> 02:14:08.736
Look at all those
beautiful faces.

02:14:28.160 --> 02:14:31.709
The class that entered in 1970--

02:14:31.709 --> 02:14:33.750
the average number of
extracurriculars coming out

02:14:33.750 --> 02:14:35.917
of high school was four.

02:14:35.917 --> 02:14:38.000
And with this particular
class, the average number

02:14:38.000 --> 02:14:40.760
of extracurriculars that
the students did was 12.

02:14:40.760 --> 02:14:44.270
So it shows you how the
culture has changed.

02:14:44.270 --> 02:14:48.230
Again, in 1970, the percentage
of students entering

02:14:48.230 --> 02:14:51.260
with a well-defined interest
in music, the number one

02:14:51.260 --> 02:14:57.080
activity at MIT, was 24%
entering the class of 1970.

02:14:57.080 --> 02:14:59.750
This particular
graduating class was 60%.

02:14:59.750 --> 02:15:03.380
So 60% of these kids that you
see out here in the Great Court

02:15:03.380 --> 02:15:08.750
graduating entered MIT with
well-defined and distinguished

02:15:08.750 --> 02:15:10.850
backgrounds in music,
and that tells you

02:15:10.850 --> 02:15:12.890
how the culture has changed.

02:15:12.890 --> 02:15:14.630
We see athletics
as well in 1970--

02:15:14.630 --> 02:15:17.060
19% of the students came
in as varsity athletes,

02:15:17.060 --> 02:15:20.160
versus 58% of the class
you see in front of you,

02:15:20.160 --> 02:15:22.610
the class of 2005.

02:15:22.610 --> 02:15:26.720
That's the effect of
Title IX from the 1970s

02:15:26.720 --> 02:15:30.800
that brought gender equity
in athletics, and also

02:15:30.800 --> 02:15:32.820
in many other areas
of life as well.

02:15:32.820 --> 02:15:34.390
It really opened up athletics.

02:15:37.930 --> 02:15:40.930
Activities in theater in
the class entering 1970--

02:15:40.930 --> 02:15:43.750
4% came in with
theater background.

02:15:43.750 --> 02:15:45.665
This particular
class-- over 28%.

02:15:45.665 --> 02:15:51.850
Some very talented performers
in the class of 2005.

02:15:51.850 --> 02:15:54.800
ROSKO: And the culture
of MIT is often not

02:15:54.800 --> 02:15:56.531
what you think it would be.

02:15:56.531 --> 02:16:02.530
It's amazing the value placed
on the arts, and just the talent

02:16:02.530 --> 02:16:03.670
that there is.

02:16:03.670 --> 02:16:06.370
As you walk down
the halls, what you

02:16:06.370 --> 02:16:10.161
might stereotype as an MIT
student is nothing like they

02:16:10.161 --> 02:16:10.660
are.

02:16:10.660 --> 02:16:12.201
And there's-- walking
down the halls,

02:16:12.201 --> 02:16:17.200
there's music playing
from various classrooms.

02:16:17.200 --> 02:16:19.240
The various talents
that the students

02:16:19.240 --> 02:16:22.070
have not just as
tinkerers, or being

02:16:22.070 --> 02:16:24.840
able to fix things
and solve problems,

02:16:24.840 --> 02:16:28.550
but are very broad in the
humanities and the arts

02:16:28.550 --> 02:16:31.620
and in the social sciences,
and within various activities

02:16:31.620 --> 02:16:34.799
and the strengths in athletics.

02:16:34.799 --> 02:16:38.400
And it's, I think, quite
surprising for those

02:16:38.400 --> 02:16:42.059
who come here as students,
even, and then also

02:16:42.059 --> 02:16:46.830
as faculty and as staff to
absorb the culture as is.

02:16:46.830 --> 02:16:49.700
JONES: The class that
entered in 1970, we

02:16:49.700 --> 02:16:53.549
admitted 40% of
that applicant pool,

02:16:53.549 --> 02:16:55.290
and this particular
class I mentioned

02:16:55.290 --> 02:16:57.290
that you see in front of
you, the Class of 2005.

02:16:57.290 --> 02:17:00.870
We admitted 18% of them.

02:17:00.870 --> 02:17:03.780
So it's a very different
kind of a caliber of student

02:17:03.780 --> 02:17:05.000
in the applicant pool.

02:17:05.000 --> 02:17:09.280
The students entering in
'70 had 627-751 SAT scores.

02:17:09.280 --> 02:17:12.000
627-751.

02:17:12.000 --> 02:17:17.390
And I'll remind you that this
class has 711-756 SAT scores.

02:17:17.390 --> 02:17:20.629
ROSKO: And we just saw the
principals, the marshals

02:17:20.629 --> 02:17:22.940
from the graduate division,
going to the stage,

02:17:22.940 --> 02:17:25.280
and I believe the
ceremony is about to begin

02:17:25.280 --> 02:17:27.680
with Dana Mead, the
Chairman of the Corporation.

02:17:27.680 --> 02:17:29.930
We thank you for joining
us, and we now turn it over.

02:17:29.930 --> 02:17:31.639
Once again, I'm Tom
Rosko, and this was--

02:17:31.639 --> 02:17:32.420
JONES: Marilee Jones.

02:17:32.420 --> 02:17:32.995
Enjoy.

02:17:32.995 --> 02:17:34.078
ROSKO: Enjoy the ceremony.

02:17:34.078 --> 02:17:34.935
Thank you.

02:17:38.820 --> 02:17:41.540
MEAD: Good morning.

02:17:41.540 --> 02:17:45.910
The Corporation and the faculty
of the Massachusetts Institute

02:17:45.910 --> 02:17:49.809
of Technology are now
declared convened,

02:17:49.809 --> 02:17:53.559
together with this
assembly, on the occasion

02:17:53.559 --> 02:17:56.980
of the commencement
exercises at this institution

02:17:56.980 --> 02:18:00.309
for the conferring
of its degrees.

02:18:00.309 --> 02:18:02.590
The stage assembly
and the audience

02:18:02.590 --> 02:18:08.320
will please rise for the
invocation by Swami Tyagananda,

02:18:08.320 --> 02:18:10.780
and remain standing
to join the MIT

02:18:10.780 --> 02:18:14.872
Chorallaries in the singing
of one verse of the Star

02:18:14.872 --> 02:18:15.844
Spangled Banner.

02:18:28.980 --> 02:18:30.780
TYAGANANDA: Swami
Vivekananda once

02:18:30.780 --> 02:18:36.030
said, "Education is the
manifestation of the perfection

02:18:36.030 --> 02:18:38.459
already within us."

02:18:38.459 --> 02:18:43.440
Our challenge is to transform
knowledge into wisdom,

02:18:43.440 --> 02:18:47.790
and this is best achieved in
an environment that promotes

02:18:47.790 --> 02:18:51.430
both unity and harmony.

02:18:51.430 --> 02:18:55.320
I will recite now, first
in Sanskrit and then

02:18:55.320 --> 02:18:59.129
in English translation,
an ancient prayer that

02:18:59.129 --> 02:19:00.709
invokes this harmony.

02:19:03.433 --> 02:19:05.341
[RECITING IN SANSKRIT]

02:19:42.750 --> 02:19:46.080
May we come together
for a common purpose.

02:19:46.080 --> 02:19:49.680
May our minds be of one accord.

02:19:49.680 --> 02:19:53.820
Let us join our thoughts
for integrated wisdom.

02:19:53.820 --> 02:19:57.270
Common be our prayer,
common our goal.

02:19:57.270 --> 02:20:01.170
Common be our purpose,
common our desires.

02:20:01.170 --> 02:20:03.450
Unified be our hearts.

02:20:03.450 --> 02:20:05.940
United be our intentions.

02:20:05.940 --> 02:20:09.630
Perfect be the unity
amongst us all.

02:20:09.630 --> 02:20:12.270
Let me leave you now
with this prayer.

02:20:12.270 --> 02:20:15.990
May the one and the
same divine being--

02:20:15.990 --> 02:20:18.990
who is the father in
heaven of the Christians,

02:20:18.990 --> 02:20:23.730
holy one of the Jewish
faith, Allah of the Muslims,

02:20:23.730 --> 02:20:28.620
Buddha of the Buddhists,
Tao of the Chinese faith,

02:20:28.620 --> 02:20:32.940
Ahura Mazda of the
Zoroastrians, the great spirit

02:20:32.940 --> 02:20:37.260
of the Native Americans,
and Brahman of the Hindus--

02:20:37.260 --> 02:20:40.470
lead us from ignorance
to knowledge,

02:20:40.470 --> 02:20:45.450
from darkness to light,
from death to immortality.

02:20:45.450 --> 02:20:50.010
May we be granted clear
understanding and the courage

02:20:50.010 --> 02:20:56.250
to pursue our goals of social
justice, nonviolence, harmony,

02:20:56.250 --> 02:21:00.730
and peace, with
single-minded attention.

02:21:00.730 --> 02:21:02.730
[RECITING IN SANSKRIT]

02:21:08.194 --> 02:21:12.360
Peace, peace, peace be unto all.

02:21:20.690 --> 02:21:22.650
[MUSIC - "STAR SPANGLED BANNER"]

02:21:33.675 --> 02:21:43.975
(SINGING) Oh say can you see,
by the dawn's early light, what

02:21:43.975 --> 02:21:53.220
so proudly we hailed at the
twilight's last gleaming, whose

02:21:53.220 --> 02:22:01.915
broad stripes and bright stars
through the perilous fight,

02:22:01.915 --> 02:22:10.974
o'er the ramparts we watched,
were so gallantly streaming?

02:22:10.974 --> 02:22:19.776
And the rockets' red glare,
the bombs bursting in air,

02:22:19.776 --> 02:22:28.716
gave proof through the night
that our flag was still there.

02:22:28.716 --> 02:22:40.879
Oh say, does that star-spangled
banner yet wave o'er

02:22:40.879 --> 02:22:52.356
the land of the free and
the home of the brave?

02:22:56.348 --> 02:22:58.344
[APPLAUSE]

02:23:03.212 --> 02:23:04.170
MEAD: Please be seated.

02:23:19.260 --> 02:23:23.720
I'm pleased to welcome the
honorable Michael A. Sullivan,

02:23:23.720 --> 02:23:28.070
Mayor of the City of
Cambridge to our stage.

02:23:28.070 --> 02:23:33.080
And I'm also pleased to welcome
the members of our 50th reunion

02:23:33.080 --> 02:23:48.110
class, the distinguished
Class of 1955.

02:23:48.110 --> 02:23:50.440
Now, it is my pleasure to
introduce our commencement

02:23:50.440 --> 02:23:51.610
speaker--

02:23:51.610 --> 02:23:54.690
Dr. Irwin M. Jacobs.

02:23:54.690 --> 02:23:59.400
His distinguished biography
is included in your programs.

02:23:59.400 --> 02:24:03.100
Dr. Jacobs is one of
MIT's most illustrious

02:24:03.100 --> 02:24:06.740
and successful graduates,
whose leadership

02:24:06.740 --> 02:24:10.790
of the telecommunications
industry, his advocacy

02:24:10.790 --> 02:24:15.140
of science and math education,
and his philanthropy

02:24:15.140 --> 02:24:19.520
in education and the arts,
are inspiring and in the best

02:24:19.520 --> 02:24:23.030
tradition of MIT.

02:24:23.030 --> 02:24:25.350
Ladies and gentlemen,
distinguished guests,

02:24:25.350 --> 02:24:29.406
graduates, and graduates
to be, Dr. Irwin Jacobs.

02:24:38.074 --> 02:24:39.240
JACOBS: Thank you very much.

02:24:39.240 --> 02:24:41.060
It's a great honor
to be here with you

02:24:41.060 --> 02:24:43.486
on this very special occasion.

02:24:43.486 --> 02:24:45.110
And I would like to
give special thanks

02:24:45.110 --> 02:24:47.960
to President Susan
Hockfield for asking

02:24:47.960 --> 02:24:51.740
me to provide this address.

02:24:51.740 --> 02:24:55.100
I'd also like to congratulate
the Class of 2005

02:24:55.100 --> 02:24:58.250
on this very special
day, and provide

02:24:58.250 --> 02:25:03.020
welcome to the family and
friends of the graduates,

02:25:03.020 --> 02:25:08.080
to the faculty here, to
the entire MIT family.

02:25:08.080 --> 02:25:12.454
It really indeed is
a very special time.

02:25:12.454 --> 02:25:13.870
It's a very great
day to graduate.

02:25:13.870 --> 02:25:17.410
I remember back to my receiving
my graduate degrees here--

02:25:17.410 --> 02:25:20.110
a master's and a doctorate--

02:25:20.110 --> 02:25:25.510
back in '57 and '59,
quite a few years ago,

02:25:25.510 --> 02:25:31.570
fitting very well in with
the 50-year reunion class.

02:25:31.570 --> 02:25:35.230
I must say that, at that
time, I could not possibly

02:25:35.230 --> 02:25:37.840
have imagined all of
the things that we're

02:25:37.840 --> 02:25:43.360
going to happen in my life
over the succeeding years.

02:25:43.360 --> 02:25:46.510
And that indeed is something
I'd like to pick as the theme

02:25:46.510 --> 02:25:47.620
today.

02:25:47.620 --> 02:25:50.950
Namely, that we are all
going to-- and in particular

02:25:50.950 --> 02:25:52.210
you're going to--

02:25:52.210 --> 02:25:55.720
be going through a
great deal of change,

02:25:55.720 --> 02:25:59.560
providing both opportunities
and occasionally some problems,

02:25:59.560 --> 02:26:04.090
but that, in fact, an MIT
education is about the best

02:26:04.090 --> 02:26:06.940
possible way to
prepare yourselves

02:26:06.940 --> 02:26:10.300
for this very exciting future.

02:26:10.300 --> 02:26:12.520
I suspect again that
a few years from now,

02:26:12.520 --> 02:26:15.850
you'll have the opportunity to
think back over the many things

02:26:15.850 --> 02:26:18.230
that you just could
not have anticipated,

02:26:18.230 --> 02:26:19.870
and so it's important
to be prepared.

02:26:19.870 --> 02:26:22.690
Be prepared for those changes.

02:26:22.690 --> 02:26:25.450
My life itself has been
a number of changes.

02:26:25.450 --> 02:26:27.820
I'll use those for an example.

02:26:27.820 --> 02:26:30.190
I actually was born in New
Bedford, Massachusetts,

02:26:30.190 --> 02:26:32.530
not too far from here.

02:26:32.530 --> 02:26:34.960
When I graduated high school,
I had always been interested

02:26:34.960 --> 02:26:37.760
in math and chemistry, physics.

02:26:37.760 --> 02:26:41.290
My high school counselor
advised me that there was--

02:26:41.290 --> 02:26:44.950
and this was 1950-- that there
was no future in science,

02:26:44.950 --> 02:26:45.850
nor in engineering.

02:26:45.850 --> 02:26:47.810
[LAUGHTER]

02:26:48.790 --> 02:26:53.190
And since I didn't really have
a measure to evaluate that,

02:26:53.190 --> 02:26:54.190
I then took his advice.

02:26:54.190 --> 02:26:55.720
My family had a
small restaurant,

02:26:55.720 --> 02:26:58.990
and so I entered the School
of Hotel Administration

02:26:58.990 --> 02:27:02.000
at Cornell University.

02:27:02.000 --> 02:27:03.760
Well, I had an
engineer as a roommate.

02:27:03.760 --> 02:27:06.100
After a year and a half of
hearing him talk about--

02:27:06.100 --> 02:27:07.450
you couldn't possibly
get those grades

02:27:07.450 --> 02:27:09.200
if you were in
engineering-- and knowing I

02:27:09.200 --> 02:27:11.740
really preferred
engineering, again,

02:27:11.740 --> 02:27:13.810
I made a very significant
change in life,

02:27:13.810 --> 02:27:19.820
and decided to transfer over
to electrical engineering.

02:27:19.820 --> 02:27:24.200
And that was a very
exciting period.

02:27:24.200 --> 02:27:26.300
I was a co-op student.

02:27:26.300 --> 02:27:27.720
That turned out
to be very useful.

02:27:27.720 --> 02:27:29.446
One of the engineers
I worked with then

02:27:29.446 --> 02:27:31.070
advised me to go on
to graduate school,

02:27:31.070 --> 02:27:33.800
and that's how I
ended up at MIT.

02:27:33.800 --> 02:27:38.270
But thinking back, in my
last term at Cornell--

02:27:38.270 --> 02:27:40.410
and this is how fast
things have changed--

02:27:40.410 --> 02:27:44.720
I took a course in the theory
and practice, building,

02:27:44.720 --> 02:27:46.340
of vacuum tubes.

02:27:46.340 --> 02:27:48.380
Built a 6SN7, a 6J6--

02:27:48.380 --> 02:27:52.270
you probably never even heard
of these terms any longer.

02:27:52.270 --> 02:27:57.220
I was reminded last week, when
I gave a talk at the Computer

02:27:57.220 --> 02:28:01.250
Museum in Mountain
View, and that, in fact,

02:28:01.250 --> 02:28:04.060
has a lot of equipment that
originally came from a Computer

02:28:04.060 --> 02:28:06.400
Museum here in
Boston, but now is out

02:28:06.400 --> 02:28:08.450
in Mountain View, California.

02:28:08.450 --> 02:28:12.100
And so, as I toured around
looking at all the equipment,

02:28:12.100 --> 02:28:16.960
seeing analog and digital
differential analyzers up

02:28:16.960 --> 02:28:19.557
to cell phones, which of
course are the latest and most

02:28:19.557 --> 02:28:20.390
powerful computers--

02:28:20.390 --> 02:28:23.520
I'll come back to that--

02:28:23.520 --> 02:28:29.000
that, it was amazing to me
how fast things have changed.

02:28:29.000 --> 02:28:32.710
And again, that's the
key issue with change.

02:28:32.710 --> 02:28:37.160
It was amazing to see all
these familiar items that

02:28:37.160 --> 02:28:41.090
had been in my life and then
passed out of it so quickly.

02:28:41.090 --> 02:28:44.150
Well, I did decide to
apply and, luckily,

02:28:44.150 --> 02:28:47.140
was accepted here at
MIT to graduate school.

02:28:47.140 --> 02:28:50.624
I originally came
thinking that EM theory,

02:28:50.624 --> 02:28:52.790
ElectroMagnetic theory would
be an interesting area.

02:28:52.790 --> 02:28:56.210
But MIT, at that time,
Professor Claude Shannon

02:28:56.210 --> 02:28:58.670
had just come, the father
of information theory,

02:28:58.670 --> 02:29:00.710
there was a lot of
interest in the theory,

02:29:00.710 --> 02:29:03.470
the mathematics, probability
theory, et cetera.

02:29:03.470 --> 02:29:06.845
And so I decided that
that would be my future,

02:29:06.845 --> 02:29:08.345
and very pleased
with that decision.

02:29:11.410 --> 02:29:13.830
One of the early
courses I took was

02:29:13.830 --> 02:29:15.930
from Professor Norbert Wiener.

02:29:15.930 --> 02:29:17.580
Now, I don't think
probably anyone here

02:29:17.580 --> 02:29:19.980
might have had the opportunity.

02:29:19.980 --> 02:29:22.074
But it was very interesting.

02:29:22.074 --> 02:29:24.240
There are many tales, I'm
sure, still running around

02:29:24.240 --> 02:29:26.280
MIT about Professor Wiener.

02:29:29.550 --> 02:29:32.550
One that I most remember,
in taking this class--

02:29:32.550 --> 02:29:36.570
probably like several of the
classes you might have taken--

02:29:36.570 --> 02:29:38.761
the lectures were-- well,
I shouldn't say this.

02:29:38.761 --> 02:29:40.260
Probably, it's not
the case anymore.

02:29:40.260 --> 02:29:44.510
But the lectures were
incomprehensible.

02:29:44.510 --> 02:29:46.714
And so each night, a
group of graduate students

02:29:46.714 --> 02:29:48.380
would get together
and try to figure out

02:29:48.380 --> 02:29:50.004
what it was we had
heard during the day

02:29:50.004 --> 02:29:53.441
and kind of put it together in
a way that we could understand.

02:29:53.441 --> 02:29:55.440
About halfway through the
term, Professor Wiener

02:29:55.440 --> 02:29:59.800
heard that we were doing this,
came to the room and said,

02:29:59.800 --> 02:30:01.590
can I look at the material?

02:30:01.590 --> 02:30:05.670
Became interested-- said we
should make a book from this.

02:30:05.670 --> 02:30:08.670
And so we, then, continued
to put the material together

02:30:08.670 --> 02:30:09.900
as a book.

02:30:09.900 --> 02:30:12.780
He would come in,
every day after class,

02:30:12.780 --> 02:30:18.190
and his only question was
how many pages are we up to?

02:30:18.190 --> 02:30:20.170
So he always had a
different slant on things.

02:30:20.170 --> 02:30:21.670
That book did come out.

02:30:21.670 --> 02:30:24.140
It's Nonlinear Problems
in Random Theory.

02:30:24.140 --> 02:30:30.270
It was the first book that
I was ever involved with.

02:30:30.270 --> 02:30:33.240
Went on the faculty
here, again, it's

02:30:33.240 --> 02:30:34.740
a wonderful way--
if some of you are

02:30:34.740 --> 02:30:38.160
considering careers in teaching,
I'd greatly recommend it.

02:30:38.160 --> 02:30:40.950
It's the best way
to learn material.

02:30:40.950 --> 02:30:45.360
And while here, decided with
Professor Jack Wozencraft

02:30:45.360 --> 02:30:48.540
to put together a textbook for
a senior-level communications

02:30:48.540 --> 02:30:53.790
course on applying what was
then brand new digital theory

02:30:53.790 --> 02:30:56.610
and information theory and try
to give it a little bit more

02:30:56.610 --> 02:30:58.145
of a practical face.

02:30:58.145 --> 02:30:59.520
And there were
many, at the time,

02:30:59.520 --> 02:31:03.610
that said that there really
is no practical use for this.

02:31:03.610 --> 02:31:06.182
You should just treat it
as applied mathematics.

02:31:06.182 --> 02:31:07.890
In fact, of course,
that's turned out not

02:31:07.890 --> 02:31:11.690
to be the case at all.

02:31:11.690 --> 02:31:16.670
I did take a leave of absence to
make my one visit to California

02:31:16.670 --> 02:31:21.650
about the time we were
finishing the book in '64, '65.

02:31:21.650 --> 02:31:24.860
We decided that might be a
good place to retire some time,

02:31:24.860 --> 02:31:29.450
came back to Boston, had a call
from a professor from Cornell

02:31:29.450 --> 02:31:31.040
saying he's going
out to start a brand

02:31:31.040 --> 02:31:36.230
new department of electrical
university in San Diego.

02:31:36.230 --> 02:31:38.300
Would we join them?

02:31:38.300 --> 02:31:40.220
First reaction, of
course, was no--

02:31:40.220 --> 02:31:41.850
family, friends, career here.

02:31:41.850 --> 02:31:43.850
But after a couple
of days, we decided

02:31:43.850 --> 02:31:46.072
that California and a
brand new university,

02:31:46.072 --> 02:31:47.780
an opportunity for a
different experience

02:31:47.780 --> 02:31:53.300
might be quite exciting in
our lives, and we accepted.

02:31:53.300 --> 02:31:56.949
Again, change-- the change from
here to a brand new school.

02:31:56.949 --> 02:31:57.740
It was interesting.

02:31:57.740 --> 02:31:59.823
A brand new school-- it
was very small, of course,

02:31:59.823 --> 02:32:01.130
very few faculty.

02:32:01.130 --> 02:32:04.100
One of the classes I started
had to do with introduction

02:32:04.100 --> 02:32:06.109
to computer science.

02:32:06.109 --> 02:32:07.650
There were some
engineering students.

02:32:07.650 --> 02:32:09.710
But there were
students and faculty

02:32:09.710 --> 02:32:13.310
from music and from
the arts departments,

02:32:13.310 --> 02:32:17.450
and in interacting with them,
developed even a greater

02:32:17.450 --> 02:32:20.360
love and appreciation for
the arts and for music

02:32:20.360 --> 02:32:23.350
that we've been able to
follow, then, ever since.

02:32:23.350 --> 02:32:28.010
It was very interesting being
in a brand new university.

02:32:28.010 --> 02:32:32.990
But that also led to another
major change in my life.

02:32:32.990 --> 02:32:37.154
Because of the MIT
background, a lot of industry

02:32:37.154 --> 02:32:38.570
in Southern
California, there were

02:32:38.570 --> 02:32:40.490
many requests for consulting.

02:32:40.490 --> 02:32:42.380
Typically, if you're
in the faculty,

02:32:42.380 --> 02:32:44.430
you might consult a day a week.

02:32:44.430 --> 02:32:46.970
And so I mentioned that to a
couple of friends from UCLA

02:32:46.970 --> 02:32:51.380
and the faculty of UCLA as we
were flying back on a trip.

02:32:51.380 --> 02:32:55.520
And they said let's start a
company and share consulting.

02:32:55.520 --> 02:32:57.020
And I said fine,
as long as I don't

02:32:57.020 --> 02:32:59.030
have to get involved
with managing it.

02:32:59.030 --> 02:33:02.240
And so we started our first
company called Linkabit.

02:33:02.240 --> 02:33:06.450
And very quickly
it began to grow.

02:33:06.450 --> 02:33:09.260
And so I did, then,
decide to take a year off

02:33:09.260 --> 02:33:13.077
and check out business, try to
get things properly organized.

02:33:13.077 --> 02:33:14.660
Didn't know, really,
a thing about it.

02:33:14.660 --> 02:33:17.120
Luckily, in the Hotel
School I had had a course

02:33:17.120 --> 02:33:19.730
in accounting, a course
in business law--

02:33:19.730 --> 02:33:22.664
so a little bit of background
turned out to be very useful.

02:33:22.664 --> 02:33:24.830
But really had to learn the
business side of things.

02:33:24.830 --> 02:33:28.160
Engineering is, by far,
the best preparation

02:33:28.160 --> 02:33:29.730
for just about any field.

02:33:29.730 --> 02:33:33.780
So that has indeed
worked out very well.

02:33:33.780 --> 02:33:37.010
Well, Linkabit, this first
company did grow very nicely.

02:33:37.010 --> 02:33:41.850
We got involved in a number
of interesting programs--

02:33:41.850 --> 02:33:44.750
one scrambling TV
signals from satellite

02:33:44.750 --> 02:33:47.660
to home that's turned into a
very major business, another

02:33:47.660 --> 02:33:51.320
what are called very small
aperture earth terminals for--

02:33:51.320 --> 02:33:53.510
if you put a credit card
in at a gas station, often,

02:33:53.510 --> 02:33:57.380
it will go over one of
these satellite terminals.

02:33:57.380 --> 02:33:59.990
The first what's called
Time-Division, TDMA type

02:33:59.990 --> 02:34:04.760
of cellular phone-- we got into
the cellular phone business

02:34:04.760 --> 02:34:06.080
early on.

02:34:06.080 --> 02:34:09.151
And actually a processor--

02:34:09.151 --> 02:34:10.650
we didn't know the
name at the time,

02:34:10.650 --> 02:34:12.775
and I don't think it was
really out-- but a reduced

02:34:12.775 --> 02:34:15.050
instruction set
processor that we built

02:34:15.050 --> 02:34:17.870
into a terminal for use
in government programs

02:34:17.870 --> 02:34:19.580
and, in particular,
for a program

02:34:19.580 --> 02:34:22.640
here at Lincoln Laboratory to
communicate with what was then

02:34:22.640 --> 02:34:24.170
called the LES-8 -9 satellite.

02:34:24.170 --> 02:34:27.740
So again, things tied
back together very nicely,

02:34:27.740 --> 02:34:30.530
but very exciting to be
able to come up with ideas,

02:34:30.530 --> 02:34:35.030
be able to apply theory
to things that were rather

02:34:35.030 --> 02:34:36.410
practical, rather useful.

02:34:39.000 --> 02:34:43.260
Well, we made the mistake, in a
sense, of selling that company.

02:34:43.260 --> 02:34:47.340
And in 1985, I retired.

02:34:47.340 --> 02:34:49.530
Retirement was a terrible thing.

02:34:49.530 --> 02:34:54.030
So I lasted about three months
and then started Qualcomm.

02:34:54.030 --> 02:34:58.390
And I very much assured my wife
that if things went very well,

02:34:58.390 --> 02:35:01.350
we might have 100
employees at some time.

02:35:01.350 --> 02:35:05.960
Put that in context, we're
now over 8,000 employees.

02:35:05.960 --> 02:35:07.680
And by the way, in
my welcoming, I also

02:35:07.680 --> 02:35:10.549
meant to welcome any
Qualcomm shareholders that

02:35:10.549 --> 02:35:11.732
might be here today.

02:35:15.520 --> 02:35:17.800
Well, we didn't
have any products.

02:35:17.800 --> 02:35:20.050
Luckily, we didn't have to
go out for venture capital,

02:35:20.050 --> 02:35:22.570
so we didn't have to
have a business plan.

02:35:22.570 --> 02:35:23.590
But we knew digital.

02:35:23.590 --> 02:35:26.290
We knew wireless would
be very exciting.

02:35:26.290 --> 02:35:29.680
And it turned out that
it was on a drive down

02:35:29.680 --> 02:35:32.590
from a consulting contract
meeting in Los Angeles.

02:35:32.590 --> 02:35:35.840
A drive down the San Diego,
about halfway, luckily--

02:35:35.840 --> 02:35:37.820
it's 110 miles or so--

02:35:37.820 --> 02:35:41.320
about halfway down, realized
that something called

02:35:41.320 --> 02:35:43.060
Code-Division
Multiple Access, CDMA

02:35:43.060 --> 02:35:47.115
would be very useful for
mobile communications.

02:35:47.115 --> 02:35:48.490
Well, the company
was very small.

02:35:48.490 --> 02:35:50.239
We had to wait a few
years before we could

02:35:50.239 --> 02:35:52.990
go ahead and develop that idea.

02:35:52.990 --> 02:35:55.730
But the time came when we
sold our first product,

02:35:55.730 --> 02:35:58.270
had a little bit of
a cash flow, and were

02:35:58.270 --> 02:36:00.850
able to, then, go
back and pay attention

02:36:00.850 --> 02:36:04.960
to CDMA, actually,
at the end of 1988

02:36:04.960 --> 02:36:06.624
began to take a look at it.

02:36:06.624 --> 02:36:09.040
Well, if any of you decide to
go into you own businesses--

02:36:09.040 --> 02:36:12.370
and some of you I'm sure
we'll be doing that--

02:36:12.370 --> 02:36:15.580
you run across a
time when you have

02:36:15.580 --> 02:36:18.430
to make a
bet-the-company decision.

02:36:18.430 --> 02:36:21.160
And so CDMA was one of those.

02:36:21.160 --> 02:36:24.490
Should you put a lot of money
into R&amp;D in a technology that

02:36:24.490 --> 02:36:26.650
may or may not be accepted?

02:36:26.650 --> 02:36:30.010
The world is going off
in a different direction.

02:36:30.010 --> 02:36:33.190
And luckily, at
that time, I had not

02:36:33.190 --> 02:36:36.220
heard one of the
projections that

02:36:36.220 --> 02:36:42.250
had been made to AT&amp;T by a
consultant a few years earlier

02:36:42.250 --> 02:36:45.610
that, if all went well,
there might be 1 million cell

02:36:45.610 --> 02:36:50.437
phones in use by the year 2000.

02:36:50.437 --> 02:36:52.020
Actually, they missed
by a little bit.

02:36:52.020 --> 02:36:54.490
It was 600 million.

02:36:54.490 --> 02:36:57.070
And that, of course,
gave a great opportunity

02:36:57.070 --> 02:36:59.710
for moving ahead with CDMA.

02:36:59.710 --> 02:37:02.120
We did develop the
technology, demonstrated--

02:37:02.120 --> 02:37:05.096
because otherwise, everything
sounds too complicated.

02:37:05.096 --> 02:37:06.470
You have to have
a demonstration.

02:37:06.470 --> 02:37:10.110
So that was, again, one of the
bet-your-company-type issues.

02:37:10.110 --> 02:37:11.860
And then the question
comes up, if you now

02:37:11.860 --> 02:37:14.540
have a good product, how do
you build a business model?

02:37:14.540 --> 02:37:16.056
What do you do about that?

02:37:16.056 --> 02:37:17.680
And so again, this
is a type of concern

02:37:17.680 --> 02:37:20.290
that you may be
having going forward.

02:37:20.290 --> 02:37:25.390
We decided to go into a mode,
which was both licensing

02:37:25.390 --> 02:37:30.880
and of selling, initially,
phones and infrastructure

02:37:30.880 --> 02:37:33.152
to get things started,
but, ultimately, the chips.

02:37:33.152 --> 02:37:34.360
And that works out very well.

02:37:34.360 --> 02:37:36.250
As you know, chips keep
getting more and more powerful.

02:37:36.250 --> 02:37:38.290
You can put more and
more capability in them.

02:37:38.290 --> 02:37:40.270
If you come up with
innovative ideas,

02:37:40.270 --> 02:37:42.310
you can build those
into the chips.

02:37:42.310 --> 02:37:46.079
And so that's exactly the
path that we followed.

02:37:46.079 --> 02:37:47.620
It's interesting
that, today, there's

02:37:47.620 --> 02:37:50.800
probably about 1 and 1/2
billion users of cell

02:37:50.800 --> 02:37:53.080
phones around the world.

02:37:53.080 --> 02:37:57.830
In 2005, there were over 600
million sold in the one year,

02:37:57.830 --> 02:38:00.640
or will be by the
end of the year.

02:38:00.640 --> 02:38:04.360
Comparing that to about 150
million desktop and laptop

02:38:04.360 --> 02:38:09.370
computers, it's quite clear that
the future is not in plastics,

02:38:09.370 --> 02:38:14.050
but really, now,
in mobile devices.

02:38:14.050 --> 02:38:16.870
And the interesting aspect
is that the capabilities

02:38:16.870 --> 02:38:18.760
keep going up.

02:38:18.760 --> 02:38:20.560
One of the things that
is now being provide

02:38:20.560 --> 02:38:22.500
is what's called
third generation.

02:38:22.500 --> 02:38:24.580
I won't, again, go into details.

02:38:24.580 --> 02:38:28.104
But if some of you have been
using, not just the wireless

02:38:28.104 --> 02:38:29.270
that's available on campus--

02:38:29.270 --> 02:38:32.740
that's called 802.11,
but a wide area coverage

02:38:32.740 --> 02:38:35.200
provided right now
by Verizon here,

02:38:35.200 --> 02:38:37.390
one can get a very
high data rate

02:38:37.390 --> 02:38:39.790
anywhere that you can
receive a cell phone call.

02:38:39.790 --> 02:38:43.000
And so that is a key step.

02:38:43.000 --> 02:38:46.400
But the interesting
part is the devices.

02:38:46.400 --> 02:38:52.750
And because of Moore's law, the
number of transistors on a chip

02:38:52.750 --> 02:38:55.150
doubling roughly every
two years or so--

02:38:55.150 --> 02:38:56.800
power going down,
costs going down,

02:38:56.800 --> 02:38:59.440
all the right things
kind of happening--

02:38:59.440 --> 02:39:01.120
there's been a major
transformation.

02:39:01.120 --> 02:39:04.570
When we first built up
our first cell phone,

02:39:04.570 --> 02:39:09.400
it took three chips to implement
the communications only.

02:39:09.400 --> 02:39:11.360
Now it takes about
20% of one chip.

02:39:11.360 --> 02:39:13.330
What do you do
with the other 80%?

02:39:13.330 --> 02:39:15.250
You can put a lot
of computing power.

02:39:15.250 --> 02:39:17.870
In fact, now we're
going to two processes--

02:39:17.870 --> 02:39:21.250
one of which is moving toward
a gigahertz-type processing

02:39:21.250 --> 02:39:22.150
speed.

02:39:22.150 --> 02:39:27.250
Two processes, a couple of
signal processing units, a 3D

02:39:27.250 --> 02:39:30.010
graphics capability,
GPS receiving--

02:39:30.010 --> 02:39:33.160
you can put a lot on
that chip, make it

02:39:33.160 --> 02:39:35.710
available at a low
cost, high reliability,

02:39:35.710 --> 02:39:39.720
and therefore very
useful to people.

02:39:39.720 --> 02:39:43.009
Therefore, since it's a computer
now, not really a phone--

02:39:43.009 --> 02:39:45.300
you may not realize it when
you're carrying it around--

02:39:45.300 --> 02:39:48.870
very powerful computer that
opens up many possibilities.

02:39:48.870 --> 02:39:52.670
And so we developed another
approach we call ROO.

02:39:52.670 --> 02:39:55.500
In fact, there's a conference
now with about 2,400 people

02:39:55.500 --> 02:39:58.770
at it occurring in San Diego--

02:39:58.770 --> 02:40:00.360
where developers
anywhere in the world

02:40:00.360 --> 02:40:03.270
can develop an application to
be downloaded to the phone.

02:40:03.270 --> 02:40:06.330
We arranged to provide a
digital signature, tested

02:40:06.330 --> 02:40:09.630
digital signature so it
won't corrupt the phone.

02:40:09.630 --> 02:40:14.490
And therefore, they can
develop these, bring them

02:40:14.490 --> 02:40:18.337
via some internet meeting
grounds we've established

02:40:18.337 --> 02:40:20.170
with the operators,
bring their applications

02:40:20.170 --> 02:40:22.128
to the attention of
operators around the world,

02:40:22.128 --> 02:40:23.650
and build the business.

02:40:23.650 --> 02:40:25.920
And I think, at
this meeting that's

02:40:25.920 --> 02:40:28.770
ongoing, it was mentioned that
there was about $350 million

02:40:28.770 --> 02:40:33.540
that had been funneled from
operators to Qualcomm, then

02:40:33.540 --> 02:40:38.760
Qualcomm back to the
developers around the world,

02:40:38.760 --> 02:40:41.730
in this last six months, in
the order of $150 million.

02:40:41.730 --> 02:40:43.620
The more exciting
aspect is other things

02:40:43.620 --> 02:40:45.745
I think that were going to
be able to do with that.

02:40:45.745 --> 02:40:48.420
We've all heard of issues with
the digital divide, access

02:40:48.420 --> 02:40:51.990
to communications, to the
internet being more limited

02:40:51.990 --> 02:40:54.000
in certain regions.

02:40:54.000 --> 02:40:55.950
I think that the phone
as a low-cost device

02:40:55.950 --> 02:40:57.533
with a huge amount
of computing power,

02:40:57.533 --> 02:41:00.390
connection to the internet,
ability to download software,

02:41:00.390 --> 02:41:03.600
process-- it had a large
amount of memory by the way.

02:41:03.600 --> 02:41:06.450
With the appropriate amount
of thinking and planning

02:41:06.450 --> 02:41:10.020
can be used to supplement
teaching in many remote areas

02:41:10.020 --> 02:41:12.410
as well as, of course, developed
areas around the world.

02:41:12.410 --> 02:41:16.650
So I think that there's a great
possibility there to move ahead

02:41:16.650 --> 02:41:17.980
with these devices.

02:41:17.980 --> 02:41:20.700
People are still just realizing
what the power in the devices

02:41:20.700 --> 02:41:21.730
might be.

02:41:21.730 --> 02:41:23.550
And again, hopefully,
some of you

02:41:23.550 --> 02:41:25.200
out there will find
this challenging.

02:41:25.200 --> 02:41:27.796
Of course there's
also medical devices

02:41:27.796 --> 02:41:29.670
that are now being
attached to the cell phone

02:41:29.670 --> 02:41:34.770
measuring blood capabilities
and moving toward eGovernment

02:41:34.770 --> 02:41:38.430
providing support, voting,
information, et cetera by use

02:41:38.430 --> 02:41:39.210
of the cell phone.

02:41:39.210 --> 02:41:41.190
So again, a device we
think of as a phone,

02:41:41.190 --> 02:41:45.291
very powerful computer,
opening lots of opportunities.

02:41:45.291 --> 02:41:47.430
Well, I mentioned eGovernment--

02:41:47.430 --> 02:41:50.460
one of the things I would like
to recommend to all of you,

02:41:50.460 --> 02:41:52.670
at least some of
you is to think--

02:41:52.670 --> 02:41:54.420
well, all of you to
think about politics.

02:41:54.420 --> 02:41:58.710
To some of you, perhaps, to
consider a career in politics.

02:41:58.710 --> 02:42:01.170
Again, I think an MIT
education prepares you

02:42:01.170 --> 02:42:03.870
for just about anything.

02:42:03.870 --> 02:42:05.670
And it was interesting.

02:42:05.670 --> 02:42:09.720
It was over a couple of years
ago-- so the previous president

02:42:09.720 --> 02:42:11.550
of China, we had a meeting.

02:42:11.550 --> 02:42:14.600
They always have
this very formal U.

02:42:14.600 --> 02:42:17.100
So myself and the president was
sitting at the head of the U

02:42:17.100 --> 02:42:19.090
and then staff is
on either side.

02:42:19.090 --> 02:42:20.730
And there's a little
bit of chit chat

02:42:20.730 --> 02:42:23.760
that occurs before
the formal meeting.

02:42:23.760 --> 02:42:25.860
What do you think
the first question

02:42:25.860 --> 02:42:32.100
that was raised by the president
of China sitting next to me?

02:42:32.100 --> 02:42:36.600
How many more generations did I
think Moore's law had to run--

02:42:36.600 --> 02:42:39.464
president of China.

02:42:39.464 --> 02:42:41.130
Discussing it with
him a little further,

02:42:41.130 --> 02:42:43.650
it turned out he actually
was trained as an engineer,

02:42:43.650 --> 02:42:47.640
as a radio engineer, as was
the prime minister at the time.

02:42:47.640 --> 02:42:51.000
That's the kind of
interest and ability

02:42:51.000 --> 02:42:52.830
to, again, think
about technology,

02:42:52.830 --> 02:42:56.160
bring it to use that I
think is also very important

02:42:56.160 --> 02:42:57.460
here in this country.

02:42:57.460 --> 02:43:02.250
And of course, there's very
little of that available here.

02:43:02.250 --> 02:43:07.320
Another aspect, when I
came to be a student here,

02:43:07.320 --> 02:43:09.212
I was lucky to benefit
from the Research

02:43:09.212 --> 02:43:11.170
Laboratory of Electronics,
but it was very well

02:43:11.170 --> 02:43:13.260
funded at the time.

02:43:13.260 --> 02:43:16.290
Now, the funding has been
cut back quite a bit.

02:43:16.290 --> 02:43:19.740
There really are reasons to get
out, to become very politically

02:43:19.740 --> 02:43:22.290
active.

02:43:22.290 --> 02:43:24.450
Well, there have
been many rewards

02:43:24.450 --> 02:43:26.516
from having this
type of an education.

02:43:26.516 --> 02:43:28.390
Being able to go out--
the world is changing.

02:43:28.390 --> 02:43:31.060
One can take advantage of
those changes, do very well.

02:43:31.060 --> 02:43:33.270
It's important, of course,
to have an impact back.

02:43:33.270 --> 02:43:36.330
And the opportunity for
philanthropy, of course,

02:43:36.330 --> 02:43:37.830
never goes away.

02:43:37.830 --> 02:43:38.910
We've been very lucky.

02:43:38.910 --> 02:43:41.400
Our focus often is on
education, but also

02:43:41.400 --> 02:43:44.451
cultural activities, other
activities around the world.

02:43:44.451 --> 02:43:46.200
And I think, again,
that's something that,

02:43:46.200 --> 02:43:49.470
as you begin to move
ahead in your careers,

02:43:49.470 --> 02:43:51.600
that you should definitely
pay attention to.

02:43:54.310 --> 02:43:57.580
So I'd would like to finish
by, again, congratulating you.

02:43:57.580 --> 02:44:01.300
You're embarking on
a great adventure.

02:44:01.300 --> 02:44:03.550
You're probably entering a
period where there's even

02:44:03.550 --> 02:44:07.270
greater change, greater things
happening around the world

02:44:07.270 --> 02:44:11.090
than was correct when
I graduated here.

02:44:11.090 --> 02:44:15.250
You might have seen the
statement back from 1899

02:44:15.250 --> 02:44:17.320
that the head of
the patent office

02:44:17.320 --> 02:44:21.340
said that everything that can
be invented has been invented.

02:44:21.340 --> 02:44:24.487
Clearly, another
shortsighted statement.

02:44:24.487 --> 02:44:26.320
But if you check with
the patent office now,

02:44:26.320 --> 02:44:30.490
you'll find that many of
the applications, many

02:44:30.490 --> 02:44:32.560
of the patents in
the US patent office

02:44:32.560 --> 02:44:34.640
are coming from overseas.

02:44:34.640 --> 02:44:36.670
And so again, the
competition is heightened.

02:44:36.670 --> 02:44:37.790
We have to move ahead.

02:44:37.790 --> 02:44:40.090
We have to improve our
education throughout.

02:44:40.090 --> 02:44:44.020
We have to remain
very innovative.

02:44:44.020 --> 02:44:46.840
You can certainly be
guaranteed that there

02:44:46.840 --> 02:44:47.800
will be there changes.

02:44:47.800 --> 02:44:49.630
You have been well-prepared.

02:44:49.630 --> 02:44:52.780
I wish you as much
fun and excitement

02:44:52.780 --> 02:44:54.877
as I have had along the way.

02:44:54.877 --> 02:44:56.344
Than you all very much.

02:44:56.344 --> 02:44:58.789
[APPLAUSE]

02:45:06.620 --> 02:45:08.360
PRESENTER: Thank you
so much Dr. Jacobs

02:45:08.360 --> 02:45:13.910
for a very interesting
and perceptive glimpse

02:45:13.910 --> 02:45:17.300
into the future and the
challenges, and I should say,

02:45:17.300 --> 02:45:20.750
the promise that it holds
for MIT's graduates.

02:45:20.750 --> 02:45:23.670
Thank you so much.

02:45:23.670 --> 02:45:27.500
And now, Mr. Barun Singh,
President of the Graduate

02:45:27.500 --> 02:45:30.890
Student Council will
give a salute to MIT

02:45:30.890 --> 02:45:33.740
from the graduate student body.

02:45:33.740 --> 02:45:37.190
And following this,
Mr. Rohit Gupte,

02:45:37.190 --> 02:45:40.910
president of the senior class
will present the class gift

02:45:40.910 --> 02:45:44.090
to President Hockfield, after
which the president will

02:45:44.090 --> 02:45:47.670
deliver her charge
to the graduates.

02:45:47.670 --> 02:45:48.170
Barun?

02:45:58.710 --> 02:46:02.310
SINGH: My fellow engineers,
scientists, humanists,

02:46:02.310 --> 02:46:06.930
sociologists, artists, creators,
inventors, philosophers,

02:46:06.930 --> 02:46:09.780
designers, planners,
entrepreneurs, leaders,

02:46:09.780 --> 02:46:16.220
architects of the future, and
friends, you gather here today

02:46:16.220 --> 02:46:17.870
in triumph.

02:46:17.870 --> 02:46:21.830
And to you have come the spoils
of years of rigorous commitment

02:46:21.830 --> 02:46:23.810
and dedication.

02:46:23.810 --> 02:46:27.230
For this day, this
graduation day

02:46:27.230 --> 02:46:30.950
from these prestigious halls,
of this grand Institute

02:46:30.950 --> 02:46:36.540
of Technology, this
day belongs to you.

02:46:36.540 --> 02:46:40.250
We live in a universe
defined by contrast.

02:46:40.250 --> 02:46:42.170
And all of your struggles
during your time

02:46:42.170 --> 02:46:47.060
here define the sheer beauty
and joy of this moment.

02:46:47.060 --> 02:46:50.260
I'm sure that, as you
walk through today

02:46:50.260 --> 02:46:53.229
in the celebration
of your success,

02:46:53.229 --> 02:46:54.770
you will relish the
magnitude of what

02:46:54.770 --> 02:46:58.640
you have gained during your time
here in your journey thus far--

02:46:58.640 --> 02:47:01.670
the encyclopedic knowledge
you have accumulated,

02:47:01.670 --> 02:47:04.520
the invaluable assortment
of relationships

02:47:04.520 --> 02:47:08.700
that you have built, the
amazing uniqueness of activities

02:47:08.700 --> 02:47:11.220
you have participated
in, and, of course,

02:47:11.220 --> 02:47:14.970
the massive amounts of
free pizza you've consumed.

02:47:14.970 --> 02:47:16.950
In all reality,
what I hope you'll

02:47:16.950 --> 02:47:19.560
relish the most is the
growth you have experienced

02:47:19.560 --> 02:47:23.720
within yourself, not teachable
from the world around you,

02:47:23.720 --> 02:47:26.120
yet necessitated by those
very requirements which

02:47:26.120 --> 02:47:28.610
have led you to this point.

02:47:28.610 --> 02:47:31.610
I offer you the words of
Sir Edmund Hillary who,

02:47:31.610 --> 02:47:33.620
along with Nepalese
shirpa Tenzing Norgay,

02:47:33.620 --> 02:47:37.430
became the first person
on record to have climbed

02:47:37.430 --> 02:47:39.930
the heights of Mount Everest.

02:47:39.930 --> 02:47:42.120
Reflecting upon his
trying experience,

02:47:42.120 --> 02:47:45.260
Hillary stated, "It
is not the mountain

02:47:45.260 --> 02:47:49.410
we conquer, but ourselves."

02:47:49.410 --> 02:47:52.950
Today, you stand here
having climbed the heights

02:47:52.950 --> 02:47:56.580
to the roof of the
world of academia

02:47:56.580 --> 02:47:59.550
and so have also
conquered yourselves.

02:47:59.550 --> 02:48:02.160
You have tested and
stretched your limits.

02:48:02.160 --> 02:48:04.440
You have learned to persist
in the face of pressure

02:48:04.440 --> 02:48:06.840
and self-doubt and
difficulty, and that is where

02:48:06.840 --> 02:48:09.870
the true congratulations lie.

02:48:09.870 --> 02:48:11.730
There are a number
of people today

02:48:11.730 --> 02:48:15.630
who take part in the celebration
of your achievements--

02:48:15.630 --> 02:48:19.070
your parents,
mentors, advisors who

02:48:19.070 --> 02:48:21.800
have taught you, who have
inspired you and supported you

02:48:21.800 --> 02:48:24.690
so that you could be here today.

02:48:24.690 --> 02:48:27.390
Your friends,
family, and peers who

02:48:27.390 --> 02:48:32.230
have grown right alongside you,
all of the students, faculty,

02:48:32.230 --> 02:48:34.590
staff, alumni, and
community members

02:48:34.590 --> 02:48:36.864
who, in playing their
own individual roles,

02:48:36.864 --> 02:48:39.030
make this Institute what
it is and, therefore, share

02:48:39.030 --> 02:48:41.890
in some part of your success.

02:48:41.890 --> 02:48:45.650
In recognizing their impact
on your own development,

02:48:45.650 --> 02:48:47.680
I hope that you can
recognize that you will soon

02:48:47.680 --> 02:48:50.110
find yourselves in their shoes.

02:48:50.110 --> 02:48:52.240
And I invite you to
act as the mentors

02:48:52.240 --> 02:48:56.470
and inspirational voices for
the classes yet to follow.

02:48:56.470 --> 02:48:59.020
Beyond the solidification
of your own ability

02:48:59.020 --> 02:49:02.890
to live and thrive in this
world, however, all of us

02:49:02.890 --> 02:49:06.040
here today celebrate
something far greater.

02:49:06.040 --> 02:49:08.860
We celebrate the hope
and promise and potential

02:49:08.860 --> 02:49:12.250
that you represent for
the future of time to come

02:49:12.250 --> 02:49:16.110
and for the world we all share.

02:49:16.110 --> 02:49:19.560
As you talk with people about
your graduation from MIT,

02:49:19.560 --> 02:49:21.220
no doubt they will
ask you if you

02:49:21.220 --> 02:49:25.370
are ready for this thing
they call the real world, as

02:49:25.370 --> 02:49:29.360
if something about your time at
MIT had been unreal, imagined,

02:49:29.360 --> 02:49:30.542
just the dream.

02:49:30.542 --> 02:49:32.000
And don't you wish
that dream could

02:49:32.000 --> 02:49:33.708
have taken place on
a nice tropical beach

02:49:33.708 --> 02:49:35.920
somewhere instead?

02:49:35.920 --> 02:49:39.480
And yet, there is some
truth to what they say.

02:49:39.480 --> 02:49:42.480
Because as you begin the
next leg of your journey,

02:49:42.480 --> 02:49:45.510
outside of this
environment, you will likely

02:49:45.510 --> 02:49:48.640
find yourself in a very
different sort of a world--

02:49:48.640 --> 02:49:51.400
a world where reason
increasingly takes a back stage

02:49:51.400 --> 02:49:55.420
to rhetoric, a world where fear
is fast becoming the norm while

02:49:55.420 --> 02:49:58.300
curiosity and the search
for scientific advancement

02:49:58.300 --> 02:50:00.900
grow ever more suspect.

02:50:00.900 --> 02:50:02.730
At the same time,
it is a world where

02:50:02.730 --> 02:50:04.920
the problems that confront us--

02:50:04.920 --> 02:50:09.420
poverty, disease,
corruption, war, famine,

02:50:09.420 --> 02:50:11.830
environmental depletion--

02:50:11.830 --> 02:50:16.820
have consequences far greater
than that of any problem set.

02:50:16.820 --> 02:50:22.510
And in this real world, you
must be the agents of change.

02:50:22.510 --> 02:50:25.210
You must be the leaders,
for you have demonstrated

02:50:25.210 --> 02:50:28.480
a mastery of that one
most essential skill not

02:50:28.480 --> 02:50:30.580
to be underestimated--
the ability

02:50:30.580 --> 02:50:35.700
to think, to reason, to consider
the global and the long term,

02:50:35.700 --> 02:50:38.650
and not just the here and now.

02:50:38.650 --> 02:50:41.140
These words may seem
hollow at first.

02:50:41.140 --> 02:50:44.920
After all, you walk into this
world, but an individual.

02:50:44.920 --> 02:50:46.330
You face problems,
many of which,

02:50:46.330 --> 02:50:49.570
have confronted mankind
for generations.

02:50:49.570 --> 02:50:54.150
Yet, I ask you, who will define
the status quo of the world

02:50:54.150 --> 02:50:57.190
to come if not you?

02:50:57.190 --> 02:50:59.660
Gandhi, a man who knew
something of the strength

02:50:59.660 --> 02:51:04.370
and power of the one in the
face of the many once said,

02:51:04.370 --> 02:51:08.250
"We must be the change
we wish to see."

02:51:08.250 --> 02:51:11.840
This quote holds relevance
for all of us here.

02:51:11.840 --> 02:51:14.810
For those of you who have truly
found paradise during your stay

02:51:14.810 --> 02:51:19.780
here at MIT, in participating
in the meritocratic ideal,

02:51:19.780 --> 02:51:23.240
in displaying that trademark
intellectual defiance,

02:51:23.240 --> 02:51:25.670
in exercising your passion
to debate and delve

02:51:25.670 --> 02:51:27.680
into the heart of
things, and your refusal

02:51:27.680 --> 02:51:30.500
to accept what is handed
to you, and in being

02:51:30.500 --> 02:51:33.080
open to the most
unconventional of solutions

02:51:33.080 --> 02:51:35.000
to the most hopeless
of problems--

02:51:35.000 --> 02:51:37.340
to you, I say, and I
charge to you today

02:51:37.340 --> 02:51:40.970
to keep alive that
passion and drive.

02:51:40.970 --> 02:51:42.465
The world needs it.

02:51:42.465 --> 02:51:44.940
And it waits for you.

02:51:44.940 --> 02:51:45.930
Thank you.

02:51:45.930 --> 02:51:48.405
[APPLAUSE]

02:52:07.240 --> 02:52:10.420
GUPTE: Since the beginning
of our life journey,

02:52:10.420 --> 02:52:14.410
we have dreamed of becoming
seekers of knowledge.

02:52:14.410 --> 02:52:17.200
We are a generation
that did not directly

02:52:17.200 --> 02:52:20.170
witness the mission to
the moon or the marches

02:52:20.170 --> 02:52:23.770
for civil rights like
our mothers and fathers.

02:52:23.770 --> 02:52:27.490
We are inspired by the
information age, spawned

02:52:27.490 --> 02:52:29.830
in our basements,
and most symbolically

02:52:29.830 --> 02:52:32.660
represented by the internet.

02:52:32.660 --> 02:52:35.129
The difference is significant.

02:52:35.129 --> 02:52:37.170
While our parents could
watch the Apollo missions

02:52:37.170 --> 02:52:41.070
with awe and envy, their
immediate participation

02:52:41.070 --> 02:52:45.110
in the growth of these important
world changes was limited.

02:52:45.110 --> 02:52:49.640
We, on the other hand, have
been gifted with the opportunity

02:52:49.640 --> 02:52:55.020
to participate in global changes
in an unprecedented fashion.

02:52:55.020 --> 02:52:58.830
We live in a world where
technology has been focused

02:52:58.830 --> 02:53:01.350
on the public for
everyday living,

02:53:01.350 --> 02:53:05.880
not just for specialized
military or government use.

02:53:05.880 --> 02:53:13.020
Technology has never been more
applied to the broad population

02:53:13.020 --> 02:53:14.680
like it is today.

02:53:14.680 --> 02:53:18.380
And we have embraced it in
all the value that it brings.

02:53:18.380 --> 02:53:20.450
Since we were
surrounded, many of us

02:53:20.450 --> 02:53:24.740
found our curious minds trying
to learn what it is all about.

02:53:24.740 --> 02:53:27.770
We opened up computers,
we built robots,

02:53:27.770 --> 02:53:29.720
we performed
research experiments

02:53:29.720 --> 02:53:33.850
long before we arrived at MIT.

02:53:33.850 --> 02:53:36.700
This world we live in
now, with applications

02:53:36.700 --> 02:53:39.190
of technology everywhere
before our eyes,

02:53:39.190 --> 02:53:43.320
fits seamlessly with
MIT's academic goals.

02:53:43.320 --> 02:53:47.830
The time and energy we have
spent in laboratories, classes,

02:53:47.830 --> 02:53:51.520
UROPs, and the unprecedented
number out-of-classroom

02:53:51.520 --> 02:53:54.220
learning opportunities
have culminated

02:53:54.220 --> 02:53:58.340
to form the experience
we call MIT.

02:53:58.340 --> 02:54:00.410
And yet, our formal
training is only

02:54:00.410 --> 02:54:03.770
a part of what we carry
away from our time here.

02:54:03.770 --> 02:54:05.660
The relationships
that we have formed

02:54:05.660 --> 02:54:08.630
within our community and
the way that we have learned

02:54:08.630 --> 02:54:12.470
to learn in such an intense
and rich environment

02:54:12.470 --> 02:54:16.440
has created the most
lasting impact on us.

02:54:16.440 --> 02:54:19.350
Our experiences at
MIT can be compared

02:54:19.350 --> 02:54:22.960
to, ironically, a vacation.

02:54:22.960 --> 02:54:26.590
Imagine you went on a vacation
to an unfamiliar place

02:54:26.590 --> 02:54:30.140
and encountered, perhaps,
the worst possible weather.

02:54:30.140 --> 02:54:32.930
If you had your closest
friends with you, together,

02:54:32.930 --> 02:54:36.900
you would still find a way
to have a fantastic time.

02:54:36.900 --> 02:54:39.030
Well, the same works at MIT.

02:54:39.030 --> 02:54:42.360
Is the people with whom we
have spent our years here

02:54:42.360 --> 02:54:46.060
that make this place amazing
and allow us to succeed.

02:54:46.060 --> 02:54:49.050
Together we have
done more than if we

02:54:49.050 --> 02:54:51.330
summed our individual efforts.

02:54:51.330 --> 02:54:55.200
Together we have
grown more wise.

02:54:55.200 --> 02:54:57.540
As this community
diverges and integrates

02:54:57.540 --> 02:55:01.620
into the larger society, we
will never forget the union.

02:55:01.620 --> 02:55:03.870
Forever are we attached.

02:55:03.870 --> 02:55:09.510
We have become citizens of a
nation, a nation called MIT.

02:55:09.510 --> 02:55:12.080
We share with our
brothers and sisters

02:55:12.080 --> 02:55:14.040
an immediate connection.

02:55:14.040 --> 02:55:16.860
When you meet someone and
hear that she went to MIT,

02:55:16.860 --> 02:55:18.990
you already understand
so much more

02:55:18.990 --> 02:55:20.910
about what she's been through.

02:55:20.910 --> 02:55:23.010
Hear of an MIT grad,
and immediately you

02:55:23.010 --> 02:55:25.320
sense the awesomeness of
his intellectual drive

02:55:25.320 --> 02:55:27.990
and his accomplishments
thus far.

02:55:27.990 --> 02:55:32.280
For to have graduated
from MIT is no easy feat.

02:55:32.280 --> 02:55:34.890
Yet, we remain
humbled as we recall

02:55:34.890 --> 02:55:36.810
our difficult experiences.

02:55:36.810 --> 02:55:39.060
It is in these testing
moments that we

02:55:39.060 --> 02:55:41.490
can see the opportunities
that allow us to put

02:55:41.490 --> 02:55:44.040
our experiences to good use.

02:55:44.040 --> 02:55:47.820
However, as members of
this marvelous citizenry,

02:55:47.820 --> 02:55:49.890
there comes a duty.

02:55:49.890 --> 02:55:53.160
With our citizenship
of the MIT nation,

02:55:53.160 --> 02:55:56.430
we must understand the
incredible responsibility

02:55:56.430 --> 02:55:59.480
of holders of this great
power called knowledge.

02:55:59.480 --> 02:56:03.330
For the world will look at
us and our patent disregard

02:56:03.330 --> 02:56:05.070
for doing the normal.

02:56:05.070 --> 02:56:09.240
The world will look at us for
our audacity and our courage

02:56:09.240 --> 02:56:12.180
to question everything
we see around us.

02:56:12.180 --> 02:56:15.540
The world will look to us,
and it is our responsibility

02:56:15.540 --> 02:56:18.000
to look back at them
and say, together

02:56:18.000 --> 02:56:21.170
we can accomplish anything.

02:56:21.170 --> 02:56:25.280
Oliver Wendell Holmes once
said, "What lies behind us

02:56:25.280 --> 02:56:28.070
and what lies before us
are tiny matters compared

02:56:28.070 --> 02:56:30.320
to what lies within us."

02:56:30.320 --> 02:56:32.780
What we have
accomplished here at MIT,

02:56:32.780 --> 02:56:35.150
the education,
the relationships,

02:56:35.150 --> 02:56:38.300
the community is by
no means trivial.

02:56:38.300 --> 02:56:41.780
But what we truly
take away from MIT

02:56:41.780 --> 02:56:45.710
is the way that these
experiences have changed us.

02:56:45.710 --> 02:56:48.360
This is what we
bring to our future.

02:56:48.360 --> 02:56:52.650
Let us go forth and
show them who we are.

02:56:52.650 --> 02:56:55.080
And now please join
me as we partake

02:56:55.080 --> 02:56:58.194
in the tradition of the
turning of our brass rat

02:56:58.194 --> 02:56:59.658
as a symbol of our
accomplishments.

02:56:59.658 --> 02:57:02.098
[CHEERING]

02:57:11.370 --> 02:57:13.770
At this time it is
my distinct honor

02:57:13.770 --> 02:57:17.280
to request President Susan
Hockfield to join me as we

02:57:17.280 --> 02:57:19.890
present the senior class gift.

02:57:19.890 --> 02:57:22.710
In keeping with the traditions
of improving student life,

02:57:22.710 --> 02:57:26.070
the class of 2005 has
designated their class gift

02:57:26.070 --> 02:57:28.380
towards the creation
of a new student lounge

02:57:28.380 --> 02:57:31.240
to take the place of the
cashier's office in Lobby 10

02:57:31.240 --> 02:57:33.000
right behind me.

02:57:33.000 --> 02:57:35.860
This year, we were
able to raise over

02:57:35.860 --> 02:57:41.548
$31,000 with 25.4%
participation.

02:57:41.548 --> 02:57:42.214
Congratulations.

02:57:42.214 --> 02:57:44.202
And thank you very much.

02:57:44.202 --> 02:57:46.687
[APPLAUSE]

02:57:58.615 --> 02:58:00.720
HOCKFIELD: Thank
you, Barun, and thank

02:58:00.720 --> 02:58:03.900
you Rohit, and the members
of the class of 2005

02:58:03.900 --> 02:58:05.340
for your gift.

02:58:05.340 --> 02:58:08.550
The new student lounge
off the Lobby 10

02:58:08.550 --> 02:58:10.320
will provide a welcome
space for students

02:58:10.320 --> 02:58:13.800
to come together at the
heart of a very busy campus.

02:58:13.800 --> 02:58:16.680
It will create a
space for conversation

02:58:16.680 --> 02:58:19.760
and for collaboration.

02:58:19.760 --> 02:58:23.840
And thank you Irwin Jacobs for
your thoughtful and inspiring

02:58:23.840 --> 02:58:25.220
remarks.

02:58:25.220 --> 02:58:27.110
Your work and your
life are evidence

02:58:27.110 --> 02:58:29.690
of a mind that has thought
far into the future

02:58:29.690 --> 02:58:33.710
to explore the expanding
promise and power of technology

02:58:33.710 --> 02:58:35.318
to improve our lives.

02:58:35.318 --> 02:58:37.835
The Institute is
honored to count you

02:58:37.835 --> 02:58:38.960
as an alumnus and a friend.

02:58:41.600 --> 02:58:47.420
Graduates of MIT,
this is your day.

02:58:47.420 --> 02:58:49.880
We have gathered here
today in Killeen Court

02:58:49.880 --> 02:58:52.550
to celebrate your
accomplishments,

02:58:52.550 --> 02:58:55.790
the successful completion of
demanding courses of study,

02:58:55.790 --> 02:58:58.690
often, lasting several years.

02:58:58.690 --> 02:59:03.050
You have our deepest respect for
all that you have accomplished.

02:59:03.050 --> 02:59:06.297
But today is not yours alone.

02:59:06.297 --> 02:59:07.880
None of you would
be here this morning

02:59:07.880 --> 02:59:10.610
were it not for the
family and friends

02:59:10.610 --> 02:59:14.210
who have nurtured and
supported you since childhood.

02:59:14.210 --> 02:59:16.600
Those people who have
embraced your dreams

02:59:16.600 --> 02:59:18.410
and lighted your path--

02:59:18.410 --> 02:59:20.670
this is their day too.

02:59:20.670 --> 02:59:24.376
Graduates, I ask you
please to rise and join

02:59:24.376 --> 02:59:27.088
with me in thanking
those family and friends.

02:59:27.088 --> 02:59:29.553
[APPLAUSE]

02:59:49.810 --> 02:59:54.220
A month ago, I stood
here in Killeen Court

02:59:54.220 --> 02:59:59.920
and spoke about what defines
MIT, about my dreams and hopes

02:59:59.920 --> 03:00:02.590
for this great institution.

03:00:02.590 --> 03:00:04.840
Those inaugural
remarks were addressed

03:00:04.840 --> 03:00:07.390
to the whole
Institute community.

03:00:07.390 --> 03:00:09.850
This morning, I
would like to speak

03:00:09.850 --> 03:00:13.030
to those of you who
are graduating today

03:00:13.030 --> 03:00:17.050
about my hopes and
dreams for you.

03:00:17.050 --> 03:00:22.930
You, our graduates, are
exceptional individuals.

03:00:22.930 --> 03:00:26.620
You arrived at MIT with
remarkable native talents

03:00:26.620 --> 03:00:30.070
already honed by years
of demanding study.

03:00:30.070 --> 03:00:34.810
Here, you've learned
from a brilliant faculty,

03:00:34.810 --> 03:00:38.390
and just as important,
from each other.

03:00:38.390 --> 03:00:41.650
You've learned about
complex subject matter,

03:00:41.650 --> 03:00:45.430
and you've also learned
about yourselves.

03:00:45.430 --> 03:00:48.400
Now, as you leave
MIT, I challenge

03:00:48.400 --> 03:00:50.680
you to put what you
have learned here

03:00:50.680 --> 03:00:55.540
to work for the good of
this nation and the world

03:00:55.540 --> 03:00:58.570
because we have never needed
your talents and skills more

03:00:58.570 --> 03:01:00.940
than we do now.

03:01:00.940 --> 03:01:04.600
We live in uncertain
and an unsettled age,

03:01:04.600 --> 03:01:07.180
and we face great challenges.

03:01:07.180 --> 03:01:09.370
To name only a few--

03:01:09.370 --> 03:01:12.820
we have challenges in
energy, in climate change,

03:01:12.820 --> 03:01:15.430
in contagious
diseases, in the design

03:01:15.430 --> 03:01:20.310
of our urban communities,
and in global poverty.

03:01:20.310 --> 03:01:24.360
You, the graduates of
MIT, are uniquely equipped

03:01:24.360 --> 03:01:26.940
to address issues like these.

03:01:26.940 --> 03:01:30.270
You are ready to make the
necessary advances in science

03:01:30.270 --> 03:01:33.900
and technology to employ
rigorous quantitative and

03:01:33.900 --> 03:01:36.960
qualitative analyses
to develop new methods

03:01:36.960 --> 03:01:42.390
of interdisciplinary
inquiry and problem-solving.

03:01:42.390 --> 03:01:46.830
So at times, in the years ahead,
when a choice of direction

03:01:46.830 --> 03:01:52.450
presents itself, I hope
you will ask yourselves,

03:01:52.450 --> 03:01:55.320
where can I do the most good?

03:01:55.320 --> 03:01:59.530
How can I make a
difference in the world?

03:01:59.530 --> 03:02:03.490
Now, you will not be able
to do this work alone.

03:02:03.490 --> 03:02:05.590
Meeting the great
challenges of our era

03:02:05.590 --> 03:02:08.860
will require teamwork
and collaboration.

03:02:08.860 --> 03:02:10.540
You will need to
draw, not only on what

03:02:10.540 --> 03:02:13.060
you have learned in the
classroom and laboratory,

03:02:13.060 --> 03:02:15.820
but also on what you have
learned about the importance

03:02:15.820 --> 03:02:18.190
of community.

03:02:18.190 --> 03:02:20.830
As the currents of your
lives draw you away

03:02:20.830 --> 03:02:24.820
from MIT's shores, you will,
in important and real ways,

03:02:24.820 --> 03:02:28.240
remain part of this community.

03:02:28.240 --> 03:02:30.280
At the close of this
morning's ceremonies,

03:02:30.280 --> 03:02:33.340
Linda Sharpe, President
of the Alumni Association

03:02:33.340 --> 03:02:38.110
will formally welcome you into
the Association's membership.

03:02:38.110 --> 03:02:41.170
We hope you will stay
connected and engaged

03:02:41.170 --> 03:02:44.510
with the life of the Institute.

03:02:44.510 --> 03:02:49.040
But beyond your own
personal connection to MIT,

03:02:49.040 --> 03:02:52.280
I hope you will also
transmit the values

03:02:52.280 --> 03:02:56.720
that define this community to
the other communities you will

03:02:56.720 --> 03:03:00.950
now join, that you will see
leadership as an opportunity

03:03:00.950 --> 03:03:05.570
to serve the common good,
that you will make integrity

03:03:05.570 --> 03:03:08.750
the touchstone of
your judgments,

03:03:08.750 --> 03:03:12.380
that you will exemplify
the pursuit of truth

03:03:12.380 --> 03:03:16.640
and an unwavering drive
toward excellence,

03:03:16.640 --> 03:03:19.010
and that you will
continue to demonstrate

03:03:19.010 --> 03:03:25.080
the value of plain
old-fashioned hard work.

03:03:25.080 --> 03:03:29.010
Finally, and perhaps
most crucially,

03:03:29.010 --> 03:03:33.570
I ask you to inspire your own
generation and those to come

03:03:33.570 --> 03:03:37.140
with a renewed sense of
possibility and optimism

03:03:37.140 --> 03:03:39.300
for the future.

03:03:39.300 --> 03:03:43.860
Here at MIT, we see, up
close, the myriad ways

03:03:43.860 --> 03:03:48.450
in which science and technology
promise to benefit humankind.

03:03:48.450 --> 03:03:50.610
If we are to realize
that promise,

03:03:50.610 --> 03:03:54.630
we need to kindle in others the
same love and passion for truth

03:03:54.630 --> 03:03:58.620
and discovery, for creativity
and problem-solving that

03:03:58.620 --> 03:04:00.870
brought us all here.

03:04:00.870 --> 03:04:06.690
I hope that each of you will
embrace this as your challenge.

03:04:06.690 --> 03:04:09.600
I would not set you this
charge if I did not think

03:04:09.600 --> 03:04:11.700
that each of you could meet it.

03:04:11.700 --> 03:04:14.040
I have tremendous faith in you.

03:04:14.040 --> 03:04:16.650
The intelligence,
diligence, and creativity

03:04:16.650 --> 03:04:21.270
you've demonstrated here at
MIT have inspired us all.

03:04:21.270 --> 03:04:24.810
And I know that, in the years
to come, you will do even more

03:04:24.810 --> 03:04:28.200
and surprise and delight
us with achievements

03:04:28.200 --> 03:04:31.480
we could never have predicted.

03:04:31.480 --> 03:04:34.830
So for now, in
closing, let me simply

03:04:34.830 --> 03:04:40.626
say congratulations
graduates of MIT.

03:04:40.626 --> 03:04:43.081
[APPLAUSE]

03:04:53.910 --> 03:04:56.550
SPEAKER: Thank you,
President Hockfield,

03:04:56.550 --> 03:04:59.880
for your first charge
to the graduates

03:04:59.880 --> 03:05:04.800
as the 16th president of MIT.

03:05:04.800 --> 03:05:09.250
And now, the presentation
of the degrees.

03:05:09.250 --> 03:05:11.830
By virtue of the authority
delegated to them

03:05:11.830 --> 03:05:14.110
by the Corporation
of the Massachusetts

03:05:14.110 --> 03:05:17.920
Institute of Technology,
and upon recommendation

03:05:17.920 --> 03:05:22.600
of the faculty, President
Hockfield and Provost Brown

03:05:22.600 --> 03:05:26.210
will now present the degrees.

03:05:26.210 --> 03:05:30.580
President Hockfield will
present the following degrees--

03:05:30.580 --> 03:05:37.840
Bachelor of Science, Bachelor
of Science/Master of Science,

03:05:37.840 --> 03:05:42.550
Bachelor of Science/Master
of Engineering,

03:05:42.550 --> 03:05:45.190
and the advanced
degrees for the School

03:05:45.190 --> 03:05:49.870
of Science and Whitaker
College of Health Sciences

03:05:49.870 --> 03:05:52.210
and Technology.

03:05:52.210 --> 03:05:55.840
Provost Brown will present
the advanced degrees

03:05:55.840 --> 03:05:59.030
for the School of
Architecture and Planning,

03:05:59.030 --> 03:06:03.100
the School of Engineering, the
School of Humanities, Arts,

03:06:03.100 --> 03:06:08.470
and Social Sciences, and the
Sloan School of Management.

03:06:08.470 --> 03:06:09.820
As they approach the ramp,

03:06:09.820 --> 03:06:12.040
[CHEERING]

03:06:12.930 --> 03:06:16.950
